      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>E.</p>
        <p>Forshaw &amp; Son, Ltd., ENGINEERS, BOILERMAKERS, IRON &amp; BRASS FOUNDERS, Mersey Boiler Works, Mersey Street, Warrington. Makers of IRON CASTINGS from the smallest size up to 20 tons weight. Clean Well-moulded and of the Highest Quality. To buy the best finished castings is to frequently save 50 per cent. in labour. RASS CASTINGS (any weight) Gunmetal, Copper, Aluminium, Phosphor Bronze, Damaxine, Acid Proof Metal and all other Alloys. - CONSTRUCTIONAL IRON AND STEEL WORK, Corrugated Iron Roofs and Buildings, Farmers' Hay Sheds, Barns, Store Sheds, &amp;c., Iron and Steel Tanks, Cisterns, Stoem Jacketted Pans, Boilers, &amp;c. Repairs by Experienced and Skilled Workmen to all kinds of Machinery and to Steam Boilers, Gas Engines, Traction Engines, Motor Cars, Dynamos, Steam Engines, Oil Engines, Road Rollers, Motor Waggons, Electric Motors. ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING. Speciality Work. Drawings and expert advice submitted free. Heavy Machining and Forging done for the trade. Turning up to 15 feet diameter. Planing up to 5 feet square. Slotting up to 18-inch stroke. Milling, Punching, Shearing and Bending, Pressing, Dishing, Fangling and Rivetting by hydraulic pressure. Unlimited Tackle and Special Appliances for efficiently dealing with Repairs and Breakdown work, accumulated during an establihsment of 60 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>McDOUGALL'S Pats* SELF—RAISING FLOUR j3SIEGUNON.</head>
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Pats* SELF—RAISING FLOUR j3SIEGUNON.</p>
        <p>So the butcher named Hawken went to the public-house to have a glass to furnish him with sourage to bear his happiness. Now, whom should he find there but Mr. Hicks, who had gone to the tavern for the same .purpose.</p>
        <p>No one else was there, only these two batehexs, and they sat at the table and eyed each other with a light ot triumph in their eyes. Each called for his glass, and each contem- plated the other with a glance of proud satis- faction.</p>
        <p>"Well, 'lcks!" said the one. • "Well, .'Awken!" said the other. "I.cat.Thed your eye," said the first. "And I reciprocated," remarked the second. "You seem chirpy this day," observed Hawken. "I may say the same for you," retorted Hicks. "I've a cause to be," replied Hawken. "And so 'ave I," threw in Hicks. .`if I may make so bold as to ask," said the former, "what is it ? " "I'm a-going to be married—and you? "Ditto, replied the other. "No—never! " "Why not? I'm as likely a man .as you." "And who's the 'appy female ? " asked Hicks. "There's money init. And your choice ?" "Money too." . "Much ? " "Two thousand pounds." "That's about my figure." "Well, now, that's curious." "Why so ? Ain't I worth it ? "</p>
        <p>"I thin't say. that. But I didn't know as there was females going about the country with so much money to spare on husbands." "The surpriSe is mutual," said Hawken. Then ensued a silence, as each man eyed the other. Hicks was disappointed. He had calcu- lated on swallowing up his rival in the butchery business, with the, assistance of the two thousand pounds. "And who may it be ? " asked Hicks. " Rosina Pengersick," replied Hawken, exultant. "Never!" exclaimed Hicks. "Why do you. say never ? Do you doubt my word ? '</p>
        <p>"I do doubt, it," said Hicks; "for that's the she as I'm about to celebrate my nuptials along with."</p>
        <p>"It's clear impossible," said Hawken—"when she's engaged to me." "It is impossible—when she booked to me," retorted Hicks.</p>
        <p>"Can't EC-3 it no way," observed Hawken. "I do see it, .and disbelieve •it," responded Hicks.</p>
        <p>"Well, then, here you are," said Hawken, spreadhlg the latex of Rosina's on the table. "Now, old boy, read and digest that." "There, look at that," said Hicks, casting his letter on the table. "How do you relish that, man alive ? "</p>
        <p>"There's some mistake," said Hawken. ".I reckon there is," retaliated Hicks. "But what can be stronger than this, • My ever dear ? ' You see, she's reg'lar over head and ears with me. How I long to see you— words can't express it! ' There's for you, man! ' Kiss and be 'appy.' What more do.I want ? That implies two thousand pounds, for there's no sweetness in kisses and no appiness without." "What more forcible than this ? " exclaimed Hawken: " If I has a heart to dispose of' —eh ? "</p>
        <p>"Heart!" jeered Hicks. "It's a bullock's heart, or maybe a sheep's, as she has ordered of you." "Haven't got one in the shop. There's mine, and mine only. Why, bless you! she'd but to walk past my little place and look in and see that there wasn't one crooked up—none but my own beating and living heart in my bosom. if she didn't mean it, it's actionable, and sue her for breach o' promise. But, 'lcks! what do you say about that there enigma ? " -Can't quite make it out," said Hicks, musingly. ' "I reckon it's something internal "It's internal and external as well," aid Hawken. "It means that she's -gone and invested that there two thousand pounds in an Argentine mine. I saw something about it. The Enigma —that's a mine as has gone scatt (broke)." "Never!" gr,sped Hicks. "If 'twas only some internal malady as would carry h_tr off, poor thing, in a year or two, I should7r t so much mind. I'd nuss her tenderly through it—but a bad investment is another coloured hoss,- that it is. Where did you see about that there Argentina mine called the Enigma ? " . .</p>
        <p>"In the Aqua ncial :Veu,&amp;" "Do you happen to have the copy ?" "No, I haven't. I wrapped up some rump- steak in it .yesterday as i</p>
        <p>"I don't believi.; a word of it—l won't til I see."</p>
        <p>"Well, go and marry her, and find you get nothing else but a dead-weight of a wife. I hold to it that the heart she asked for was mine." "-Not possible." "Ain't my personal attractions enough 9 " "I don't say nothing against them, but you have red hair." "And I hold it's me she Wants. She'd never care for you with your blazin' red cheeks and a great pimple on your nose." . . . . -</p>
        <p>It i just because she is- so lily-white and has black hair that she favours me. Women's likings always is for contraries." "Well, go and ask her what she means by it," said Hawken, "asking for my heart and halul."</p>
        <p>"For a heart, and never a word about a hand," retorted Hicks. "I'll go also and inquire what she means by wishing to kiss and be 'app) with me—and "—with a doubt—"about them thousand pounds.", _</p>
        <p>"Don't -let us steal a march on each other. Let us deal honourable and gentlemanlylike between us," said Hawken. "All right. We'll go together and have it out at once."</p>
        <p>"I can't go at once," said Hawken. "I've got a lot to do till to-morrow." . _ _ _ _ . _ _</p>
        <p>"And so have I," said Hicks. "We'll go and ask her meaning to-morrow." "Done with you. And now another glass to him as sees and convers." "That's me," said Hicks. "I reckons it is me," said Hawken. So t.',42 two butchers retired to their respective shops. . Miss Rosina Pengersick was disappointed that neither the &amp;art nor the shoulder of mutton arrived, and she was preparing to do for her own supper a rasher of bacon, When a tap came at tha door, and going to it she received from Hawken's boy a parcel. On opening it she found a consignment of veal for cutlets. "With Mr. William Hawken's most grateful and kind regards. Heart at your dis- posal." Ten minutes later, another tap, and she. opened the door to Mr. Hicks's man who handed to her another parcel that contained leg of lamb, with the note: "Miss Rosina Perwersick,. in token of admiration, respect, and affection._ _ _</p>
        <p>_ "What does the man mean ? " she asked of herself, as she read this. "Admiration I can understand. I have always- been admired. Folk in Porthscawn would have very little taste if they didn't admire me. I have a figure such as have no others here. Respect is reasonable enough, I've always kept myself respectable, and then with my aunt's two thousand pounds, that is enough to make anyone respect me. But affection—that conies rather strong! I have never given him any encouragment. 1 must order my meat for a fortnight from Hawken, and bring Hicks down a pcg. I wonder why Hawken hasn't sent me a heart when I ordered it, and sent me cutlets of veal instead ? I sup- pose he thought it would be more tasty. Well, I have here enough for a nice little dinner for Selina and for that other whom she brings with her, whoever he or she may be. But she's a scheming woman and a match-maker, and I do believe it's a he."</p>
        <p>She considered, and said: "I'll keep to a rasher for myself this evening, and have the lamb and the veaf eutleps, 'these latter with egg and breadcrumbs, for dinner to-morrow, and let then) see whoever conies as I'm able to turn out as nice a little dinner as anyone would like to eat. And we'll have a junket and cream, and tartlets. roast the leg of lamb to-day, and have it cold with mint sauce and a salad—a dinner for a king." Next day at 11.38 the train from Penzance drew up at Porthseawn, and from it stepped Miss Dunstone and a gentleman carrying a basket. llosina was not at the station to meet them, for she had her preparations for the dinner to make, and she was aware that her cousin- knew the way to her house. So a quarter of an hour after their arrival at the station Miss Selina Dunstone and her cousin presented themselves at the door of the dress- maker, and the former introduced Mr. Joseph Ham—in the vernacular, 'Am.</p>
        <p>"Oh, my dear Rosie," said Selina, "Mr, 'Am TCDtlAre. Q bringing yoi. litOe prcqppt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>(ALL RIG HTS RESERVED.)</head>
        <p>(ALL RIG HTS RESERVED.)</p>
        <p>ROSINA:S LOVERS.</p>
        <p>BY S. BARING GOULD,</p>
        <p>Author of "Mehalah," "John Herring," "The -Gaverocks," "Richard Cable," "Cheap Jack Zita," "The Pennycomequicks," Sz-c.</p>
        <p>In the little Cornish fishing port and watering- place of Porthscawn lived Rosin Pengersick, a dressmaker, aged twenty-six. She was tall, well-made, with a pleasant, but not beautiful face, and although she had attracted the atten- tion of several eligible young men,none ever had---- to use a Vulgarism she herself employed- come up to the scratch ;-that is to say, none had proposed to change her MUM. - But her beauty had been greatly enhanced in the eyes of the available young men of Portlis- cawn latterly wh6n an aunt left her. two thonsa.nd pounds in the Three per Cents. The most cligibk: of those who hovered around her were the two butchers in the place, by name Hicks and Hawken, or, as they elected to call themselves, and were called generally, 'leks and 'Awken.</p>
        <p>She divided her .custom equally between them, so that neither could claim a decided _preference. 'The nearest approach to shewing a marked in- clination had been when she had ordered spare-rib from 'lcks. But as Mr. Hicks remarked to his mother, he wanted her as his own spare-rib, aucl not to furnish her with one. _ .</p>
        <p>There was an advantage to her in ordering her meat from both, for each vied with the other in supplying her with prime cuts, in sending her very little bone and fat for her money. And now and then Hicks sent "with his respectful compliments "-a present' of a sweetbread, and Hawken "begged her acceptance" of a calf's head. There are various ways of putting a thing. Vernon, in the Temple Gardens, said: "I pluck this pale and maiden blossom here," but Hicks expressed himself thus : "I'm blowed if I won't knobble that there Rose Pengersick— with her, two thousand pounds, if she'll have me. Two thousand pounds would just about set me up first-rate, and I'd beat that old 'Awken out of the field altogether, and no mistake." On the other hand, Hawken said to him- self : "If I could secure Rosina with her two thousand pounds and what she makes with her needle, I'd be a gentleman, and 'ave only to stretch my legs, put my 'ands in my pocket, and smoke and enjoy myself at the Three Donkeys</p>
        <p>_ every-day." _ "There's been a little awk'ardness about them sheep of Farmer Penrose," mused Hicks. "Parson Jones's dixr had the rabies and bit one, and that there ewe went tearin' mad and bit the rest of the flock, and they was all killed and I bought the meat. There's a talk among the womenfolk as I've given hydrophobia to the men</p>
        <p>through the mutton, as there's- been so many of 'em on the spree since and took a lot of liquor,'. but not water. It's damaged my business, and 'Awken 'ave made the most of it, and got sonic custom away from me. If I could crush 'Awken out of the field and 'ave it all to myself, then the customers might grumble, but they could do nothing against me. I've a mind to try my luck with Rosina. " And on his side Hawken mused: "Two</p>
        <p>thousand pounds in the Three per Cents. is foolishness. I could invest it at six, and that would come. to a hundred and twenty pounds per annum, and Ilosina could earn thirty with her needle and taking lodgers, and on a hundred and fifty My word ! I needn't do no more butchering, nor no nothing at all. That would be prime ! I'll venture on it, though they do tell me she's a bit strict in her_views." - Some of the most important events in history and in biography are brought about by small causes. To the elopement of Paris and Helen was due the memorable siege of Troy and its destruc- tion; and to it we are indebted for the immortal Iliad and Odyssey. What would the world of letters have been without that foolish escapade of two silly young people? The determination of Rosins Pengersick's life,</p>
        <p>and the closing up of the ambitions- of 'kits and 'Awken were due to—a- wasp, and a wasp which buzzed and did not sting. It came about thus: Rosina Pengersick had received a letter by the post. It was from her cousin, Miss Selina Dunstone : "My dearest Rose, —I haven't seen you for a long-time, and .now I propose coining over and visiting you to-morrow from Penzance, always hoping that this finds you, as it leaves me, middling well. I daresay you will be so kind as to give us a bite of dinner. I say us, because I'm going to bring someone over with me, and I hopes sincerely as it will be pleasing and accept- able to you. to receive such, and that the 'acquaintance will ripen into something more. Drop me a line by return.—Yours ever affection- ately, SELINA DUNSTONE. To which Rosina replied: "My ever dear,—How I long to see you— words can't -exptess it. But I must tell you that I am troubled—about an enigma. I don't understand it.. Excuse more. We shall meet and kiss, and be happy in each other's society.— Ever yours devotedly, ROSINA PENDERSICK: • "Well, I wonder now," said the dressmaker, "whom she can be bringing? I fancy it's Mr. Joe Ham; but I can't say. - I wish she had written more plain. If it's a man coming, we'll want a lot of victuals, and I must order in more meat—they do eat prodigious do those men. It -ever I do get married, what a butcher's bill shall have to pay! Let me see, what shall l have" Rosina Pengersick bit the end of her pen. "Let me consider," he said ;"I must have</p>
        <p>_ something for my own dinner to-day, and then something for Selina and that other to-morrow. Drat that apple-drain ! "—as a striped yellow and black wasp, locally called an apple-drain, . . . „ . _</p>
        <p>began, to • buzz about her head—"at shall have ? Bother the creature! It will get into my hair. I *know—l will have a shoulder of mutton for them, and order it from Hicks, and I'll have a sheep's heart for. myself from flaw ken."</p>
        <p>She took a couple of sheet of note-paper and</p>
        <p>wrote on one: "Miss Pengersiek will be obliged to be furnished forthwitll a shoulder of lamb."</p>
        <p>On the other:</p>
        <p>"If Mr. Hawken has a heart not disposed of, Miss Pengersiek will bo much obliged if he will reserve it for her." _</p>
        <p>"Hang it! There is that apple-drain again," and Miss Pengersick battled with the insect: now striking at it with her pen, and then flapping about her with her pocket-handkerchief. But the wasp would not be beaten off. Rosina had that morning put -•pomatum on her hair scented with Bergamot, and this strongly attracted the insect. ln a flutter of fear lest she should be stung, Rosina put the notes into their envelopes, and with many interruptions, and now and then a squeal, as the wasp lighted on her, addressed the three notes. Now, it so happened that letters and addresses were wrong, all through the confusion caused by the wasp. Consequently Mr. Hicks received the note intended for Miss Selina punstone who was served with that intended for Mr. hicks, and at once comprehended by its tenor that a mistake had occurred. The wrong letter—the order to the butcher--had been put into her envelope. So she said to her cousin, Joe Ham:</p>
        <p>"I say, Joe, it's till right. Rosina expects us, and has ordered a shoulder of mutton for our dinner." But Hicks received the note couched in these terms:</p>
        <p>"My ever dear,—How I long, to see you— words can't express it. But I must tell you that I am troubled about an enigma. I don't understand it. Excuse more. We shall meet und kiss, and be happy in ,_,,ach other's society. Ever yours devotedly, ROSIN A PENOERSICK. "It's perhaps a little forward," said Hicks, "there's no denying it. I have charms, and great allowance mult be made for the women. In Cornwall women swarm and men are scarce, since the mines have been shut down, and all the men have gone • off, to South. Africa and</p>
        <p>Brazil, and lvlierever there are mines working. Val, I didn't quite think it of Rose, with her grave face and high principles, but—there, when U ripe plum drops into one's mouth, one must wallow it."</p>
        <p>'About the Same time Mr. HalVken received his note:</p>
        <p>. "If Mr. llawkcn has heart not disposed of, Miss Pengersiek will bo. much abliged if he will reserve it for her."</p>
        <p>"Of course, my heart is ta 1)1 disposed of, and Inv hand also." said 'Am-ken. " You're iii luck's way, Billy, my boy—Ha, ha! Icks,' and he snapped his fingers. "I'll go to the Three DeaeYo awl have a 4-ink on my geoci</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>Re is a pork-butcher by trade, and he thought that you might like a nice brawn and some pig's puddings, black and white." "Gemini !" exclaimed Miss Pengersiek. "You couldn't have given me anything I like better. Here at Porthscawn we are badly off. There are two butchers, it is true, but neither of them do anything in porkery." "It is a separate branch," said Mr. Ham. . "I knew it," said Miss Pengersick ; "and that's one deficiency here. There is no one set up in that line. it is tin that there is a man as kills the pigs—pigs. kept by private persons, but none as sells porkery ; no brawn, no —chitterlings, no .sausage gisto be had. And I have always felt a void, a something wanting to make one's happi- ness complete. One gets tired of the ordinary butcher's meat, and wants some change. I cannot tell you how I shall enjoy these puddings. lam glad I have lived to this -day." ' "My dear," said Selina, "I hope you will have other occasion for that wish. What time do you peck ? " "At half-past twelve." "Then,, with your favour, I have a little com- mission in the place, and I will leave Mr. Joe with you. He will help to dish up. I know you will excuse me." Thereupon Miss Dunstone departed. Punctually at the hour named she returned. All was ready; clean napery, plates, glass, the cold leg of lamb at one end, a bowl Of salad —crisp lettuce, pale green ,with white hearts, deep green threads of Olives peeping out among the leaves, and here and there ruby points of radishes. Hot potatoes steamed, so did the cutlets at the other end of the table, bedded in mashed potatoes. On a side table stood an iron- stone bowl with junket crowned by 'golden cream, and a large dish of tartlets—raspberry and greengage. "May I ask you, Mr. Ham to preside at the leg of lamb ? " asked Miss Ham, "I will help the cutlets." When the three had taken their places, and as Rosina was about to put her spoon to the mashed potatoes, there sounded a tap at the door. Instead of rising, she called out: "Come in ! " Then entered- Mr. William Hawken and Mr. Thomas Hicks. "Oh, gentlemen!" exclaimed Rosina, rising; "so delighted to see you both, and so oppor- tunely to find how we relish your delightful presents. May I have the honour of introducing you to Miss Dunstone and to Mr. Joseph Ham ? Is it asking too great a favour if you will sit down and take a bite with us ? "</p>
        <p>. "Thank you, miss—but—" "Thank you kindly, miss—but--" ' "I will take no refusal, listen to no buts. We owe to your kindness this excellent leg of lamb and these dainty cutlets. I will lay knives and forks, and you must allow me to insist that you eat with us."</p>
        <p>The two butchers looked at each other, then cast a withering glance at 'A.D.. Ham, and hesitatingly submitted. After the first helping, Miss Pengersiek said, turning to liawken : "I am sure we are most indebted to you Lot these exquisite cutlets. I never tasted any sc good in my life. And really—when I had asked for a heart." _</p>
        <p>"The heart was at your disposal." "But you did not send it. "I would have torn it out of my bosom and presented it to you." "But I did not ask for yours." "Not for mine ? ". • "No—for a sheep's heart." "Ha!" shouted Hicks. "I said so!</p>
        <p>"I had none," replied Hawken gloomily. "And a bullock's heart .would have been too large for my private consumption," said Rosina. • "Ha, ha! " rejoiced Hicks. Then, turnbiE with a knowing 'leer to Miss Pengersick, hE said: "What you. promised •me-1 shall kg delighted to receive—in private," " promised ! I promised nothing," said Rosin&amp; •</p>
        <p>"Oh no! but you did. We'll talk of that by-andlbye—in private." " What was it? I protest—" exclaimed Miss Pengersick. "Presently—when we .are alone," said Hicks, knowingly. "But I wish to be told." "Not before company." Then Mr. Joe Ham rose to his feet. "Mr. Hicks. I must demand an explanation." "Mid I insist on it. What did I promise ? " "Ki.;ses," sniggered Hicks. - "Kisses ! " echoed Rosina and Joe Ham at once. .</p>
        <p>"Yes—kisses, if you will have it," said Hicks. "There you are—your own fist—read. 'My ever Jear '—that's me. 'How I long to see you that again is me. 'We shall meet and kiss.' There you are: 'and kiss' !—`and be happy in each other's society.' Here I am, and here are you, Rosie, my pet, my idol, my darling. Deny your words if you can: 'ever yours devotedly.' Ever devotedly to me—Thomas Hicks, buteher. Rosina; look! Your own handwriting." "Miss Pengersick," said Joe. Ham, stiffly, "if this be the .way in which you address other parties, then there is a party here, in the pork butchery line, who begs to withdraw from an arrangement " "Stay," said Miss Selina Dunstone. "There has been a mistake. Look at this letter that I received: 'Miss Pengersick will be obliged to be furnished with a shoulder of lamb.' The letters were put in the wrong envelopes. That which reached Mr. Hicks was intended for me."</p>
        <p>"I am satisfied," said Mr. Ham, sitting down. "Gentlemen, Miss Pengersick has consented to become Mrs. Ham."</p>
        <p>. . An hour later, at the Three Donkeys, Mr. Bill Hawken and Mr. Thomas Hicks were seated at the table, each with a glass before him. "I told you so," said Mr. Hawken. "And I told yeti," said Mr. Hicks: "She never would have done for either of us," observed Mr. Hawken. "Did you notice—there was only temperance drinks, no ale, no whisky, only lemonade. Bah!" "I'd rather have my glass, and be without the two thousand," remarked Hicks. "'Tisn't worth it." "No," said Hawken; "and that's what enigma mesnt—it's a new sort of teetotal drink!"</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PILES FOR FORTY YEARS.</head>
        <p>PILES FOR FORTY YEARS.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk Makes a Remarkable Cure.</p>
        <p>A naval pensioner, Mr. I'. Henderson, has unfolded to a "Torquay Times "reporter a sensational story of almost half a century's suffering from piles which defied every treatment but Zam-Buk. I served 18 years in the Royal Marines," explained Mr. Henderson, who lives at 1, Springfield Cottages, Plainmoor Road, St. Mary Church, Torquay, to the reporter. " During the greater part of that time I suffered from bleeding piles. I had often to sit for a long time on a wet boat seat, and I believe that caused the piles. I was really never out of the doctors' hands, and after spending a long time in Stonehouse Naval Hospital and undergoing four operations without getting any relief, I became such a wreck that I got my discharge and was invalided from the navy. That was in 1878.</p>
        <p>"For 30 years after my discharge from the navy I continued to suffer from the bleeding pile's, despite the special treatment I received from four or five doctors and the numerous ointments I used. My wife wife was always bringing something in for me to try, but everything proved useless until she brought home a box of Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>" When I started with Zam-Buk all I hoped for was a little relief. This I got very early on, and after I had been using Zam-Buk for a little time I had improved so much that hopes of a cure were revived. Gradually the terrible burning pciin was subdued, then the bleeding stopped altogether, and the piles were driven away. "Since Zam-Bul: cured me I have not had the slightest sign of a return, and I am feeling better in health now, in my seventieth year, than I have done for many years." Zam-Buk is put up only in sealed boxes and sold by chemists at Is. 11d. 2s. 9d., or 4s. 6d. a box. Direct at eame prices from The Zam-Buk Co., 45, Cow, Cross Street, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Matches for Saturday, September 11th. WeMeld V. Howley. Walkers v. Whitecross. Newton Amateurs v. Earlestown St. John's. Results of Match September 4th. Earlestown St. John's 3, Calber Mersey 0. The League have vacancies. Applications to R. E. Brandreth:Orford, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Musical Society was held in Cairo-street Schools on Monday evening. There Wf's a fair attendance. Letters of apology for non-attendance were read from MEszrs. J.C. Parr and Roger Parr.</p>
        <p>Air. R. T:FAIRCLOUGII on being elected to the chair said he was willing to do all be could for the benefit of the Musical Society as he always had done. He was sorry that Mr. J. C. Parr was not there to be able to take the chair.</p>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>RECENT DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. ATKIN (hon. secretary) presented his annual report as follows :—Before commencing the regular business of this meeting I feel it would be your wish, as well as my own, to pay a tribute cf respect to the memory of Mr. John White, whose loss this society has very special reason to mourn. Mr. White's close interest in its welfare was marked by his long ccnzinued and most active participation in its affairs as a member and, up to the very last days of his life, his support was most kindly and willingly afforded. His advice and influence as an amateur of more than average musical ability and taste was never failing when asked for, and the Musical Society has reason to feel that it can ill afford (as indeed Warrington generally does feel) the loss of such a true friend. lam sure it is your wish that we should record our sense of this and our deep sympathy with Mrs. White and his relatives. (Hear, hear.) By his death so suddenly since the close of the season 1908-9 the list of friends lost to the society is lengthened, and we cannot pass by without noticing the memory of such friends as Mr. John White, Mr. Reginald Silcock, Mr. W. B. Roberts, Mr. E. H. Clare, and Mr. J. Parkinson. Their names must stand in the respectful memory of us all, and we mourn the loss to the society to which they were all so devotedly attached. (Hear. hear.) The work of the society, -at least es regards the opera section, is almost continual from January to December, year after year and the season of 19C8-9 was once again opened by the performances of " Marrie England" during the week October 12th to 17th, 1908. I do not propose to touch upon the financial position of affairs as regards the opera or our regular series of sub- scription conceits, further than to say that, since it is obvious to you all that the finances are not in the con- dition we would wish, we have to remember that Warrington is not by any means alone, as other ton as and even large cities, have found the past season an impossible one from the standpoint of making money. Your committee has year after year to deal with the question as one of speculation, and year after year the difficulty of making both ends meet apparently gets greater. In doing so I think you may be sure your committee has not overlooked, and is not overlooking, the need for economy, and though possibly there is still room for further efforts in this direction, it is to be remembered that there is a limit beyond which the expenses cannot be cut down if the quality a the per- forniance and the high standard of the music given on the stage and concert platform are to be .maintained. (Applause.) No one will., I thick, deny the excellence of these opera and concert performances, and whilst I am far from advocating reckless expenditure, I think-I may well take this opportunity to plead for a much more genereus support of the society at its conceris from the public of Warringtcn and neighbourhood (Hear, hear). The treasurer(Mr.Reynolda)and your com- mittee have under consideration a scheme for a special endeavour to bring up again the very much diminished list of subscriptions, and the society is called to con- sider this-at a special meeting on the 21st of this month. Mr. Atkin referred to seNeral other matters of interest to the members, after which he said: Mr. J. P. Huxtable the (hon. registrar) has most carefully taken note of the weekly attendances of members at the practices, but has tot afforded me actual figures. 1 am inclined, however, to think that though the figures show a fairly good proportion, the possible is not attained and there is room for some improvement in the regularity of coming to weekly practices by certain members. The average should be larger than I make it appear by figures afforded me from the registrar. During the season (not including inforif al meetings, of which there were several) the committee were called together four times only, but each of those meetings were long sittings and the work done and arrangements made received very careful and somewhat anxious consideration. My personal thanks are due, and very freely offered, to the officials. Mr. Payton, Mr. Pearson Mr. Johnson and Mr. Huxtable have rendered, as always, their invaluable help to the society and to Mr. Reynolds and myself. (Applause.) The gentle. men who have so kindly acted as stewards for the sale of tickets by members also are entitled to, and are most cordially offered my thanks. I could wish that each and every member would, if possible, take a still greater interest in the business side of the society's affairs,and I most sincerely hope that the efforts still to be made will result in a largely increased subscription list, so that the society may be placed in the position of being able to carry out its work with less anxiety as to its financial status, and continue to do the undoubtedly good work from a musical and artistic standpoint, which I am pleased to think it is doing, and has done for so many years. (Applause.) He also referred to the bad condition of the organ in the Parr Hall, and he had communicated to the Town Clerk about it who replied that the committee had decided to do something with it. The CHAIRMAN said they were all obliged to Mr. Atkin for his elaborate report. He moved that the best thanks be accorded to him and also for his services of last year. He was sure Mr. Atkin had done everything it was possible for any person to do. Mr. A. EVANS seconded and it was uranimously carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolts P. REYNOLDS submitted the treasurer's report. The following are the receipts :—Members' subscriptions, £5O, concert subscriptions, £76 16s. 10d., sale of tickets and programmes, £B7 13s. 3d., total, £214 10s. Id. The following is the exp.nditure :—Pro- fessional assitance, £169 2s. 6d.; hire of hall, school, mus'c, &amp;c., £4O 7s. 4d.; printing and advertising, £62 Bs. lid. ; sundries—new music stands, £1 155., miscellaueocs,£2l 12s. Hid. ;total £295 ss. 11d., showing aloss on the reason of £BO Is. 10d. There were losses on all the three concerns, viz.: 1, £34 17$. 3d.; 2, £2O 10s. 20., and 3, £25 Bs. 44d. It was not a satisfactory report. The society always had been handicapped, and he was afraid always would be unless some very big effort were made. They *1 had certain schemes under considera- tion with a view to increasing the subscriptions and also if it were possible to increase the reserve fund. The operas for the last two seasons had proved unprofitable notwithstanding the excellency of them and the pains that bad been taken. They had been going back seriously this last reason. Most other towns were in a similar position financially. There were not many places which could boast of choirs such as Warrington could. All the choirs in Warrington had benefited by the Musical Society. It was a positive disgrace to Warrington that there Ives only very slightly over one subscriber out of every thousand of people in Warrington. (Hear, hear). It ought to be altered. They wanted to get the subscription list up a lot higher than it was at present. Last year it ens £B6, whilst some time ago it was £3eo. That was partly due to the reduction in the prices to get more people. If they bad kept to tbe sune prices they would have been in a bet,er position, if they had got a small attendance. They really wanted to get £lOOO in hand which would give them an income of £4O a year and then they would be able to snap their fingers at anybody. (Applause). Mr. CHARLES WHITE irtmoving the adoption of the report said he did not see why so much should be spent upon refreshments. He thought the professional singers only ought to have refreshments. If people could not go from 6 o'clock till 10 o'clock without anything to eat it was a great pity indeed. Mr. J. NEWCOMBE seconded.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN thought they were very much indebted to Mr. Reynolds for his report and for the trouble he had taken. He had one suggestion to make and that was that Mr. Reynolds should publish in next years accounts the preceding accounts as well. He was sorry that the Musical Society had not done so well. Of course they -all knew the benefit the Musical Society had teen to the town and neighbonihood. Every choir in the district had been made better in some respect by that Soeiety, because nearly all had been members at one time or another. They were really doing a public work and they ought to have better subscriptions than they were having at the present time. (Hear. hear.) Previous to the opera they bad gone round with the bat and they thought that if they could get one going they would get money without begging from anybody. He hoped the Opera Committee would try and make it as successful as it was in the previous years. The adoption of the accounts and a vote of thanks to Mr. Reynolds were passed. The CHAIRMAN proposed the re-election of Mr. J. C. Parr as representative of the Society for the ensuing year. They knew the interest he had taken in the Society, and he was sure they could not find anyone who could take a better interest than he. (Applause). Mr. NEWCOMBE seconded and it was carried. The following were elected vice-presidents for the ensuing year :—The Mayor (Alderman W. J. Forshaw), Messrs. Z. Armitage, It. T. Fairclough, R. C. Parr, F. Monks, R. Pierpoint, C. E. Parker, John White, Captain Crosfleld, H. Roberts, Jo!in Fairclough, A. H. Crosfleld, M.P., F. A. Frost, J. Monro, W. N. Hutchings, W. E. Atkin, James Dodds, Hutton, W. P. Rylands and Canon Morley Stevenson. The CHAIRMAN proposed the re-election of Mr. W. E. Atkin as secretary. He thought they had hardly a single member of the society who could fill the position as he could.</p>
        <p>There was no need for a seconder and the motion was carried unanimously. Mr. ATKIN replying said it was getting on for 20 years, with just a break of one year,and then they made him treasurer, that he had been secretary. He did not see why tbe willing horse should do all the work. With their co-operation he would certainly do his best to try to make things go smoothly for the treasurer. He did not think that the work could be carried on more harmoniously than it hae been. Continuing, he proposed Mr. Reynolds as treasurer. Mr. Reynolds had taken infinite pains with</p>
        <p>the accounts and he was also doing something w.hich they would be pleased to hear. He was compiliw4 a history of the society and it would soon be completed. (Applause.) Mr. NEWCOMBE seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. REYNOLDS replying said that last year's was a very disappointing result, but he was 'willing to take it on for. another year to see if they could make it better. Referring to the history of the society which he was compiling he said it was in the press. It was all very well for the officers of the society to work,but they could do comparatively little unless they wyre helped by the members.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the CHAIRMAN, Mr. A. Field. house was ro-elected auditor.</p>
        <p>Mr. Johnson Was re-elected Librarian on the proposi- tion of Mr. PA MN, seconded by Mr. Fieldhouse. Mr. HUXTABLE was also re-elected registrar. Mr. FRANK H. CROSSLEY, the conductor, said That the society was a very harmonious one. The work was so thoroughly, genuinely and good-heartedly done. There was never a hitch in any way, and that made the work of the society very pleasant. (Applause.) He felt a personal debt of gratitude to Mr. Payton for his excel- lent accompanimen's and also to Mr. Pearson. With regard to the coming season, the first concert would be held on November 17th and the second on Boxing Day At the first concert "Elijah" would be given, and arrangements were practicully complete for Sir Charles Santley to be present. This sear being the centenary of Mendelssohn's birth it made a very good opportunity. (Applause.) rhe "Messiah"would be given on Boxing Day. The date of the last concert was not fixed, but it Nv.s intended to give something of quite a different char- acter, as the work of Edward German, "The Princess of Kensington," would take up half of the concert. It was extremely melodious and very taking and pretty. Akre tenors and basses were wanted for the choruses, and he suggested with reference to the meeting to be held to discuss the financial position of the society that the present committee should have power to add to their number to canvass for new subscribers, and that ladies should also be members of the committee. (Hear, hear.) He hoped they would have a successful season. (Applause.) Mr. CHARLES WHITE proposed and Mr. NEWCOMBE seconded a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Fairclongh for presiding. Replying the CH XIItNI AN said he thought he was the oldest member of the society. He bad certainly been connected with it for over 40 years and during the whole of that time be had only missed three concerts. He thought that that was a fair record. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Youngest Peer.—Lord de Clifford, the young peer, who was married three or four years ago, to the actress Miss Eva Carrington, and last wcek met with a fatal accident whilst motoring in the neiahhourhood of Brighton, was the twenty-sixth holder of his title. He was one of the six peers who have had as many as i twenty predecessors n their titles, with whom should be included Lady de Ros, who is the twenty-fifth holder of the title, which dates from the year 1264. The late Lord de Clifford was only twenty-five' and his son who now succeeds him will be three next January. He has the distinction of being the youngest living peer, and the next youngest is the Marquis of Donegal], who was born in the year 1903. The King in Scotland.—The King, after being the guest of Lord and Lady &amp;wile, at Rufford Abbey, for the Doncaster Race Meeting, will commence a round of shooting visits, and is to go to Scotland next week. His Majesty is expected to be the guest of the Duke and Duchess of Sutherland at Dunrobin Castle, after a short stay at Balmoral, and for this reason the Scottish celebrations in honour of Lord Stofford's coming-of-age, were put off. The King was last at Dunrobin Castle in 1902, but as Prince of Wales he was present in 187 2at the festivities there, which marked the coming of age of the present Duke of Sutherland, and, as on that occasion, every kind of entertainment is to be provided at the forthcoming celebrations. Lady Rosemary Levetion-Gower, the only daughter of the Duke and Duchess of Sutherland, is now 16, and is to ,finish her education in Germany in company with her cous'n Lady Enid Pane. ,The two girls are to make their entry into Society in the Season 1911.</p>
        <p>Holidays for One.—Mr. Sidney Low declares that the matrimonial experiences of authors, are often unfortu- nate, because the husbAnd and wife have too much of one.another's company. That we know was unhappily the case with Thomas Carlyle and his ;sire, and Mr. Low's views seem also to be shared by the husband of the young wife, who recently applied to a Londcn magistrate to ask "Has my husband any right to go for holidays without taking me ? " Opinions are divided as to whether it is best for husband and wife to take their holidays together or separately, but this was obviously one of those cases in whioh there are to be only holidays for one, and the husband, with his accustomed chivalry, thinks it best to take them himself. Wedding Presents.—Just as the autumn wedding season is setting in, an engaged couple publicly announce their desire that no presents be sent them on ,their marriage The protesting couple are to live in the Soudan, where the bridegroom-elect holds a high official position, and one can understand the inconveni- ence of taking out h number of salad bowls, cruets, salt-cellars, candlesticks, afternoon tea cloths, cake- stands, and various other articles mostly more showy than useful. But probably the avoidance of trouble on their part is not so much the motive of this self-denying ordinance as the desire of the engaged couple to spare their friends the obligation to give wedding presents; a custom which in certain grades of society has become a heavy tax upon mere acquaintances. They are not by any means the first engaged couple to relieve their friends in this way, hut it would be a good thing if their erimple were more generally followed in these circles where wedding presents are not essential to the start of a married life. It is different with middle- cases brides and bridegrooms, and in cases where wedding presents are a genuine assistance Us the setting up of housekeeping, and presents and the trousseau are so large a part of the charm of an engagement fcr the bride, tbatoi hero this necossity exists, people will always cheerfully give presents, according to their means. _</p>
        <p>Attire.—There has been a controversy going on in one of the London papers, concerning the true philosophy of clothes. There were letters from the "mere man," with remarks about the absurdities of the clothing of women, from the narrow pointed shoes, into which she squeezes her toes, to the enormous millinery creations she carries on her head, and there were retorts from the other side concerning the ugliness and the monotonous utilitarian uniformity of men's attire. Obviously the subject contains an endless element of disputation. It depends upon one's point: of view on such matters, but it may be s del that the poets have always been on the side of the extravagantly dressed woman, Pope was, for one, and 'Thomson says in his lines on beauty One thing I mind—a spreading hoop she wore, Than nothing which adorns a rady more— Women, it must be admitted, often do make themselves ridiculers by wearing extreme eccentricities, but men have always encouraged them in the extravagances of dress incidental to being a feminine person. Men at any rate should congratulate themselves upon the fact that custom has imposed on them the least inconvenient form of dress, and has relieved them from the thousand worries consequent upon the ceaseless change and infinite range and variety of woman's attire. Ermin to the Fore.—The recent cold snap brought furs well to the fore in shopland, and never perhaps before have we witnessed such.a display of ermin. In times gone by, the idea was rampant, that of all furs, ermine was The least desirable, that is, from the point of view of becomingness, but we have at length, and fortunately, discovered that though perhaps it is wiser to discard it when the bleak and cutting winds of winter and spring are playing havoc with our desirable possession. For dressy afternoon wear, long stoles made up of strips of ermine, alternated with strips of lace, or gathered chiffon, ending in a collection of tails and chiffon ball-fringe, are seen at the movement and suggest a capital and most desifable way of utilising the narrow bands of fur that can so often be "picked up "cheaply at sale times, or in late summer. White Felt Hats.—Owners of white felt hats that did duty last winter, and which, save for a layer of dust and spots, 'ire possible wear this autumn, will find that the following process speee'ly removes all signs of former wear, and that under it the hat appears as fresh and dainty as though it were perfectly new. First and foremost, of course, all trimming must te removed, and the bat well brushed, then a paste made of one penny- worth of powered calcined magnesia, and cold water should be rubbed on to the felt with a moderately stiff nail brush. The bat should then be laid aside until perfectly dry, when another perfectly clean brush should be employed to remove the powder and with it all traces of dust, spots and general grime. The Care of Crushable Clothes.—There are two paints wearers of crushable costumes should bear in mind, if they desire to.preserve a smart appearance, namely the necessity for ironing all such garments after each time</p>
        <p>of wearing, and of raising coat tails when sitting down. Many women utterly refuse to consider the latter idea, though why it should be more ungraceful cariied out by a woman than it is by a man, it is hard to under- stand. ./V little practice soon enables a woman to eisilY and gracefully draw aside coat tails, and the benefics accruing to her by persevering in this direction are inestimable.</p>
        <p>Curious Skirts.—A new skirt that is finding much favour shows a group of flat pleats on either side =915 perfectly plain panel both back and front. The skirt is quite short, so that it stands in no need of being raised from the ground, a procedure calculated to prove a very difficult and certainly inartistic one. An by the way, it should be bourne in mind that like so rainy of the modes, this skirt is only suitable for the. long of limb and slender, and quite impossible for the "abundant," or " chubby" as they prefer to be called. MADGIC.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>BOY DROWNED AT LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>BOY DROWNED AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Inquest.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coroner Brighouse held an inqueaton Wednesdor at the Borough Police Court, on Harry Wroncb, aged nine, who lived with his mother, Mrs. Ann Parkinson, 117, 'Mehrall-lane, Latchford, and who was drowned in' the Manchester Ship Canal on Monday afternoon. Mrs. Ann Parkinson, wife of John Parkinson, an mother of Harry Wrench, said thlt her first busbancE died 18 months ago. Her son left borne about one o'clock on Monday to go to school. In the afternoon she went into the town and on returning at five o'clock was met by her son George, aged 16, who told her that Harry had been drownei in the Manchester Shin Canal. She went to the canal and at about eight o'clock the body was recovered. She identified it as the body of her son.</p>
        <p>Alexander Everett, aged nine, living with his mother, in Thelwall-lane, Warrington, stated that about four o'clock on the date named, be came out of Christ Church schools, and with George .Burr and deceased went for a, walk along the canal. When near Latchfor.l Railway Station they saw a boy named Jones in a garden on the opposite side of the canal and they asked him to throw a, pcaracross the water.lt fell into the water near to where. they were. Deceased tried to retch it and he fell into the water. Witness along with his friend ran and told Mrs. Cotteril and then two men came down and they- started to grapple for the body. Alfred Jones, an orphan, employed by 1%1154 Massy, us an orrand boy and living at 50, Hopwooc.l-- street, said that he threw the pear. After he threw it he turned away. George Burr, aged 8, 64, Thelwall-lane, corroborate' Everett's evidence and added that they told deceased not to go, but he took no notice. William Cook in the employ of the Manchester slap Canal Co., said he helped to recover the body. In repll to the coroner witness said that the boys were trespass— ing when they walked along the canal bank. The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death:'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>TUIE APPETITE THAT FAILED.</head>
        <p>TUIE APPETITE THAT FAILED.</p>
        <p>Acute Suffering followed this early Sign of Indigestion. A recent Cure by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>The fact that food of any kind had become dis- tasteful to Mrs. Walker, a Manchester lady, was not regarded seriously by her at first. But when this loss of appetite developed into the acute sufferings of Indigestion, which seemed to be aggravated by ordinary medicines, Mrs. Walker's health became- precarious. A friend advised her to take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and recently Mrs. 'Walker, who resides at 29, 'Hardman Street, Manchester, explaineul. how these Pills cured her :</p>
        <p>Became very depres:</p>
        <p>"I had usually beel able to enjoy the good things of life,"remarked Mrs. Walker," but not since I lost all appetite. The mere sight of food</p>
        <p>caused me to turn faint and sick. Even the daintiest food was repellent. The cause of this distaste soon: proved to be acute Indigestion,and for months I never knew what it was to be free from torture arising from my being unable to digest any nourishment. After every mouthful of food, sharp stabbing pains seized me in the chest and right across the shoulders. Then' the 'food would remain undigested on my stomach like a heavy weight. "I was so short of breath that I had difficulty 111- walking about. The undigested food gave rise to flatulence that caused great distress and my heart would beat wildly; sometimes the pain was so - agonising that I feared my heart was affected. In time I became very depressed, my strength gave waY, and I grew so weak and nervous that I hardly knew - how to get through the day's work. "As time went on I had to live for days together on nothing but a little tea and dry bread. FrequentlY I trembled from head to foot, and dizzy attacks often- seized me so that I had to sit down to save myself from falling. "I was constantly taking medicine of different sorts. but these common drugs caused internal irritation ancr only aggravated the Indigestion. Finally, a friend advised me to, take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, telling me that they had cured her of Indigestion. I took her advice, and-was only on the second box of the Pills when there was an improvement. I was more cheer- ful and a little stronger. I was able to eat; what I took agreed with me, and there was no pain in the chest and ba3k ; no heavy feeling in the stomach. 313" nerves were braced up, and the shakiness and trerab- ling left me. Through continuing with the Pills I was able to resume my ordinary diet and enjoy everything I took. Daily I grew stronger; indeed, lam now better ja health and spirits than I have been for years." Disorders of the Digestion have been dispelled repeat3dly by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, because these Pills create New, Good Blood, that strengthens tiler' debilitated organs of the stomach. They have al° cured Ancemia, Eczema and Skin Disorders; Rheu- matism, Sciatica, and Nervous Diseases. TheY strengthen the nerves of weak men, and cure the ills of women. Sold by all dealers, but accept only the, genuine Pills, always labelled in full: "Dr. Williams Pink Pills for Pale People." If in,doubt send 2s. 9d• for one box, or I3s. 9d. for six, to Dr. -Williams Co, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London.</p>
        <p>VEGETABLE GARDEN: NOTES FOR THE AMATEUrt• —Take advantage of dry weather for earthing up Celq,7, and Leeks and tying up Lettuces and Endives. 1'1" up south borders with Lettuces and Endive for winter use. Some of these may be lifted and planted in frames later. Radishes and Mubtard and Cress should be soya under glass now. Draw earth up to the sterns of Brussels Sprouts, Cauliflowers, and other greens, t° support the stems. When the Onions are placed nuclei cover, prepare the ground for Cabbages. A dressing ° soot lightly forked in will, says a gardener in " Garden" ing Illustrated," be all the preparation required, as the land was done well for Onions. Make a further sowin., of hardy Lettuces, to remain in the seed-bed for tbg winter. Reduce the foliage of Tomatoes, and stop :11., leaders. Thin young Turnips to 12 inches apart, clear off all old crops of Peas, Cabbages, etc., and trenca the land.</p>
