      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>MoTBA</head>
        <p>MoTBA</p>
        <p>FA- TM</p>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>I think there will be more interest at the present trioutent in the team and prospects for to-day's match, than in comments on the Abortive engagement with Lelgh, which must therefore take second place.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt Warrington ought to win at uochdale, though they failed by one point to accom- plish the feat last season. The Hornets do not seem to have mastered all their difficulties, and on Saturday were badly beaten at Barrow. Taking a line through this result Warrington should earn the maximum Points this afternoon. Of course, I am taking it for granted that they can play better than they did last aturday.</p>
        <p>The selected team has created a surprise in the tOWE, for not only is O'Malley down to play,but O'Neill "Pears in the list. The Australian arrived during e Week-end, and looks the picture of physical fitness. i•ne is, moreover, eager for the fray and has expressed confidence in Warrington doing great things this season when one or two 'weak spots have been remedied. O'Neill has written expressing his willing- nices to return to the side and has accordingly been Placed on the list for to-day. With men of the stamii Pr O'Malley, O'Neill, and Thomas in the front rank there should be an improvement in handling when the 13all comes loose. O'Neill has already proved how Ploeh a forward can do in the way of making open- and the reputation of O'Malley as a loose zerward is second to none.</p>
        <p>Dickenson has soon joined the ranks of the injured, but his arm is expected to be alright again with another week's rest. He got it badly strained in trying to tackle Clarkson by the legs. Lloyd has been relegated to the" A" team, and Jenkins put down to partner brooks. After Tilley's slow-footed exhibition of Saturday last it could hardly be expected that he would tetain the confidence of the , committee, and young kclntyre has been promoted on the strength of a fine exhibition at Leigh. There are great hopes of the Stockton Heath lad, and if he will make safety his watchNiord, and not attempt too much, he should train wi into a class back.</p>
        <p>The team selected for kochdale is as follows : ticlntyre, full back; Fish, Cartwright, Taylor and radahaw,threequarters;jenk ins and Brooks,half backs 0 SMalley. Thomas, O'Neill, Shuomrs, Boardman and armer, forwards.</p>
        <p>,I am sorry I have no good news of Frawley. The t'glit winger 's injured ankle has made slow progress, and he has had to consult a specialist this week. As result he is now on crutches, a broken bone having peen discovered. Calling in a Specialist after three Ijeeks is like shutting the door when the horse has ;_ieell stolen. Frawley will probably be a month 'afore he is fit.</p>
        <p>The forward whom Warrington were hoping to 'lave secured last week was one of O'Malley's late .ona.rades who was expected with him. Unfortunately ;no player in question sustained a broken leg in his zast match in Australia and had to forego the trip to llgland. No effort will be spared to strengthen the tam, and other surprise captures may be looked for afore long.</p>
        <p>e Now for Saturday's match with Leigh. The best ning about it was the " gate," which exceeded all Vtpectations. Such a solid ring of spectators has not ueen seen at an opening match before. This fact, a„Ild the way members are pouring in, augurs well for Lne support of the club during the season. Enter- rising management and good football will soon be Quad to be paying their way. Leigh are to be con- patulated on getting at point at Wilderspool. They !lad. some lucky escapes, but the defence held out to '''te end.</p>
        <p>Warrington didlot play well, but they were a bit oetter than the visitors. Good football was out of the Pestion in such a downpour, but where Warrington led most noticeably was at half-back and full back. ;;;Ith a nippier man than Lloyd working the scrum carrington must have gathered points in the second t but it was "like to be.', Fortunately Leigh Were no better off in the same quarter, and the policy °I playing Bolewski at half stood condemned. He Was indistinguishable from the ruck, and if I Were a Leigh man I should want something 1310 re promising for the investmerit of such a large run in one player. With Clarkson playing so finely. t is difficult to see how the Mather-lane men are 4°lng to get the value of the Kangaroo. The Leigh c,astodian was in grand form and emphasized the fact !slat Warrington were badly in need of a successor to Pley. The latter—ever loyal and a trier—has had 1118 day, Youth must be served!</p>
        <p>The Combination champions rendered a good ecount of themselves at Leigh, where they triumphed "Y the margin of 13 points to nil—all gained in,the Zecond half. Cat twright scored a couple of tries, and 'Lae his chance to-day in superior company.</p>
        <p>The Hornets " A " visit Warrington and will meet ifte following thirteen : Daintith, full back; Oakes, 'Jordan, Landor, and Hockenhall, threequarters ; Kay a_rid Lloyd, half backs; Cheaters, Naylor, P. Board- ran, Bradshaw, Worrall and Wright, forwards. The "'to latter are local lads Who are on trial.</p>
        <p>To-day's matches are as follows : Rull v. Leeds. Pewsbury v. Bramley. tlunslet v. Keighley. Bradford v. Barrow. Halifax v. Merthyr Tydvil. Huddersfield v. Broughton Rangers. Widnes v Batley. Wakefield Trinity V. Wigan. Treherbert v. Hull Kingston Rovers. Salford v. Oldham. 1-4,,eifth v. Swinton. Aochdale Hornets v. Warrington. St. Helens v. Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Eiffel Tower Eiffel Tower Lemonade is the most delicious; cool, refreshing drink obtainable—so health- ful, so convenient, So cheap. A 4id. bottle makes 2 gallons of delicious Lemonade. Lemonade</p>
        <p>04 ~LL • 4, tia % - eTIC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S Ai-NIMES. THE NOMHERN UNION LEAGUE. *</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. *Leeds *Wigan 5 1 13 Hunslet.. 0 1 3 *Batley _ 2 3 .13 Bradford Nortb. 1 1 5 *Warrington *Huddersfield *York *Barrow Hull *Keighley 1 1 5 Wakefield T. ... 1 0 2 *Runcorn *Hull Kingston R 3 3 15 Oldhsm *Swinton St. llelens 2 2 10 *Broughton R. 2 1 7 . . . .</p>
        <p>* denotes home club.</p>
