      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Warrington v. Leigh—No Score.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Leigh—No Score.</p>
        <p>The result of the first engagement of the season was disappointing to the Warrington men vs h.) had to be content with a pointless draw. Both sides were well repreEonted end a very hard game was played, but the persistent downpour of rain prevented any display of combination and rcduced the sides to a level. The most, ' gratifying feature from a Warrington pint of view was the fact that the gate -was the largest ever seen at an opening enpgement and nrimberrd almut 8,000, £l3B was taken at the turrstiles and in members' subscription,. Frawley, the Australian three-quarter, was unable to take his place in the Warrington ranks owing to an injured ankle. Isherwood,fer ta dive years' Warrington's left centre, received a cr.rdial welcome' when he appeared as Captain of Leigh. The teams were composed as follows:— Warrington.—Tilley, back : Fish, Dickenson,Taylor, and Bradshaw, three-quarter backs ; Brooks and Lloyd, half backs ; Thomas,P. Boardman, A. E. Boardman, Belton, Cheaters, and Shugars, for war ds. - Leigh.—Clarkson, back; T. Johnson, S. Johnson, Isherwood and Davie', thrce-quarter backs ; Bolewski and Battersby, half backs; O'Neil, Smith, G llop, Lee, Woods, and Jones, forwards. Referee, Mr. Taylor, Hunslet. Torch julges, MEssrs. H. Ball, Leigh, and J. R. Lae ton, Oldham. Warrrington won the tr as and the forwards early rushed the ball to the railway goal, where Clarkson was called upon. A most effective clearance was the result and from a pack near the half-way line Battersby got away and the ball was handled in turn by Isherwood and Davies, but the Welshman was soon upset by Bradshaw, who assisted the " Wirepullets " to reach the Leigh line, Clarkson staying a fine dribble in brilliant style. The vis;ting custodian was next tacklei in possession and from the ensuing pack the home forwards rushed the ball over, where Shugars had hard luck for a try. Thomas was hurt and was absent from the field of play for several minutes. Leigh profited by the laxity of finish on the .rart of the three-quarters by forcing matters beyond the half-way line, where the brothers Johnson, with a concerted movement, caused Tilley trouble. A penalty against the visitors was well utilized by Fish, whilst a second, taken by the same player a minute later; fell short of the desired goal: O'Neil gave Davies a pass and this player at once made tracks along the reserve touch. He failed to escape the clutches of Bradshaw who tackled accurttely. Passing by the visitors was nullified by Fish, who intercepted and looked likely for a long run, !alien he slipped and was immediately overhauled. Another attack by Bradshaw saw Clarkson maintain his brilliant form, and the return negatived the attempt to invade. A weak kick fell into the hands of Davies, who made but a poor attempt to break through. Faulty passing in the direction of Fish ended in Clarkson again saving his side. Bradshaw made a capital effort on the right wing until he was pulled up by Isherwood, and next the Leigh players swept down the open on the home line,where the •.‘ Wirepullers " were penalized, and O'Neil bad a futile shot at goal. At half-time there was no score. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The homesters showed fine form on resuming, and Dickenson charged Isherwood 's kick near the line. He dribbled past Clarkson grandly and would have scored but for the post being in the way, and giving Leigb time to Make the ball dead. Warrington plodded on aud Leigh could not escape their quarters. A round of passing_between Brooks, Lloyd, Taylor, Dickenson and Fish witnessed the wing man make a brilliant effort, but superior numbers told their tale, and Fish was held a yard inside play. Warrington continued to raid the pavilion goal, until Chesters was just a trifle too prema- ture in following up a return from Tilley, and the subsequent penalty _gave the Leigh men welcome relief. A sharp dribble by Fish was well met by S. Johnson, who kicked back to the quarter-line, where Dickenson gained possession and tried a drop at goal. A great cheer went up, but the Warrington spectators were dissppointed,for the ball bit the post and rebounded into the field of play. A moment later the Warrington side was weakened when Dickenson, in tackling Clark- son, was injured and took no further part in the match. Bradshaw was well held near the line, but near the close Leigh made a great effort and Fish was forced to kick dead. Leigh were penalized for foul tactics and the final stages were of a scrappy nature. Both Lee and T. Johnson were hurt, and when the final whistle went for " no side " neither side had been able to score a point. Result, a draw._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>RUNCORN'S FINE RECOVERY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'S FINE RECOVERY.</p>
        <p>About 2,000 spectators assembled at Runcorn for the opening match with Salford. The visitors brought a good team, while on the home side Padbury, the captain, was an absentee, owing to an injury. Richardson com- menced for Runcorn, and Salford were hard pressed. Preston and Lomas quickly transferred play to the other end, but Jolley saved. Lomas opened the score with a penalty goal from almost mid-field, and had another shot at goal without success. Jolley, Watton, and Moran gained considerable ground, and Buckler was caught in possession. A moment later, however, Lomas dashed alongside touch in brilliant fashion, and evading the home tacks, scored in a good position but failed with the goal kick. Salford continued to attack for a time, Jolley eventually transferring play to the Salford half, where Watton kicked across to Hughes,' who ran over, scoring behind the posts, Jolley just missing the goal. Several fine dashes by Watton ended in Walker' Richardson, and Butterworth dribbling over the Salford line,,Butterworth grounding the ball in a good position for Moran to add a goal. Half-time : Runcorn 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points), Salford 1 try (3 points). After the interval a round of passing by the visiting backs was frustrated by Hughes, who rent the ball to John, who, losing possession, enabled Butterworth to pick up and score another try, which Richardson failed to convert. Salford played on the defensive for some time, and it was only the brilliant play of John which prevented several scores. Near the close Jolley got possession from the pack and dropped a fine goal. Final result4—Runcorn 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points), Salford 1 goal and 1 try (5 points). Teams Runcotn—Harrison, back ; Deakin, Butterworth, Moran, and Hughes three-quarter backs ; Jolley and Watton, half tacks; Walker, Richardson, Woods Langley, Kennedy, and McDonald, forwards. Salford—John, Sack;. Hymn, Adams, Thomas and Cooke. three-quarter backs ; E. Thomas, Buckler, Rees. Warwick, Curzon and Thom, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. 11. Wood, Huddersfield. Touch judges, Messrs. T. Austin, Wigan, and F. Ratcliffe, Sw inton. WIDNES GAIN A POINT.</p>
        <p>Swinton opened the season with a visit from the Widnes team. The game from the start was of a very poor description. The opening stages of the game found the home side attacking, but they failed to press home their advantage. Barnett finishing up the attack with an ineffectual drop at goal. Folio% ing this Widnes took up the attack, and for a considerable time Swinton were penned in their own half. Eventu- ally Lloyd kicked the ball away from a scrummage, and Wallwark mulling, lace easily scored, but Lally missed the goal. Crossing over with three points against them, the home side played up much better, and they made repeated attacks on the Widnes line. However, the visitors defended stubbornly, and for a time held the home side well in hand. A fine piece of work by Gastrell enabled Wharton to cross and equalise the scores, but Da,vids failed to improve, and the result-was Swinton 1 try (3 points), Widnes 1 try (3 points). Teams : Swinton—Thomas, back; Wallwarlr, Young, Parker,</p>
        <p>and Davies, three-quarter backs; Jones and Whartan, half backs • Simister, Barnett, Pollitt, Neen, Davies and Gartrell, forwards. Widnes—Jones, back ; lace, Taylor, Read, and Lambert, three-quarter backs; Lally and Noon, half backs ; S. Aspey, E. Aspey, Carr, Lloyd, M'Arthur, and Rigby, forwards Referee, Mr. Ennion. FINE PERFORMANCE BY ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>St. Helens opened the season at Broughton on Satur- day, before about 9,000 spectators. The Rangers were very unfortunate' as they lost the services of Ediss, who sustained a broken arm almost at the start of the game while Harris also retired at the beginning of the second half. In the first half Broughton were much in front on the play, and Ruddick scored an unimproved try. , The second half came as a revelation to the onlookers. After James had increased, the home,sides lead w ith a penalty goal. Briers got over for St. Helens, after floe play by Flanagan and Toole. Turtill kicked a goal, whilst shortly afterwards Tienwith placed his ride in front with a finely dropped goal. James levelled the game again with a penalty goal from mid-field, but close on time Flanagan scored an unimproved try for St. Helens. Result :—St.Helrns 2 goals 2 tries (10 points), Broughton Rangers 2 goals 1 try (7 points). Teams;— Broughton Rangers—M'Cabe, back; Harris, C. James, Jackson, and Hardyman, three-quarter backs; Flynn and George half Lacks ; Ruddick, J. L. Clampitt, R. Clampitt, 13( etham, Darlison, and Ediss, forwards. St. Helens—Turtill, back; Flanagan, Toole, Green- wood, and Harris, three-quarter backs : Kelly and Frenwith, half backs ; Prescott, Briers, Lee, Lilley Johnson, and Bradley, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>*Salford A Warrington A 2 3 13 *Leigh A 0 0 0 *Wigan Highfield 2 416 Leigh Shamrocks 0 0 0 *Rochdale Hor. A 2 313 Ince</p>
        <p>Broughton Ran. A 1 3 11 *St, Helens A... 0 1 3</p>
        <p>WARRING TON " A " V. LEIGH "A."</p>
        <p>The Combination champion 3 opened the season with a capital win at Leigh. There was a good crowd. Leigh had the breeze behind them in the first half and were the first to attack. A resolute defence was, how- ever, set up by Cartwright, Jordan and Mclntyre, and effective clearances were the order. Leah failed at an easy penalty, whilst Whittaker also made a poor attempt with a drop at goal. Turning round, the champions soon made their presence felt and the game had not long been restarted before Cartwright put on the finishing touches to some capital passing. Another try to Cartwright as followed by Holbrook putting on a try, and Mclntyre landing two goals/the Warringtonians thus won by two goals three tries (13 points), to Leigh nil.</p>
      </div>
