      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>GOSSIP ON THE GAMES.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP ON THE GAMES.</p>
        <p>BY B. BENNISON.</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED BY JACK DODWORTH.</p>
        <p>How sympathetic is the working man with the cause of the Players was strikingly shown when Manchester United appeared at Clayton to en- gage in their first match with Bradford City. If ever the Players were in doubt as to whether their point of view was shared by the epectators it was dispelled absolutely when they took the field at Clayton. They took their places to a roar of applause. and the outburst of enthu- siasm, unrehearsed as it -was, must have had a tremendous influence in bringing about a per- manent settlement of all differencee. One is glad that a halt has been called in the war between the Football Association and the playere, for the reason that on the first day of the season the public showed that .they were just as keen as ever on the game. I do believe that the quarrel which has been carried on for 60 long will do an. immense amount of good; it ' has told all:foot- ballers in quite an unexpected way that they</p>
        <p>have a constituency to whom they can, and do, appeal; and this knowledge will tend to the betterment of the game in every way. The foot- baller, in spite of himself, will be more anxious to do well and be more keenly interested in his profession, and as a natural sequel the game will become improved. So far the matches have revealed an enthusiasm that makes for general good—l mean the enthusiasm of the Players themselves, and the desire which has been very apparent to do well. Bradford City Begin Well. -After their many troubles, everybody will be pleased to know that Bradford City—a much better team last season than most people sup- posed; I really do not think that actual results in regard to the play of the Yorkshire- men mattered in the slightest degree in one's estimate of their capabilities—have begun their campaign in a very pronnsing way. They cer- tainly gave the Cupholders a very good game, and the impression they conveyed was that they were very little inferior to Manchester United. And in speaking of Bradford City as a team that knew much trouble last term one must be im- pressed with the beginning of Aston Villa. Their opening of a new programme has been more in keeping with the past reputation of the club. The fact that the Villa beat Bolton Wan- derers is significant; the news that they played really brilliant football is most eloquent of mean- ing; if the Astoa Villa players can live up to their start; then We shall' have a combination of men who will uphold the best traditions of As- sociation football.</p>
        <p>The Ever-Green Bloomer. Middlesbrough have been so used to strug- gling that their very heavy defeat of Sheffield Wednesday must have put their supporters in high feather. At Owlerton Stephen Bloomer, after-all his years of service, was quite an out- standing personality. It is difficult, when you see the old Derby County man playing, to realise that I</p>
        <p>. &gt;. . '</p>
        <p>Chelsea beat Liverpool 2 to I at Stamford</p>
        <p>Bridge</p>
        <p>of footballers. I always think that Bloomer stands as an object-lesson to every young player. If ever there was a master in style it is Bloomer. Even now he is quite by himself in bringing the best out of a partner. Pentland has long been a good player, but I think that when he is in the mood, and is ready to fit in with Bloomer, he is made to appear—and; in fact, he is—one of the best outside rights we have. When you see Bloomer you must always think how easy it is to play with such a man as he, and how possible it is to make a deep impression with such a com- panion.• I should not be at all surprised if Pent- land were asked to be England's outside right in all the Internationals this season. "A Son of the Rock." When auld Jock Taylor" appeared at Goodison Park he had a very fine reception. "Good old Jack!" everybody shouted; and if over a footballer deserved to be made a hero it is Taylor. Many, years ago Taylor was one of the shining lights of Dumbarton; it is difficult to believe now that John Bell has given over play- ing—that Taylor is almost the sole surviving "Son of the Rock." Taylor is essentially a man who is only as old as he feels. A more conscien- tious player never lived; and I will go so far as to say that if he can keep up the form he has shOwn in the opening game Everton are certain to have a good season. Taylor in form is one of those players who holds the fortunes of his team in the hollow of his hand; almost everything depends upon him. And because he has been such a credit to professional football I lope he will continue to be able to keep pace with the young ones. En passant let it be recorded that Freeman has given further proof that for practical purposes he is about the best centre- forward Everton have had since the days of Fred Geary, Football in London.</p>
        <p>- - Southerners generally, and those in London especially, are wondering whether Tottenham Hotspur are going to justify their promotion. It</p>
        <p>Tottenham Hotspurs were beaten at Goodison Park by Everton by 4 to 2.</p>
        <p>is too early, of course, to form any idea as to what Tottenham can do; but I think one may say, that the absence of Vivian Woodward will be severely felt. There is a lot of enthusi- asm at Tottenham, and I do not think there need be much fear that they will hold their own. If Tottenham are successful the importance of London football cannot be exaggerated; and if they make headway in their new sphere of life the game will be given such a fillip as to make London the very centre. of Association i football. The possibilities of the metropolis are un- bounded. Assuming that there s no further trouble between the Football Association and the Players,. Chelsea and Tottenham can bring about a wonderful change. Fulham, of course, will make another bold bid for promotion; and It it should be tout tnere aro three kirst .lieague clubs in London, and three clubs all doing well, the difference in football life as it is understood te-day will be astonishing.</p>
