      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Swimming Notes.</head>
        <p>Swimming Notes.</p>
        <p>A well-contested game was witnessed between the Warrington Polo team and Everton on Tuesday evening. Warrington bad to submit to defeat, the score being Everton 4, Warrington 2.</p>
        <p>Combination seems to be lacking in the local team's play. B. Beaman, who played centre half in the initial part of the game, did not show his true form and T. Pemberton took his place afterwards.</p>
        <p>R. Woods, the goal-keeiier, was sorely pressed on Everton scoring their second goal. He rushed out to tackle one of Everton's players, who was about to shoot, but passed to an unmarked player who leisurely put the ball into goal.</p>
        <p>That was a good shot from half-way by Ince when he scored Warrington's first goal. In fact he played splen- didly and saved his team from many perilous situations.</p>
        <p>Warrington were too hasty in their methods or the score would have been reversed. As it was how many times did the ball reach the crossbar?</p>
        <p>Pemberton and Pepper played good games but their efforts were frustrated by other players.</p>
        <p>A word of praise must be allotted to Whitfield o Everton who by splendid play scored their four goals.</p>
        <p>Much interest was centred in the squadron races. Pearson and Knowles met Monks' HaU, and Crosfleld's met Walker's Brewery. Walker's team won easily by threequarters of a length. The race between the two iron works teams was more exciting, Pearson and Knowles only winning by a quarter length.</p>
        <p>MERRY WEAT HER ON</p>
        <p>WATER SUPPLY &amp; FIRE PROTECTION</p>
        <p>County Mansions; Experts sent to all parts to Repoti on Existing Arrangements.</p>
        <p>Write for Pamphlets Merryweather &amp; Sons</p>
        <p>63, LONG ACRE, LONDON, W. C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>A4'NIGHT ON NEW BRIGHTON TOWER.</head>
        <p>A4'NIGHT ON NEW BRIGHTON TOWER.</p>
        <p>The lift attentlaul at New Brighton Tower, upon making his first ascent, on Wednesday morning, was surprised to find a woman and her young child at the summit. They had gone up the night before, but before making his last descent at 9.30 the attendant looked—tOund-and Saw no one. The night was cold, wet, and stormy, and both lhe. woman and the child had suffered considerably.</p>
