      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WARRINGTON REVISION COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON REVISION COURT.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING POINTS RAISED.</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. C Parr and R. C. Parr</p>
        <p>Lose Votes.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H.M.Whartan,revising barrister,attended at the Warrington Town Hall on Wednesday morning to corn- tl!ence the revision of the voters' lists for the borough. ,Aotice had been given of 33 claims and 49 objections. ▪ addition there were twelve lodger claims. Mr. J. ,s2c▪ lowes, jun., appeared on behalf of the Liberals and Mr. n• Holbrook and Mr. Herbert Hayes for the vo,rservatives. The assistant overseers present were :—Warrington, .51r. W H Woodcock; Latchford Without, Mr. Jos. ; Winwick, Mr. E. C. Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>LODGER CLAIMS.</head>
        <p>LODGER CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister intimated that he proposed to -deal with all the lodger claims at once, as he had determined, if necessary, to summon any lodgers as witnesses so that he might know exactly how things ztood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WORD TO THE SUFFRAGISTS.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO THE SUFFRAGISTS.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister said there was another matter e would like to mention. He was told by the assistant "orseer that he refused to enter upon the list the names of two women who wished to be put on division 9oe (Parliamentary).He (the Revising Barrister)supposed lt was the usual attempt on the part of suffragists to create a disturbance. Mr. Woodcock xisely refused to enter them on the list and ue (the Revising Barrister) hoped all the assistant overseers iu the division would refuse to do so. The effect of acting otherwise might be very serious, because they might have a hundred of these Women's names printed on the list at the expense of the county, the whole of which would have to be struck cot, \ asting the time of the Court, and all to satisfy these silly people. Therefore be thought Mr. Wood- °ck had done per fectly right in acting as he had done. -Alr, Holbrook : I have had several applications 'rota lady suffragists and I have informed them that until the law is altered we cannot assist them in lad The Revising Barrister : That's right, lam very g. _ .</p>
        <p>Howes: I have had a number of applications • in one case the lady's claim has been printed and luther this evening or in the morning I believe it is 'let intention to appear before you. It is a division 012 e claim. h The Revising Barrister: She will have a very short eating from me, I promise you. Jr. Howes : I have tried my best to persuade her ?lot to attend, but the claim is printed and the lady rends to Ippear. Both the agents have refused to any illegitimate claims. r. Joseph Smith: Asasslstant overseer for that rrish, I would like to have the opportunity of attend- -2g as I have certain observations to make on her claim "bleb I think would prove fatal. 4_lhe Revising Barrister: I don't think you need ."Ile the slightest trouble, so long as it is an .7PlAication to be put on division one. Any applica- ;lon to be put on the other division, of course I must .15ten to., 11:r. Smith: She is not an inhabitant occupier in the irtrish for which she claims. That is the singular way in ,aich the lady makes out the clam. She does not • w, where she lives. The Barrister : They must not come bothering me, • else they w ill hear something. 01 :he Barrister then proceeded to deal with lodger 113[1s, but no points of exceptional interest were 'TESSRS. J. C. PARR AND R. C. PARR'S VOTES OBJECTED TO. LIVELY DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>- J. Howes jun., had given notice of objection to votes on the Parliamentary list of Mr. Joseph rlton Parr. Grappenhall Hayes, and Mr. Roger arlton Parr, Springfield, Grappenhall, in respect of e and coach-house adjoining the bank premises, "law ick-street. Mr. Holbrook said Mr.J. C. Parr was connected with a ales Bank. Ile was a former Mayor of the borough Arilu a deputy-lieutenant of the county at the present time. Hol I% R• C. Parr was also a deputy-lieutenant. He (Mr. brook) did not know what the objection related to, ant be had a letter from Mr. Parr. Ile Barrister: Are you the objector? Mr. Holbrook : No, sir. • The Banister: • Let us have the objector. Where he? thAlr. Howes : I am the objector and I am waiting for e Persons objected to to appear. ,ThAilr. Holbrook put in a letter from Mr. J. C. Parr ,Ch the Revising Barrister perused. e Barrister (to Mr. Howes): What is the prima evidence of objection. What evidence have you -ginst the name being kept on the list ? ti IMr, Bowes: Fint of all it is necessary for the I:Dant or someone on his behalf to appear. 'be Barrister: No, it is not. la'Alr• Howes: I don't think I in called upon to state -round of objection before the person appears. .-te barrister: I think you are. q;elr. Howes: If it had been an ordinary person had been objected to he would have been brought 'but because he happens to be a man of wealth he -I,,taken this means of getting someone else— ne Barrister: You have given notice of objection. 114°,11 have to appear, and if you are not an overseer vou 'e to give prima facie proof of your objection. What .?..eur prima facie proof ? is ur• Howes : My prima facie proof is that the stable :stri3ut eccupied for purposes of trade, but I do most th °ngiY object to going into this objection without ,appearance ,us Barrister: It is DO use your objecting, because lraold as a matter of law that you must give some ence. 1 cannot listen to you unless you do. Howes: My evidence would be got from the I.IIC n I have objected to. , Barrister• You ought to have summoned him to a, . t:tricre• Howes: I base summoned him by giving him Trile Barrister: That is not enough. • Howes : He must appear to answer the Objection. Barrister : No, he must not. ‘"lr. Howes: Someone on his behalf must appear. .fle Barrister: No. ~ ▪ Howes : Are you quite sure of that? •N'T'le Barrister : Yes, I am quite certain. Howes : That the person objected to has no • to appear in Court ? yO''tle Barrister: 1 don't say that. I say first of all -00 must give some evidence in support of your itztion. .ao'cir• Howes : Have I to give that without the appear- Of the person objected to? 'ae Barrister : You are to give it before I listen to Ilttiling else. • Howes: What evidence do you want? 's Barrister: I am not going to tell you what k'ence to bring. That is your business. Ir, Howes : My evidence is that this person really 18 not occupy. \Pe Parrister : Where is your evidence? Howes We cannot produce evidence on that. .10'oua Barrister: You must, or else 1 cannot listen to • Howes; My evidence would be the man himself. .14:us Barrister : You should have summoned him. I g,°, jag to conduct the matter in strict order. First I want some evidence of objection. Here are ° men on the list. Apparently they have been put. on ,(11)e r 1.7-1 am hound to take that for granted. Before y ''Ke them off the list I want some evidence. When 'tile have given evidence that they are improperly on 151. 1 will call upon the other side. •st Ir• Howes : My ground of objection is that the leables are not used for any business, trade or pro- which is necessary according to law. vie Barrister: I want some evidence. Howes : I will ask the assistant overseer th;:ther he knows of any trade' business or profession is being conducted from that stable. K'r• Woodcock : 1 believe Mr. Parr is a director of s Bank. tfu• r. Howes : Neither of them is a director of Parr's I.le Barrister : Your prima facie evidence is against 111 to, t• Howes : I can give you evidence that thef are i,•,directors of Parr's Bank. ti=ue Barrister: I don't think that would make any at zerence. You might as well give evidence that they liot the Prince of Wales, the Duke of Wellington, or ta:lebody else. It would not make any difference. This t ter has been discussed carefully in the Courts ecentiv ' it i„*ff owes : You must see how utterly impossible Or me to bring any evidence from the persons it "ave objected to. I go there to make ihriries and find the stable open. The clerks at ILhank are using it to store their bicycles in. fit tr Barrister : They may be doing that by consent is a cccupiers. If you find a bicycle in my stable it evidence that the stable is not mine and thatJ am 'aillsing it. Itarl'• Howes : No, but it would be evidence, if it ti Le continually used, that these people have a perfect tto use it. lbe'suri Barrister : Why don't you call a bank clerk fto nowes : Because I was given to understand ap 1)1 Mr. Holbrook that the persons objected to would Holbrook: I told you I was writing them. lisvfe Barrister : Is that all your evidence? You tilled the assistant overseer who proves just what \vett! did not want him to prove. You asked him if they At.d,irectors and he said he thought they were. task' Woodcock : I know they are connected with the 14" t041,,,* dowes : They are not directors ; I think that is '''°ll property. Would you take the kalance sheet</p>
        <p>of Parr's Bank,Ltd.,to prove that they are not directors? The Barrister: I don't think so. At present I hold you have not given prima facie evidence of your objec- tion. Mr. Howes : There is another point I want to raise—that of value. The Barrister: The objection says " You hive not for twelve months occupied as qualifying tenant." You have given me no evidence that they are not tenants. Mr. Howes : Will you adjourn the case till six o'clock this evening, when I will ask someone from the bank to attend. Mr. Holbrook : If this course is allowed, will you allow me to produce their solicitors 2 W.ll you grant their costs ? The Barrister (to Mr. Howes) : I have been through two years' revision, and I have never made an order for costs. If you want an adjournment till six o'clock you shall have it, but if you do not succeed I shall make you pay the costs of the other side. Mr. Howes : Can you make me pay costs just because I do not succeed?</p>
        <p>----- The Barrister : Well, you will see. Mr. Howes: Don't you think it is better to say if you find that my object on is vexatious or frivo- lous and without grounds,yon NV:11 mike me pay? The Barrister : You wait and see. You can put it that way if you like. Mr. Hoes: I am so convinced that this is the solitary Llot on the Warrington Parliamentary register that I intend to go right through with it at any cost. The Barrister : Well, I will adjourn this case till six o'clock. Mr. Howes: As to the letter put in—a most improper proceeding—a private letter from the person objected to to another gentleman. The Barrister : I haven't received that as evidence at all. .</p>
        <p>.I‘lr. Howes: But you have read it. The Barrister: Yes I know 1 have, but I have taken DO notice of it. It has not had the slightest effect on me. There must be no nonsense about it to-night. In another case the Revising Barrister took Mr. Holbrook's testimony on oath as evidence. Mr. Howes asked if the Barrister would take his woi d in 1\ lessrs. Parr's case. The Barrister : I shall not take your word now. Having asked for an adjournment, I am going to give the other side the opportunity of calling witnesses and put an end to it. You said this wAs a blot on the register and I won't have any blots on this register. At a later stage Mr. Howes again contended that be had given proof of objection by putting in the formal notice. The Barrister: Proof of objection does not mean proof that you have objected. Mr. Howes: That is the only proof of objection it is necessary to give. The Barrister: I do not agree. Let it be quite understood that notice of objection is no evidence at all. It is only evidence of the notice of objection and not of the facts stated in it. We should have people sending notices of objection from all parts and running the risk of people turning up, because their business was likely to keep them away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>, ,</p>
        <p>EVENING SITTING.</p>
        <p>The Court again met in the evening when Mr. F. G. Taylor, solicitor (Messrs. Henry Greenell and Co.), appeared on behalf of Mr. Joseph Charlton Parr.He said he understood the ground of objection was that Mr. Parr was not tenant of the premises. He would there- fore put in a certificate from Parr's Bank Ltd., to the effect that Mr. Parr had been for the last ten years and , still is tenant of the premises, and that there is no rent in arrear. The certificate was put in as follows :—This is to certify that Mr. Joseph Cha:lton Parr, of Grappenhall Heyes, has for the past ten years paid to this bank the sum of £lO per annum by way of rent for st,ibles and coach-house situate in the rear of the bank in Winwick- street, Warrington. The tenancy still continues and the rent was last paid in March, 1909, and is not now in arrear. William Skinner, manager. Mr. Howes: Notice the amount of rent paid—£lo per annum. I mention that in showing you what a bogus case this is. First of all Mr. Joseph Charlton Parr is on the register under the qualification " stable (joint)." Evidently we have got so far—that he is not the joint but the sole tenant. Therefore he cannot be tenant according to this description. Mr. Holbrook: Mr. Taylor is appearing for Mr. J. C. Parr and for no-one else. Mr. Howes: We will show you what a juggle the claim is. Mr. Taylor: I must object to that. I claim that Mr. Parr has been a bona fide tenant for the past ten years. The Barrister: I don't take any notice of those s-irt of remarks. Mr. Howes: I claim he cannot be a bona .fide tenant on this description "joint." The value is not sufficient. Furthermore, it has to be shown that the premises are separately occupied for purposes of residence or of trade' business or profession. These are stables that were built in connecticn with Parr's Bank. The Barrister: I thought I adjournei this case for you to bring some evidence. -Mr. Howes: I will go on oath and state that these premi,es are not occupied for purposes of any trade, business or profession. The Barrister, after referring to a recent case, Afdritt v. Matthews, said Mr. Howes had to give him prima facie evidence that the premises were not occupied for purposes of trade, business or profession. What evidence bad Mr. Howes? Mr. Howes produced the balance sheet of Parr's Bank Ltd., containing a list of directors, and this showed that Messrs. J. C. arid R. C. Parr were not directors or local directors of the firm. The Barrister: I think that is sufficient prima facie evidence that Mr. Parr is not a director, and you Fay that the stable is part of the buildings belonging to the bank. Mr. Howes: It, is part of the buildings not occupied for business, trade or professional purposes. The Barrister: I think you have made out sufficient prima facie evidence to be rebutted. (To Mr. T.sylor) Can you show me that this stable is occupied for the purposes of a business. Air. Taylor : Tam afr Id I shall not be able to do ,1 that for the moment, I have not any evidence here. I The Barrister: It is absolutely necessary. Mr. Taylor: I must ask you again to adjourn the case. I was not instructed that this point was to be raised. The ground stated in the notice of objection is that "you have not for twelve months past occupied the qualifying premises as tenant." The Barrister: That is what the statute states, but this case decides distinctly that you must occupy the premises for purposes of business. Mr. Taylor: That is an objection of which I was notlinformed. The Barrister: I want to be perfectly candid. Supposing it turns out that Mr. Parr rents this place and has rented it for years, it won't make any difference supposing he only rents it for the purpose of say, turn- ing his motor car into it when be comes into town. Mr. Taylor: That 3ou would not censider sufficient The Barrister: I say that is not suffic7ent. In the course of further discussion, the Barrister said Mr. Howes had given prima facie evidence that Mr. Parr had no business at Parr's B inlr. If he were a director it would be part of his duty to be there. At the request of Mr. Taylor, the hearing was adjourned until Thursday morning. Mr. Howes said his objection was the same in the case of Mr. Roger C. Parr, and that case was &amp;so adjourned, the Barrister remarking that he was not goine to strike -anybody off the register, if he could help it. Everybody must remain on who was entitled. Mr.Howes : lam quite agreeable to that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>UNSUCCESSFUL CLAIM.</head>
        <p>UNSUCCESSFUL CLAIM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes claimed a vote on behalf of Frederick Thomas Light, 145, Weilfield-street. It was admitted that the claimant did not become tenant of the house until his father's death on December 31st. last, but Mr. How t s held that as his father's successor be was entitled to all the benefits of his predecessor's qualification. Mr. Light said his father did not make a will and he (claimant) WAS not the owner of the house. The vote was refused. "KEEP ON TRYING."</p>
        <p>The Liberals claimed a vote for David Peters, 12, King-street. He stated that he had lived at the house for ten years, and had paid the rent during the whole of that time. The house was in his wife's name when be married her and be did not trouble to have it altered until last year—too late for his applicatiea to succeed at the 1008 Revision Court. Evidence was given to the effe3t that the change of tenancy was only effected on August 21th of last, year The Barrister said he was very sorry, but the tenancy was not yet long enough for claitpant to be entitled to the vote. The change should bare been made not later than July 15th, 1908. Claimant : Perhaps I will get it next time. Mr. Howes: Keep on trying. (Laughter).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>-THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>-THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The proceedings were resumed on Thursday morning. Harry K. Mather applied for a Parliamentary vote in respect of the lock-up shop, 43, Church-street. He said he paid £l3 per annum rent for the premises. Mr. Woodcock said the shop was only rated at £9 and therefore the claimant was on division three (municipal). The Barrister remarked that it was not the payment which entitled a rran to vote. It suns the value of the premises. He refused the claim. 71r. Holbrook : You must get the place assessed higher. (Laughter). The Barrister : Of course, the overseers only require evidence of the value of your property in order to raise theassessnaent. You have just sworn on oath that it is worth more than £lO. Probably they will recon- sider it next year, and raise your assessment, and then you can come here and get a vote. (Laughter).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>MESSRS. PARR STRUCK OFF THE LIST.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. PARR STRUCK OFF THE LIST.</p>
        <p>The objection to Mr. Joseph Charlton Parrs vote in respect of the stable and coach-house attached to the bank premises, was again considered. The Revising Barrister perused the certificate of pay- ment of rent already referred to, and asked " What do you mean by by way, of rent " ? Mr. Taylor : He is the bona-fide tenant. The Revising Barrister: Why on earth do they write a thing like this. Here is a bank which has been in existence for years and the tank manager writes a</p>
        <p>ridiculous thing like. He certifies that "he has for ten years paid to the bank the sum of £lO per annum by way of rent." That sound suspicious to start with. Mr. Taylor: The bank endeavoured to mike it as clear as possible that Mr. Parr was tenant of the premises. In reply to the Barrister, Mr. Taylor said Mr. Parr occupied the premises by verbal agreement. Mr. Howes: You put in the certificate in place of receipts for rent ? Mr. Taylor: At your request. Mr. Howes: That you should produce receipts. , The Barrister : lam not going to take a document like this. Mr. Taylor Ills was done at the request of Mr. Howes who wrote yesterday afternoon asking that we should, if possible, produce receipts for rent (we could not do that because Mr. Parr is in Worcestershire), also document:fry evidence from Parr's Bank, Ltd., as to their receiving same. Mr. Howes: You will notice that Mr. Joseph Charlton Parr pays the rent as tenant. The qualifica- tion on the register, however, is that he is "joint tenant." Mr. Taylor: I am appearing for Mr. J. C. Parr solely.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes: And he is the sole tenant. Mr. William Silcock, chief cashier at Parr's Bank, Warrington, was then called. Mr. laylor : Is it within your personal knowledge that Mr. Parr rents this stable and coach-house from the bank?</p>
        <p>Mr. Silcock : Yes, that is so. And that he pays to the bank a rent of .ClO in respect of it?—Yes.</p>
        <p>And that he does in fact occupy it?—Yes. The Barrister: That is a question for me. Mr. Taylor: And how does he use it? Mr. Silo:y.lc : For his horses and carriage. The Barrister : During the last 18 moaths how often has he used it? _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Sllcock : Frequently. When he is staying at Grappenhall he is very often in town. How long has he resided at Grappenhall during the twelve months preceding July 15th?—! cannot say that from memory. What I want to know is this: What business has he at the bank beyond keeping his account there ?—He has business relations with different people in the town— solicitors and others. _</p>
        <p>I don't want to know anything about the town. Mr. Taylor: May I take it up? The Barrister: No, sir. I would rather you didn't. What I want to know is: What business has he at the bank, if any? If be has not you Must say so. Mr.Silcock : He interviews people on business matters. At the bank ?—A room there is plt.ced at his disposal by the bank. 'ls he a director ?—Not new. _</p>
        <p>Do you keep rooms for the use of the friends of the directors?—lt is an unused room. The Warrington office was formerly the head office and the room in question was the directors' room. It was placed by the bank at the disposal of Mr. Parr. And anybody else?—Nobody else. I suppose that is because be is practically proprietor of the place ?—lt is a limited company. He is a big shareholder?—A shareholder. And he is Mr. Parr. lam taking it that that is really what it means—he is Mr. Parr.—Tbe bank places the room at his disposal and Ile occupies it for his own purposes. Not the Lank purposes ?—No. Further questioned by the Revising Barrister, Mr. Silcock said he should say that Mr. Parr lived at Grappenhall for more than three months in the year. When he was in residence at Grappenball he came to Warrington two or three times a week. Mr. Taylor: He meets people by appointment at the room ?—He uses it as his private room. So far as you know, he US4I3 it generally for purposes of his business ?—Yes. _</p>
        <p>The Barrister: Now that is the %hole point. You are putting into the mouth of the witness what is the whole question lam trying to get at. lam not going to take that down, because it is your es idence and not his. As I understand, Mr. Parr has no business. Is not that so? What is his business ? _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Silcock : He is a County Councillor and a County magistrate. But what is his business ?—A country gentleman. I don't call that a business,do you ?—He is not in any absolute business.</p>
        <p>No, I thought not ?—As an ironmonger, grocer or anything of that kind. (Laughter). Or any other business ?—Not in any other business, but he has business relations. _ _</p>
        <p>So does everybody vsho is out of business. He has relations with his grocer, baker, and candltstick maker —(laughter)—pays their bills and orders things like everybody else.—He is a landed proprietor and has business relations with s)licitors. Of course, a landed proprietor must see a solicitor occasionally to know Nvhother he is to renew documents and, when he has lent money to people on property. whether he wants to call it in.—Still he uses the room for business purposes. In answer to Mr. llowes, Mr. Silcock said Mr. J. C. Parr paid the £lO as rent for the stables and no other payment was made. The amount was duly entered in the books of the company. The £lO covered the use of the room as well as of the stables. Mr. Parr had been a director of the company. Mr. Howes contended that be had clearly established his objection. The qualification was not merely the stables. It was admitted that the £lO covered the use of the room as well. Mr. Taylor said that according to Mr.Howes' argument the position WAS that no-one who was not actually engaged in some labour, shop, business or profession, apart from his own private affairs, could have any right to a vote, unless he was a director of a public company, in connection with premises occupied by the company. In the present case there N'as ma distinct and separate occupation of the stables and if Mr. Howes was right, he was going to exclude a whole class of people from a vote of the same character as the one under consideration. Ile (Arr. Taylor) submitted that Mr. Parr did carry on business matters in connection with his occupation of the premises. Whether it might be his oan private business or not was a point which his Honour would no doubt consider carefully. Here they hal a bonafide tenancy of the stables and they conducted it in relation to their own business affairs. That being so, be sub- mitted they were entitled to the vote. The Revising Barrister, after perusing the case of Aldritt v. Matthews which Mr. Howes brought to his notice, said it seemed to him th it there were two qualifications—the burgess qualification which meant occupation as owner or tenant for business purposes of a house, tenement or place of the value of £lO and upwards, and the ordinary inhabited house qualification with which they had nothing to do in Ibis case. Until this case quoted by Mr. Howes was decided, people had been concluding that it did not matter whether they had any business or not—all they had to do was to occupy some tenement,and so long as it was £loa year that was all that was necessary.lf that had been correct,a man might have created a vote if he had thought it was worth while paying £lO a year, by simply taking a room which could be said to he worth £lO a year, paying that sum, and never going near it. The point that Mr. Taylor had raised was raised by Mr. ArteEuus Jones in the case cited, and argued very persistently, but the judges would not have it. They came to the conclusion that ' this qualification necessitated that a man must have a business or profession, and be must use the room which was his qualifying property, for the purpose of that business or profession. It seemed abundantly clear, and he found as a fact that Mr. Parr had no trade, business or profession for which be desired to use these ' premises. He might be a barrister—perhaps was. There were many barristers who were barristers in name only and it am not worth their while to practice because they had too much money. (Laughter.) It was very fortunate it was so. But there MR no evidence of ' that in the present case, or Mr. Parr might have used the rooms for the purposes of chambers, which would have been perfectly right. Ile had a business once which be (the Revising Barrister) should have held to be a busi- ness,and that was the director of a bank.He should have called the directorship of El bank a business or profession and if he had been using the stables then for the purposes of the bank or whilst he was attending to the bank's business,he should have said Mr. Parr was occupy- ing them as tenant for the purpose of the bank's busi- ness. But he had ceased to do that, and for the simple reason that he was Mr. Parr he was allowed to use a room for nothing in the bank, where he met his friends and, Mr. Taylor wanted to insist, his enemies too—if he ever had any—(laughter)—at any rate, anyone he chose to meet. He .was also to use the stables to put up his carriage and pair when he came into town, instead of putting them up at the Patten Arms or any similar stablishment. That did not give him a qualification if he (the Ba-rister) was to rely upon the case quoted, and he thought he was bound by it. He found as a fact that Mr. Parr had no business in connection with the stables, that he did not use them for the purposes of trade, profession or business, and that therefore he had no qualification. The objection was sustained and he must come off the list. There were other points which might have been taken but it was not necessary for him to deal with them. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes : There is the case of Mr. Roger C. Parr, on siruilar lines. Are you going to defend that? The Barrister: You proved your notice of objection in that case. Is he represented ?</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor: No, ido not appear for him. The Barrister: Then they both come off. I am always disposed to keep names on the register, but in this MEC I cannot.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC-HOUSE TENANCY.-INTERESTING POINT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes had entered objections to the votes of Messrs. Herbert Thomas Bolton, John Bolton, and Samuel Bolton, in connection with the tenancy of the Moulders' Arms public-house. Alderman Bolton appeared for the voters referred to, who rare his brothers. The objection in each case was "that you have cot for the twelve months preceding July 15th occupied the qualifying premises as tt nant."</p>
        <p>111 r. Howes pointed out that up to February Ist of this sear Herbert Thomas Bolton was the licencee of the how, but on that date the licence was transferred to George Billington who at present held it. Mr. Howes' contention was that in the appeal case of Kent v. Fittall it was distinctly stated Leyond all dispute that the licencee could not surrender control of any</p>
        <p>portion of licensed premises and that therefore the brothers Bolton were-not the tenants in this case. The real tenant was the licencee. Although Billington did not live on the public-hause premises, he must be the tenant. • The Revising Barrister was unable to find an orficial report of the case quated by Mr. Howes. He added: " You bring some wretched book published by a political party and they put in just what suits them- selves and nothing that suits the other side." Mr. Howes : object most emphatically to that statement. The Barrister: I have been carefully examining the books on both sides, and they are both alike. (Laughter.) There is a tendency to just put in exactly, what is wanted for one side. I want some authorita- tive report. You shall have every assistance from me so lone as you give me an authority, but I am not going to take the authority of some political association. I want the authority of a judge. Mr. Howes : 1 did not give the authority of any political association whatever. The Barrister: It conies to this. We have looked for this ruling and cannot find it. I have read Kent v. Fittall, all three cases, and I do not remember any such statement being made. The Deputy Town Clerk (having looked at the book from which Mr. Howes quoted) : It is supposed to have been said by the Lord Chief Justice. The Barrister : The mere fact th it a man is licensed by the maebistrates does not affect the actual facts. I know that every day dummy agreements are put before the mae•'!strates for the purpose of getting licences granted, and all kinds of tricks are played on them. I don't know that they do not deserve it, but still it is done. Mr. Howes: We don't suggest this a trick. The Barrister: It does not affect me. I want to know what is the actual contract between the tenant and the landlord. I don't care two-pence about the ma gistrates or the licence. Mr. Howes : My contention is that the licencee must be the tenant. I admit that Mr. Bolton may call 13illington the manager. Alderman Bolton : Oh, lie is the manager. Mr. Howes: My point is that the licencee is the man solely responsible for the conduct of the house. Alderman Bolton : Still he is not the tenant. Mr. Howes: In law he is the tenant because he cannot surrender control. The brothers Bolton live there, but I contend they are lodgers. Alderman Bolton : They have lived in the premises I fifty odd years. Samuel is the manager of the brewery. Herbert was ill and not in a fit condition to be answer- ' able to the magistrates' in case of emergency. Billing. ton was the manager and I told him the man who managed the business should be the licencee and responsible to the magistrates for the control of the house. But all three of them are tenants._ _ . _</p>
        <p>Giving evidence on oath, Alderman Bolton said he was the owner of the house and his brothers paid him sums of money amounting to £6.1 6s. per year. There was, however, no real contr. let. He put in an agree- ment to show that Billington was nothing but a manager. He had no control over the money or the premises. He was paid a weekly wage with a mouth's notice to terminate his services. The arrangement with the brothers had been in force nearly thirty years. The Barrister held that the question of the licence had absolutely nothing to do with anybody except the man who held the licence and the mag:strates It was the real state of things between the man and the owner with which he (the Barrister) had to deal. If this had leen a beer house, then undoubtedly there would have been a difficulty because the real resident occupier must be the licence holder, but that did not apply in the case of a full licence, Therefore he saw no reason why these people should not be joint tenants, There was something more than mere tenancy on sufferance in this case; they were annual tenants. He had no doubt in the world about this case; it was perfectly right and the votes would be allowed.</p>
        <p>_ Alderman Bolton asked for expenses on the ground that the objection was frivolous, but the Revising Barrister refused to grant them bezause Alderman Bolton " had nothing in the world to do with the case." Alderman Bolton : I hive had a lot to do with it this morning. (Laughter.) BIRCEI FOR SUFFRAGETTES.</p>
        <p>During the morning the case of Miss Annie Gomall, Latchford Without, who had put in a claim for a Parliamentary vote, was mentioned. Cleitnant did not put in an appearance. Mr. Smith (assistant overseer) pointed out that even if the claim were allowable by law, it was bad. Mr. Howes : Absolutely. Mr. Smith said the qualification wis incorrectly described. The lady wanted another qqalification. Mr. Howes remarked that these ladies appeared to want the earth. (Laughter.) The Barrister : You don't know what they wan'... We want an Act of Parliament to birch them. (Laughter). The claim was struck out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TEA AND CONCERT AT LEGH-STREET.</head>
        <p>TEA AND CONCERT AT LEGH-STREET.</p>
        <p>The quarterly tea and concert in connection with Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church was held on Thursday evening in the schoolroom adjoining the church, and was provided on this occasion by the married men, assisted by several friends. A goodly number sat down to tea, after which the concert was proceeded with which proved to be a very enjoyable one and was presided over by Mr. J. T. Rowlands, who in the course of his remarks made some "impres- sions " on his recent visit to America. Mr. J. Willett, who was in fine voice, gave capital interpreta- tions of the songs "Sailor's Grave," "Roses," and " Mary." Mr. G. Gough was very successful in his rendering of " Lighterman Tom " and " To-morrow will be Friday." Mr. Cox rendered the recitations : " In a signal box " and " Christmas Day in the Work- house," very feelingly. Mr. .T. Shaw's recitations " Summin things up " and " Advice to husbands " created outbursts of laughter. Messrs. Phillips and Spruce sang the duets " Flow gently Deva " and " Two proper men," the latter of which caused some amusement, Mr. S. Dodd delighted the an Bence with his comic song " The chilly man." An amusing dialogue was given entitled " The five clever men of Ruetown," in which the five men Mr. Chippis a carpenter, Mr. Boxom a shoemaker Mr. Flynn an Irish Tailor, Mr. Bloombell a Lanca- shire Lad, and Mr. Snatchem a Detective, as an experiment in domestic economy followed the advice to send their wives away for a month and manage the household themselves. The doleful experiences related by each at the month's end left no room for doubt that if ey had made a sad mistake. They there and then unanimously decided to humbly beseech their wives to come back as quickly as possible. The farce closed with a chorus in praise of " A jolly little wife with a happy smiling face." The friends who took part were, Messrs. Parkes, Phillips, Dodd, Bailey, Lander and Shaw,—A feature of the evening was that each singer and reciter had to be a married man. Mr. Joseph U. Foster acted as accompanist with his usual ability. A vote of th3nks was proposed by Rev. J. B. Buglass seconded by Mr. J. Griffiths and supported by Mr. T. Elwell.. The meeting was brought to a close by singing the doxoloc.T.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CRICKET MATCH.</head>
        <p>CRICKET MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKHOUSE V. PA DC LTE COTTAGE Homus.—A match between the staffs of the Workhouse and Cottage Homes, assisted by the Guardians and friendr, was played on Thursday at the Workhouse and resulted in a victory for the Cottage Homes by 12 runs. Scores :</p>
