      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>LETTER OF THANKS FROM RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Baker, 9, Ellesmere-street, Runcorn, writing on the 25th August, 1909, says :—" I have been using Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa for four years, morning and night and sometimes at meal times throughout the day. I find Vi-Cocoa far more sustaining and power giving than any beverage or food I have taken. I can safely say I am better in bodily health than I was four years ago, thanks to Vi-Cocoa for it. I can also recommend Vi-Cocoa to all working men as a food and beverage that will build up a strong and healthy constitution and fit a man for any amount of hard work." Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa contains all the elements that enrich the blood and restore the enormous nervous waste so common among all men and women. It is a perfect food. Do not ark your Grocer for Cocoa. Ark for a6d packet of Vi-Cocoa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LORD ADVOCATE'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>LORD ADVOCATE'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>Budget Demonstration on Monday Next</p>
        <p>Mr. Alexander Ure, K.C., M.P., the Lord Advocate, will visit Warrington on Monday next to speak at a demonstration in support of the Budget in the Parr Hall. The great reputation of Mr. Ure as one of the most eminent and convincing exponents of land reform should be sufficient to attract a crowd gathering. The other speakers will include Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. The chair will be taken by Mr. B. Wynne-Jones, president of the Progressive Labour League under whose.imspices the meeting is to be held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>MR. URE AT LEYTON.</head>
        <p>MR. URE AT LEYTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ure, addressed two large meetings in support of the Budget, at Leyton, on Wednesday night, one inside the Town Hall, and the other, an ovealow meeting, outside the building. In the course of a speech lasting close upon an hour and a half, Mr. Ure claimed that the Budget carried out the principles laid down by Adam Smith that be should never take more money out of the ratepayer's pocket than we were going to put in the Treasury, and that we should tax all men in the community on their ability to pay. The land taxes, he said, were capable of very wide application and wide extension. They might ''n•et a very small sum this year, but it would be a larger sum every year,and in the end they would get millions a year from the land taxes. A resolution supporting the Budget was carried almost unanimously, amid great enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MR. R. L. OUTHWAITE.</head>
        <p>MR. R. L. OUTHWAITE.</p>
        <p>Budget Campaign in the Knutsford</p>
        <p>Division.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. L. Outhwaite, who has been addressing very successful meetings during this week in the Knutsford Division, visited Higher Whitley on Wednesday even- ing and spoke to a fairly large gathering of villagers. He said he had come there to speak to them about that great Finance Bill, which was causing such an extra- ordinary amount of controversy in the country. He did not suppose that in the history of this country there had ever been such a budget. It was necessary to increase taxation because the Government a few months ago established a very great scheme of old age pensions and the bulk of the fresh revenue had got to finance the old age pension scheme. It was going to do more than that, because a great number of old people who were now debarred from having their pensions owing to their having recourse to poor relief were going to have their pensions in the near future. Therefore 200,000 more people would before long receive their pensions. The Budget was making provision for the extension of the system he had spoken of. They could claim the support of the Tory party in so far as they promised to give old age pensions at the time of the elections. (Laughter.) When the Tories came into office they forgot to provide for the old age pensions. (Laughter.) The Tories dared not as a party come out and say that they wished to abolish that scheme. On that question the country was decidedly with the Liberals. The Budget also had large and popular support, because it was necessary to increase the revenue of the country to maintain the British Navy absolutely supreme upon the seas. (Applause.) It was required to spend something like three millions more a year upon the Navy. Now, what- ever Liberals might think as to the necessity for the increase of armaments, they could claim the support of the Tory party for that proposal, because a little while ago they were hoe-ling for eight "Dreadnoughts." Now that we were going to have eight "Dread- nought" they were howling about paying for the a. We were all agresd that it was wise to raise revenue for those purposes, but the great question was how it was to be raised. In the first place the Budget proposed an alteratien of the income tax system, and a super-tax on big incomes. When a man had a huge income and at the same time boasted that he never worked, as the poor peers and dukes did, it showed that they were having the wealth that the community had created. The Budget was taxing the appropriations of monopo- -I:sts. There would be reasonable restraint from the income tax duties if the community had not created it. There was also an increase in the death duties, which would lead to great estates paying more in the future. He did not think we were going to trouble over the tribulations of those who received a million or two from their ancestors. The Duke of Marlborough addressing a Tory meeting a little time ago, said that. the Budget was such a terrible one that life would be so miserable that he was thinking of throwing himself out of the window. It was not for us to prevent him. But the Duke went on to say that he had decided not to do so because when he came to think of it he found that his unfortunate heir would have to pay 10 per. cent, under those duties. The Duke of Marlborough only contemplated leaving £1,000,00D. Tbe next tax was one upon licences. A licence to sell drink was a privilege panted to an individual, which the State did not grant to all individuals, and because the State granted it to certain persons and not to all,it became of enormous value. He proceeded to illustrate this point. Say a house was worth £5,000 and the State granted a licence for the sale of drink, from the moment that the house had that licence it might be worth £15,000. That increase in value of £lO,OOO was not due to anything that the owner of the premises had done, but was due to the action of the State in giving the occupier of those premises the right to do something which the State denied to every citizen. When the State taxed them it was only taking tack what it had created. Those taxes were direct taxes and they were taxing monopoly. He referred to the tax of Bd. a pound on tobacco. He held th it indirect taxes were unfair and unjust. That was why he was a Free Trader and not a Tariff Reformer. (Applause.) The great advantage of the Budget was that it taxed directly. Indirect taxes would in time be abolished. The Liberals had reduced the tax on sugar by one half arid they were going to get rid of all the tax. They bad reduced the tea duty and they wanted to get rid of that. When the revenue from the Budget came in next year the Chan- cellor of the Exchequer they hoped would have such a surplus that they would be able to further reduce indirect taxes. The Tariff Reformer pointed to the tobacco tax as being unfair. They said it was very hard to tax the poor man's luxury. How splendid was the attitude of the working classes! How splendid was the attitude combined with that of the rich plutocrats and the rich monopolists of the country. The workers had shouldered their share of the burden and we were going to see that the shirkers shouldered their burden also. It showed, at any rate, that the workers were more patriotic than the dukes and the lords. The lords and the dukes were always howling about patriotism, the Navy and the Empire, but when it came to paying for them it was another thing. We would find that the Tory Party were not opposing the taxes which were going to bring in the most revenue, but those taxes which were going to bring in the least revenue in the near future. That was surprising on the face of it. The land taxes which were going to bring in so much in the near future were not only revenue producing taxes, but they wore going to make a start with the breaking up of the land monopoly system which at Present existed in this country. They were going to make a start in forcing the land out of the hands of the few great monopolists into the hands of the people who required land to earn their livelihood. Continuing he said there was going to be an increment tax on urban land. When it was found in the future that urban land changed hands and the value was increased then the State would step in and take a fifth of the increased value. That tax might in the future bring in a very considerable amount of revenue.. That was not the important part of that tax. It necessitated the valuing of every acre of land in the Kingdom, and it was the valuation that the Tories were opposing. For this reason—that when the people saw what was the value of the land in the Kingdom, when they saw how gigantic the value was, they would see how great was the "Treasure House," as the late Sir Henry Ca.nipell- Bannerman called .it, and the people would demand, when the Government wanted any money for the social well-being, that they went to the "Treasure house" and not to the poor by way of taxing their necessities. (Applause). Mr. Outhwaite addressed well attended meetings at Daresbury on Thursday ; and Stockton Heath last night. He is to speak at Grappenhall to-night (Saturday).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TREASURE TROVE.</head>
        <p>TREASURE TROVE.</p>
        <p>The recent discovery near Yeovil of an ancient British gold torque and the consequent enquiry by the coroner and his jury as to the manner and nature of the find, are of some interest to landowners as well as to antiquarians. Treasure trove, according to an old definition, is any gold or silver in coin, plate or bullion found concealed in a house or in the earth or other place, the owner thereof being unknown. The essence of the thing is concealment, the article may have been deliberately hidden or buried by the owner; if it has only been abandoned,.thrown away, or lost, it is n)t treasure trove, and b-e4ongs to the finder as against everyone but the true owner when he turns up and claims it. If the article is treasure trove, on the other hand, it belongs either to the Crown or to the person, generally to the lord of the manor,to whom the franchise of treasure trove may have been granted by the Crown. It is the duty of any person who finds an article that may come under the designation of treasure trove to inform the coroner of the district, who thereupon calls together a jury to enquire what the find consists of, who was the finder, and who was the original owner, so far as any information on the last point is available. Such enquiry_ has, however, no jurisdiction to determine any question of title to the find, nor to decide whether , the article is treasure trove or not; these points must be settled, if there is any dispute, by the decision of the King's Bench Division ; but where gold or silver articles whose owner is unknown are found concealed or busied in one place, all lying together, there is a presumption that they were intentionally hidden for the benefit of the depositor, and that they are, there- fore, treasure trove.—" Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>NIGHT IN A DOSS HOUSE.</head>
        <p>A NIGHT IN A DOSS HOUSE.</p>
        <p>SOME OF WARRINGTON'S SUBMERGED.</p>
        <p>There is an old proverb which states that poverty makes strange bedfellows and the truth of this saying I proved on Monday last when I spent a night in one of the many</p>
        <p>common lodging-houses in Warrington. had walked during the day from Manchester and on the road had twice been wet through. Even when it was not actually raining, the cold wind pierced my threadbare garments. _ _</p>
        <p>When I arrived in Vv-Tarrington, therefore, I was in desperate case and only too glad of the warmth of the" doss" house. During</p>
        <p>my tramp from London I have spent many nights in such places, and have frequently come in contact with strange characters. Nevertheless, in the matter of such queer fish Warrington certainly takes the pro- verbial biscuit. In the "kip " house where I stayed there was, of course, the usual assortment of tramps, hawkers, " griddlers " or street singers, and professional beggars. In these I felt no interest as they belong to types _ commonly _met with in every such</p>
        <p>"workman's hotel." But there were three men whose appearance spoke eloquently of a long-lost respectability,and after I had eaten a cheap but plentiful meal I made _my way towards the corner where they were sitting. One was an ex-solicitor ; the other two were actors.</p>
        <p>The lawyer still retained in his clothing some hint of what he had been in the past. There was the battered silk hat, the closely buttoned frock coat—now gradu- ally turning green—the frayed grey trousers and a pair of patent leather boots, the soles of which were departing in pieces. He was the son of a clergyman who had held a small living. His father had educated him up to the age of 18, when he was articled to a firm of London solicitors. The young man worked hard, passed his examinations with flying colours, and returned to his native town to take up a partnership which his father had purchased for him. He was a brilliant success, and won the confidence of everyone. Then the senior partner died, and Mr. X., still a young man, practically monopolized all the legal business of the town. He became a Justice of the Peace, Mayor of the Borough, and was trusted implicitly by everyone. Especially was he trusted by ladies who had money to invest, and very lucky they considered themselves if he would consent to act on their behalf. Then, one day, the Mayor was missing. No one knew where he had gone, and his many clients became alarmed. Enquiries were made, and it was found that he had taken boat for the Argentine Republic. He was brought back and had to stand in the dock before his late colleagues charged with con- verting to his own use .£25,000 of his clients' money. He had been keeping up two estab- lishments—one in his native town, the other in London. The latter was ruled over by a lady whose tastes, like those of the Second Mrs. Tanqueray, were "expensive," and she had a preference for champagne suppers, diamonds, and a box at the opera. The result was five years penal servitude, and Mr. X., left the prison a broken old man. Nevertheless, he still makes a living by law-work. For half-a-crown he will give you as good advice as, in former days, would have cost you 6s. Bd.,and though he may no longer appear in court as a solicitor the law cannot prevent him sitting in court by the side of his client and giving him advice. He makes, I was told, about 30s. a week. For that sum he could live in comfort but there is no need to ask why he frequents the common lodging-house. The bleary eye, the red nose, the flushed, puffy face, all tell their own story, and even in the early hours of the morning he carries wher- ever he goes the suggestion of stale spirits. The two actors were not less interesting characters and they were both to be sincerely pitied. One, whom I will call Mr. A, was an elderly man with the typical actor's face. At the age ot 2 years he played " Little Willie "in East Lynne ; later he became " Little Lord Fauntleroy," after which he was entrusted with older parts. Then, being ambitious, he joined a well-known Shakes- pearean stock company and was fortunate enough to attract the attention of the late Sir Henry Irving who made the young actor an offer which literally took his breath away. For nearly ten years he was with the great actor, and then, one night, the fire curtain gave way and injured his left shoulder. Amputation of the arm followed, and partial paralysis of the left leg. As far as the stage was concerned his career was at an end, and he now lives on the 10s. a week allowed him by the Actors' Bonevolent Fund. The case of the other actor—a much younger man— was equally sad, and was told me by the older man. The young man's speciality was playing the heroes' parts in the sensational kind of melodrama with which we are all familiar. In one of these plays, a thrilling gght took place between the hero and the villain,the weapons used being long stilettoes. One night the scene was more realistic than was intended, and the hero gave a scream of. agony, falling to the ground. He had been accidentally stabbed in the throat. Some Idisease of the throat and tongue set in,and the doctors removed the latter. He is now dumb, and lives, too, on a small weekly allowance from the Actors' Benevolent Fund. Yet he looks cheerful, if not happy. And so now he plays the hero's part in real life.</p>
        <p>S. G. L.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SIR JOHN BRUNNER'S HOUSE</head>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER'S HOUSE</p>
        <p>ROBBED.</p>
        <p>Silverlands, the residence of Sir John Branner, M.P. at Chertsey, Surrey, was visited during the early hours of Tuesday morning by burglars, who succeeded in getting away with a quintity of silver presentation keys and trowels.</p>
        <p>_ Although the value of the stolen goods does not amount to a very large sum, yet they were particularly prized by Sir John, being mementoes of many pleasant stone-laying and other functions at which he had per- formed the ceremony in the Northwich division which he represents in Parliament. The articles %sere kept in cabinets, which had Lem smashed by the intruders. Information was at once given to the Chertsey police on the burg'ary being discovered. Investigation showel that an entrance ban been forced by the breaking of a pane of glass in a ground floor window,and the pushing back of the catch.</p>
        <p>There was also evidence that the work was that of more than one man. Four or five rooms had been ran- sacked by the men, who were apparently disturbed. The light in one of the rooms was turned on about two o'clock in the morning, and it is believed that the burglary took place about that time. A thorough search of the premises resulted in an old bicycle being found in the grounds. It is possible that it may be useful as a clue, but the police are nA very hopeful on account of its condition. Sir John Brunner trlvelled down to Chertsey on Tuesday on receiving a message from Lady Brunner. Neither her ladyship nor any of the servants who were sleeping in the house heard anything during the night</p>
        <p>HOME NI"RsING.—A little book has just been issued on the subject of " Home Nursing" by the Proprietors of one of the well-known Specialities. While the main object of the book is to advertise this Bpeciality, it contains much valuable information regarding the sick room, ventilation, and the general treatment of the sick, also first aids to the injured, antidotes for poisoning, hints on baby troubles and the general care of infants. This book will be sent to anyone who will enclose a penny stamp to The W. H. Comstock Co., Ltd., 21, Farricgdon-avenue, London, E.C.</p>
