      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>iIIT</head>
        <p>iIIT</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER UT!!, 109.</p>
        <p>When August ended, THE SLIPPERY the North Pole, for all POLE. the world knew to the contrary, was as far off as ever it had been. Expeditions were some- where amid the Arctic ice trying to reach it; expeditions had been up there .any time this hundred years past. Peary, the greatest liv- ing explorer, had been away a year or more; but Peary's voyages had been so numerous that nobody was thinking of him. Since Elizabeth's days the approach to the Pole had been painfully gradual--a few miles at a time. There were a good 200 miles left to wrench from the exclusive grasp of King Frost, and had any of us been asked what we thought about the Pole we should probably have remembered that the old stalwart (one regards the Pole as an almost human thing) was good for at least half a century yet, and then changed the subject in favour of the Budget or Aeroplanes. That was where things stood at the end of August. Before one week of September had fully gone, two separate explorers were claiming to have reached the "top of the world" by separate routes, with varying methods, and at times with a year's interval between them! It is not for us to trace the history of the arrival and reception of the news; but all was intensely dramatic. First came the little message from Lerwick to the effect that Dr. COOK, who had been gone so long that prac- tically everyone thought him dead, had reached what the Esquimaux called the Big Nail. Then we saw his "New York Herald" story, with its thrilling account of his long struggle with cold, hunger and solitude, unaccompanied save by two faithful Esqui maux. He went to Copenhagen and con- vinced Danes and journalists he was honest and truthful. But Europe and America were torn with Cookite and A nti-Cookite dissen- sions. Some thought Dr. COOK a hoaxer,some thought him suffering from hallucinations, many advanced the proposition that it was utterly impossible for anybody to get to the Pole by any means. Then, into the midst of it all, on t he evening of September 60, came the little cablegram that put a summary end to at lea st half the disputes. Whether Dr. COOK, a man of undoubted bravery and experience, had got to the Pole or not was a matter of comparative unimportance. For somebody had got there, and that somebody was an American. The effect upon the world was a mixed one; first it breathed again— relieved of its doubts—then it sighed. For now that man the insatiable has at last devoured the miles between civilisation and the Pole, what is the result of it all? To be frank— precious little, as far as we can see. Dr. COOK possibly and Commander PEARY probably will be able to tell and show us something about the geological formation, the fauna and flora (?) of the regions around the Pole. Even if they could give us the most complete accoun ts, the positive good derivable from it would be doubtful. In face of the difficulties of access to the Pole, no natural deposits there, short of a radium mine, would be worth working. And if a radium mine were found, we should be face to face with one of those clashings of pecuniary interests which cause so much bitterness be tween nations. Even now there is quite a co ntroversy, albeit a good-tempered one, as to whether the Pole belongs to the U.S.A. or Canada. Material good,then,is not derivable from these exploits. Spiritual good we may perhaps extract from the spectacle) of man "facing fearful odds," walking deliberately into the jaws of death for an ideal of sorts. But, really, we can see spectacles similar in kind, if not in degree, in many fields of sport—high-speed motor racing and aviation,for exam ple. We are coming more and more to the opinion that the only effect of these expeditions is that one more of those boundaries of the unknown which completely hedged in primeval man, and have gradually been diminishing before his descendants, has been lost to us. Never more shall we be able to think of the frozen North as of a mar- vellous place of inystery, fascinating us and threatening us, inspiring us with gorgeous dreams. Soon the last spot of untrodden ground will have been charted, and the romantic soul of man will have for only field and outlet the stars and illimitable spaces of heaven. Those, thank goodness, seem likely to retain their virtue. It will take a good many Cooks to get to Orion or Ursa Major. Even the Peary who conquered the Pole is not likely to scale to the Pole Star.</p>
        <p>Of persons who died THE last year only one in UNPOPULARITY each 900 was cremated. OF CREMATION. During the last half- century so many dis- tinguished people have ordered for their bodies that form of disposal that on that account alone the claims of the practice ought to have more general recognition. Of course, cremation is no new thing, and the Romans were wise enough to make it among themselves a common custom ; but chiefly on account of a definite religious doctrine which it is not necessary here to discuss, it was abandoned, and for centuries prac- tically forgotten. Only when scientists repudiated that doctrine in its literal sense was it possible to re-introduce the custom to civilisation. Its advantages have been well advocated; so that there seems to remain no sound argument against its universal adoption; yet from the small number of cremations and from personal irkquiry it is evident that there is a common feeling of dis- taste for it. Indeed the subject is usually dismissed from conversation because it is thought to be gruesome. It should not be difficult to prove that a large field, hiding under its hideous surface decaying human bodies, and surrounded by streets of inhabited houses. is infinitely more gruesome and objectionable. So strong is custom and the senti nent that thrives upon it that no mere arguments will affect it directly. Yet arguments appeal strongly enough to people of individual thought, and they can at least by example exert some influence. Let them bear in mind that cemeteries, whether " beautified " or plain, are an exceedingly gloomy, and there- fore undesirable, form of open space, as well as' sources necessarily of ill-health. Let</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>them realise that cretnation, though the only satisfactory method of disposal of the dead, is necessarily more expensive than interment now, chiefly because it is so infrequently sought; and let them see at once that their wills contain a clause ordering it for their dead bodies. So, as its evident advantages are realised, the practice may become common, and in time universal.</p>
        <p>Votes are all one size A REvrsioN COURT and weight fortunately, SENSATION, but our Conservative friends will doubtless feel that they have sustained a heavy loss this week by the removal from the voters' lists of the names of Messrs. J. Charlton Parr and his son, Mr. Roger Parr. These gentle- men are Tories of large size politically, and it must be more than a little galling for them to be locally disfranchised by the action of the Liberals. Mr. J. Howes, jun., questioned the validity of Messrs. Parr's qualification—the £lO tenancy of a stable at Parr's Bank—and after a lengthy and keenly-argued discussion between the Revising Barrister, Mr. Taylor, solicitor for Messrs. Parr, and himself, the Liberal agent cal-fled his point. Mr. Howes displayed an extensive knowledge of the law and practice in regard to a rather knotty point, and the result is a feather in his cap. It may now be taken as law that the mere tenancy of a room or building for £lO per year does not buy a vote. The qualification necessitates the use of the premises for the purposes of trade, profession, or business—all the difference in the world between a manu- factured and a genuine title to vote.</p>
        <p>It is not creditable to a THE CLAIMS OF town like Warrington, THE which has some claim MUSICAL SOCIETY. Ao be considered musical, that th e excellent work of the Musical Society should receive such scant support. The fact that on last season's concerts there was a loss of £BO 15s. 10d. indicates that the townspeople do not realize their obligations to a Society which has played an important part in the education of musical taste in our midst. The concerts are not nearly so well attended as they ought to be and the number of subscribers—which works out at one per thousand of the population—is what the treasurer termed it—" a positive disgrace." The expenses of a society which seeks to present the best works in an adequate manner, must be considerable and it behoves lovers of music to see that the high standard of the concerts is not jeopardised by financial difficulties.. There is a most attractive pro- gramme for the coming season, and the probable visit of Sir Charles Santley, the eminent tenor, on November 17th will be looked forward to with keen interest as a very notable event in local musical life.</p>
        <p>The Bridges Corn- WARRINGTON mittee and the Town BRIDGE. Council have spent a few hours this week on preliminaries of the Warrington Bridge scheme. As a result we are inclined to think some little progress has been made, but it would not do to be over-confident. The -Council has advanced even further on past occasions, and retraced its steps, so that it is not unreasonable to imagine they may do so again. In fact the decision of Tuesday evening was reversed by the special Council meeting of Thursday evening. The point at issue—upon which two such notable antagonists as Councillor Roberts and Coun- cillor Pemberton split—was as to whether the Corporation should engage an eminent consulting engineer to draw out the specifica- tions and design and superintend the work throughout, or whether the Borough, Surveyor should take these initiatory steps, advertise for tenders, and leave the .way clear for expert advice at a later date. According to Councillor Pember- ton there is a difference of about £l,OOO in the cost of the two methods. The Council did not seem to fully grasp the situation,and the erroneous idea that no engineer was con- templated by the economists resulted in the minutes of the Bridges Committed instructing the Borough Surveyor to proceed, being referred back. On Thursday the members had the specifications and report of the Borough Surveyor before them, aud they were approved of. Tenders are therefore invited for a new bridge 134 feet long and 70 or 80 feet wide, from firms experienced in ferro-concrete construction. It seems to us that this course of action—if adhered to— will secure for the Council the very best brains in the country at no special cost. When the Council have the designs and estimates before them,then will be the time to call in expert advice. We sincerely hope that-progress may now be reported.</p>
        <p>There is a transition THE LICENCE stage which has to be DUTIES. passed before licence duties can be equitably • assessed. The .equitable basis is not the rateablgivalue, since that falls on the build- ings which for one reason or another have been erected, while the Government rightly desire to tax the monopoly value. The Opposition set themselves rigorously against the proposal to establish a register which would give a satisfactory basis for this assessment on the compensation value. Hostility to valuation seems to be an obses- sion of the Conservative mind. But the plain man cannot understand why the compensa- tion value is not also the equitable value for taxation purposes. The Government has given every proof -'of its desire for fairness. Is it, for example, the belief of average common sense that the higher the value of the premises the lower should be the duty paid? Yet that was the existing state of things, and to remedy this was one of the principles observed by the Government in levying new taxation. Hotels and restaurants have in the past been practi- cally forced to accept assessment on public- house lines, which naturally pressed on them unfairly. The Government gave them relief if their receipts for liquor were •not more than one-third of their total receipts. As it appears that some hotels, owing to seasonal conditions, would be Prevented from receiving this relief—for instance, sea-side hotels with an influx of visitors during the summer— this special 'case has been met by a special concession.</p>
        <p>"What will the Lords How THE LORDS do?" That was a ques- WILL ACT. tion which Liberals some time ago were asking with strong curiosity. Now it is the Opposition organs which are on the tip-toe of expectation. 1 he attitude of Liberals is changed. Lord Crewe and Mr. Birrell, both speaking on Saturday, put the same thought into words, in the one case of courtly irony—irony in the literal sense of the word,. that is, humorous under- statement and in the other of picturesque and racy effect. The House of Lords, Lord Crewe said, contained a number of men of great experience who would do whatever they did with full deliberation and a full sense of the tremendous responsibility which would weigh upon them. Mr. Birrell meant the same thing when he said, "All</p>
        <p>the Tory Tadpoles and Tapers, the brewers' agents and Primrose Leaguers, have, consti- tuted themselves a court of public inquiry, and if thq Lords are told, and believe what they are told, that the Budget is unpopular, then they will do theik best to force a General Election. But if the Tadpoles and Tapers say, I am sorry, my Lord, but the sympathy with landowners is not so keen as your Lordship would desire,' then these great patriots will obey the Constitution."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FWTS AN3 NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>FWTS AN3 NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Our neutral contemporary of Saturday last was worthy of note in that it did not contain a portrait of either Sir Gilbert or Lady Greenall. * * *</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert says." he never knew an inde- pendent candidate who was not a Radical." This speaks rather badly for a number of Conservatives. It also reminds us of the gaying attributed to the Rev. Mr. Pilcher : "Scratch a neutral and you will find a Tory," —the truth of which experience has long since proved, * *</p>
        <p>During the debate on the Development Bill, Mr. Lloyd-George said explicitly that the £600,000 a year is in the first instance to be used "to improve the present roads "—in making them dustless, in improving and sometimes in widening them. This will be a welcome intimation to the motorists, most of whom would have no objection to paying a few taxes if the roads were correspondingly improved.</p>
        <p>* * * Pity the poor dukes! The last one: to squeal under Budget pressure is the Duke of Buccleuch. The amount which this noble- man can no longer subscribe to the Nithsdale Wanderers Football Club is one pound. The Duke's Chamberlain (how splendid the title sounds!) writes to the club I would have been very glad to have sent you a subscription, but owing to the large prospective increase in taxation caused by the present Budget it has been foutid necessary to curtail very largely his grace's annual subscriptions to such objects,and I must regret, therefore, that it is impossible for me to send a subscription.</p>
        <p>Slmon in the Mersey ! Shades of 50 years ago! But the fact remains that a fine salmon weighing 1511bs was caught in a gully of the Mersey at Fiddlers' Ferry last Saturday morning, by Thomas Kenwright and John Ashton of Penketh. Probably the salmon had mistaken the Mersey for the Dee, but it was not allowed to rectify the error. Its captors sold the catch piecemeal and there was salmon for dinner at more than one house in Penketh on Sunday. * * 'Mr. R. L. Outhwaite, whom Warring- tonians will well remember for the stirring address he gave at Parr Hall a few years ago on the inner history of the Boer War, has been addressing meetings this week in the Knutsford Division. On Thursday ,evening he was at Moore with the Liberal van, and had pitched his camp at Smithy Bank when the local agent for Sir Gilbert Greenall insisted on his removing from this site. Mr. Outhwaite was by no means dis- comfited and subsequently held a successful meeting in the village, where the signifi- cance of this revelation of landlordism was not lost sight of. * * *</p>
        <p>Most of our readers will recollect the forged ticket trick which was attempted at Parr Hall on the occasion of the Licensing Bill Demonstration addressed by the Bishop of Liverpool. They will therefore be inter- ested in the repetition of the same tactics at fashionable Bournemouth, where a week ago, &amp;out 100 persons got into a Budget Protest League meeting by the use of forged tickets. Such tactics are to be condemned wherever they are tried, but the amusing feature of the Bournemouth case is the sequel. The " Times " on Thursday morning contained a half-page advertisement offering £lOO for information which will tell the League who printed the tickets, &amp;c., &amp;c. All this" in the interests of clean political life, &amp;c., &amp;c." One would really imagine that this sort of thing never happened before.</p>
        <p>* * * We are glad to notice that the Warrington Town Council is to be represented on the forthcoming Continental Town Planning tour by Alderman Bennett, chairman of the Street Improvement Committee. The tour has been organized by the National Housing Reform Council and will be taken part in by municipal representatives and others inter- ested in the housing and town planning question. The party left London yesterday and they are to visit, amongst other places, Rheims, Nancy, Strasburg, Stuttgart, Ulm- on-the-Danube, Vienna, and Paris. They will be received in most of these places by the municipal authorities and special facilities will be afforded them to study the unique examples of town planning which may there be seen. The information gained will no doubt be of the utmost service, in view of the probable early passing into law of the Town Planning Bill.</p>
        <p>The trade returns for August bear out the hopeful augury of the figures for recent months. The country is decidedly doing better, even though the exports have not yet made up the deficiency as compared with 1908. That year, however, was marked by good trade in its earlier months and by rapid decline later on. The present year, on the contrary, has only begun to show revival within the last two or three months. Now that has set in there is no doubt at all about the improvement. August imports, as compared with those of the same month in 1908, were 13 per cent, better, and the exports were s'B per cent. higher. More- over, on the eight months of the year the imports show an increase of over 3 per cent. The most, gratifying feature in regard to this is that the improvement is almost entirely, in food and raw materials. Manufactured goods account for no more than £550,000 in a total increase of £12,532,000. The figures may be commended to Tariff Reformers, who believe that a vast revenue can be got from diities on imported manufactures. * * *</p>
        <p>A correspondent draws our attention to the fact that the "Guardian," which weekly affirms _ its " neutrality," has for same time not given a syllable of a report of the great open-air meetings held at Bridge Foot by the Progressive Labour League. The matter does seem worthy of nnte, and especially when contrasted with the fulsome accounts—which the same paper never fails to publish—of comparatively small gather- ings with the opposite political complexion, such as those of the Women's Tariff Reform League, and its masculine but moribund relative. Our correspondent asks a question which we do not feel competent to answer. It is: How long,will that interesting bit of fiction continue to appear at the top ot. your esteemed contemporary—l mean the line that reads" Neutral in all matters political and religious ?" For our contemporary's information we will just add that the Pro- gressive Labour League is holding another big meeting on Monday in Parr Hall, at which Mr.,Ure, Lord Advocate, is announced to speak. ''Twould be a pity to miss this!</p>
        <p>What we are witnessing in the House of Commons, where Mr. Balfour is thumping the barrel every night with the hope of pro- ducing an echo, is a repetition of the argu- ment that went on over the land clauses. All through this fortnight there has been one sustained effort to ring the changes between the big man and the little man, just as they rang the changes between the big landlord and the little landlord during the land debates. The argument is quite simple. The first stage is to scold the Government for being too •severe on the little man. 'Very well," reply the Government, "then we will let the little man off lightly !" The second stage is to say, "Ah ! but - how unfair on the big man !" And so they fall back on their own true love the Big Brewer." _ _ * * *_</p>
        <p>The big brewers Wish to have it every way. First they passed it on to the public in increased prices. There they over-reached themselves a little, as they did in their speculations in "tied houses" ten years ago._ Then their next step is to go to the House of of Commons with tears in their eyes and to complain of their misfortunes. You are crushing us ! " they say. They entirely forgot to mention that they b,P,vQ passed every ounce of pressure, multiplied many times, over to the great consuming public. Even if the great brewing companies have lost over the transaction, it is difficult to feel very sympathetic. The instinctive feeling towards them is that towards a bully who has tried to thrash another boy and received instead an unexpected thrashing himself. * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith's conSpicuous patience and forbearance has really taken all the sting out of these fierce attacks. With calm logic he has shown the hollowness of their tempers and alarms. He has proved that the new licensing duties are justly and equitably graduated to the breadth of the back that will have to bear them. They fall lightly on the small man,and the burden only gradually increases as the back becomes broader. Is not that a fair principle of taxation? It is the principle that runs through the whole Budget. But the great objection of these gentlemen has been to valuation. That is where the brewer and the landlord really sympathise. Mention the word" valuation" and they grow pale to the lips. And yet, every little shopkeeper and workingclass freeholder has his house valued every five years for rates and taxes. Why should the landlord and brewer go free? Why should they be so afraid of letting us know the value of their possessions? It really looks very suspicious. * * *</p>
        <p>We are not astonished, therefore, that the idea of defeating the Budget through the licensing duties seems to be rather weaken- ing. At the beginning of this week, there was a decided chill on the subject throughout the Opposition ranks. There seemed to be no response in the country. It must have been decidedly discouraging to find that a week of barrel-thumping in the House of Commons ended up with some of the most enthusiastic Budget meetings that have yet been held in the country. There was Mr. Winston Churchill's speech at Leicester, Mr Birrell's at Man- chester, and Lord Crewe's at Leamington— a trio of magnificent demonstrations. The result of these demonstrations, indeed, is that the enthusiasts for the intervention of the House of Lords seem to be rather weakening. One energetic Sunday paper now stands almost alone. The " Spectator " shivers on the brink. The "Spectator would have the House of Lords throw out the Budget as long as the words " Tariff Reform's were not mentioned in the subse- quent General Election. That sounds likely, does it not? "Nature abhors a vacuum," and the British public shares that prejudice with nature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Irfte ; LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>Irfte ; LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services will be held in Buckley- street Methodist Church to-morrow (Sunday) at 10.30 a in., 2.30 and 6.30 p.m., by Mr. J. W. Cannon, Wesleyan Lay Missionary, of Haydoek. There will be a musical service at 2.30.—0 n Monday afternoon at 2.30 Mr. Cannon will conduct a service. There will be a public tea at 6 o'clock, and afterwards Mr. Cannon will give his popular lecture- entitled " Remarkable.lncidents in My Life Work." The chair will be taken at 7.30b3 Mr. Thomas Gandy, of Penketh. The annual distribution of Sunday School prizes in connection with the Warrington Ragged School and Mission, Bank-street, will take place to-morrow at 3 o'clock. Mrs. C. E. Parker, of Penketh, has consented to distribute-the prizes. The autumnal sermons in -connection with Hill Cliffe Baptist Church . will by preached to-morrow (Sunday) by the Rev. A. Spurgeon Hollinshead, of Bradford.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a concert will be given by the Primitive Methodist Choir, Legh-street, at the Salvation Army Barracks, Academy-street. Mr. A. Spruce Ix-ill conduct. The chair will be taken by Captain Waring at eight o'clock. The price of tickets is 3d. each and the proceeds will be in aid of local needs. Inspector Hill of London will conduct the meetings to-morrow. The classes at the Borough Gymnasium will re-open on Monday, September 13th. Sudden Death. The police have been notified of the sudden death yesterday of Richard Nettles, aged 45, of Greenall- street. Heart' disease is believed to be the cause. Mr. David Clegg's Organ Recital. - -</p>
        <p>There was a--large gathering in Bold-street Wesleyan Church .on Monday evening to hear a recital by Mr. David Clegg, the famous organist and composer,who had ingratiated himself with a numerous section of the public by his clever performances on the occasion of previous visits to Bold-street. Mr. Clegg is unquestionably a highly talented organist and his brilliant powers of execution never fail to gain admiration. He has made himself .master of the instrument and knows how to produce a great variety of tone colour and rich orchestral effects. While his methods are not always orthodox—indeed they have been the subject of considerable con- troversy—he invariably enchains the attention and wins the appreciation of his audiences and he appeals to a ,much wider public than many painstaking exponents who devote themselves more strictly to classical works. Mr. Clegg's programme on Monday evening gave him full scope for the display of his skill in various styles of music. A. pleasing feature was the introduction of the celestial bells, a delightful combination of chimes played in conjunction with the organ by Mr. Ronald Baker, of Queen's Hall, London. Many of, the items were Mr. Clegg's own compositions, two‘• of the most popular being the "Invocation to Melody" (written for full military band, including solos and 'cadenzas for . different instruments), and a "New organ symphony." Mr. Clegg played the finales to Mendelssohn's third and sixth organ recitals, with considerable. taste, and we ,should like to have heard him in some of the more ambitious movements from the sonatas. He also gave a fugue on an ancient melody by Bach. An attractive item was the " Invocation to Service at Lucerne Cathedral on a Festival Day" (St. Moritz) which introduced a wealth of picturesque effect. The programme closed with the celebrated storm piece, the representation of the thunderstorm which interrupts the revels of the peaceful rustics in a Swiss village, being remarkably realistic.—We understand that Mr. Clegg has again been specially engaged as solo-organist at the coming great Musical Festival at the Crystal Palace, London.</p>
        <p>-VICTORIA PLUMS, 2s. PER Doz. DAMSONS V next week. Watch my Prices. CANARY BANANAS, Gd. per doz. McDERMOTT, "Cheapest Man Alive." MARKET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seddon, MP. and Lord Rosebery. Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., in moving a resolution supporting the land clauses of the Finance Bill at the Trades Union Congress, yesterday said the Laird of Dalmeny would, to-day, be playing at politics, while the burden of taxation was crushing the heart of the people. Local Bankruptcy. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>On the application of Mr. T. S. Steel, the debtor's solicitor, a receiving order was made at the War- rington County Court on Tuesday, by Mr. Registrar Ridgway, on the debtor's petition, against Edward Hopley, residing in lodgings at 36, Ayton-street, War- rington, and lately residing and carrying on business at 41, Buttenuarket-street, Warrington as a musical instrument-dealer, but now out of business. The debtor was also adjudicated bankrupt. TABLE AND HANGING LAMPS, new designs now</p>
        <p>showing.—J. S. GARLA.ND, 51 Lo 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Crosfleld's Band at Belle Vue. .</p>
        <p>Twenty brass bands competed on Monday ;:t Belle Vue for the challenge cup and the other prizes which are offered every September. The test piece was a selection from M. A. Martiani's opera " Il Bravo." The result of the contest was to bring to the front a band of which tomparatively little has until now been heard—the band of the Foden Steam Waggon Works, Sandbach. This band never figured amongst the prize-winners at Belle Vue until last 'July when it got the second place. On Monday, however, it took the championship cup from the famous Black Dilke Mills Bgml, which had tc Dc ccliWnt with the second prize The other four prizes were won by the following bands :—Three, Perfection Soap Works, Warrington; -I, Hebden Bridge ; 5, Wingates _Temperance ; 6 Irwell Springs. Buffaloes Initiated.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart., Mr. Robert Pierpoint, Councillor J. Fultoh-Smith and Councillor Thomas S. Steel were initiated members of the "Samuel Wakefield" Lodge of the Royal Antediluvian Order of Buffaloes at a gathering held at the Midland Hotel on Tuesday evening. The ceremony of initiation was impressively performed by Bro. " Sir " T. Walter Boden, Past Grand Primo of • England, who was assisted by Bro. " Sir " Isaac Thompson, R.O.H. K. 0.31., Bro. Ivan Goodwin, K.0.M., Bro. William Leigh, K.O.M , and Bro. J. W. Taylor. There was a link of 113 for the newly-initiated brothers. After the ceremony, speeches were made by Bros. Isaac Thompson, W. Boden, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Robert Pierpoint and Councillors Fulton-Smith and T. S. Steel. An excellent musical programme was given by the Rose of Denmark Glee Party and other artistes. "HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES save 50 per cent of fuel.—Agent J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55 Bridge at., IVarrington.—Advt. The Late Mr. John White. " Holy Trinity Parish Magazine" says :—Since our last issue more than one untoward event has happened in our midst, but the one thathas overshadowed every other was the sudden and unexpected death of Mr. John White, who has perhaps filled a larger space in the public eye in this district than anyone else for something like 35 years. Although prominently identified Itith the social and political life of the town, his chief interest was given to church matters, and there is not a parish in Warrington that has not felt his influence for good and benefited by his assist- ance and counsel. In our parish he was greatly interested, and in everything attempted in connection with church extension we benefited by his kindly and valuable advice. Some of the works in which his interest was shown were :—The enlargement of the Mission Room in Liverpool-road, the building of the Museum-street schools, the renovation of Trinity Church, (involving re-seating, Szc.), the erection of St. Luke's Church, the acquisition ot the house in Sankey- street for a parsonage, &amp;c. In former days he frequently assisted at the Sunday evening services at Factory-yard by preaching. He never spared him- self in order to meet the calls the church made upon him from time to time. - Women's Circle.</p>
        <p>The second session of the Warrington Women's Circle was opened on Sunday afternoon in the new premises in Bridge-street, when the meeting took the form of a members' roll-call. Mrs. Clatworthy pre- sided and gave as her response "Every action has a keynote ; let Christ set it.' Many helpful responses were given, Mrs. Baker, Mrs. Taylor and Mrs. Elwell speaking at length. Miss Winifred Cox sang "0 dry those tears" and" God that maketh earth and heaven." Mrs. Letberen said her most earnest desire, looking on to the future work, was that the circle might ring out With no uncertain sound, old-fashioned truths. These were days of unsettlement, of doubt, :chaos, much- error, and many farse teachers. One was painfully.bnpressed. by the loose hold of Christian people generally on great truths, which even a few years ago were universally acknowledged to be essential and fundamental ; their belief in doctrine depended upon their experience of that doctrine. A shallow idea and indiscriminate experience went hand-in-hand. She hoped the circle would always stand for and cleave to the three It's--Ruined by the Fall, Redemption by the Blood, Regeneration by the Holy Spirit. The ' value of co-operation was emphasized not only with the leader, but among the members. Sometimes they were questioned "Are you always in difficulties? Why is it your circle is always asking for more ?"—becanse of its success. If it were failing there would no he the new calls and the desire to embrace new opportunities. On Monday evening a lecture on "The history of English drama " was given by Miss Qonway. This class will be continued next Monday by Miss Waring At the meetihg to-morrow afternoon Mrs. Owen will take the chair. The subject "What the Circle stands for" will be dealt with- by Mesdames Moore and Letheren. On Monday at 8 p.m. Miss Waring will hold her literary class, and on Thursday at 7.30 a fancy work class will be superintended by Mesdames Jones and Barbour.</p>
        <p>PEVERIL " FIRE GRATES still unsurpassed, Tnspection invited.—J. S. GARLAND, Kitchen Range and Store Warehouse, 51 to 35, Bridge at., Warring- ton.—Advt. - Old Lady's Sudden Death. At the Borough Police Court, on Wednesday, Mr. Coroner Brighouse conducted an inquiry into the death of Ann Annis, 71 years of age, late of 5, Hedgecock-yard, Mount-street, which occurred at the Infirmary on Monday —Elizabeth Evans, sister of 'deceased, 48, Eldon-street, Stockport, said that deceased was her sister. She last saw her alive in March last, when she was apparently in good -health. Since the death of her brother she had been getting her living as a charwoman. She was not so very active.—Fred Thompson, chauffeur in the employ of the Patten Arms Hotel, said that at 8.45 a.m. on the 24th August he was driving along Sankey-street, and when he reached Market-gate and was about to turn into Horsemarket•street, the constable stopped him whilst he cleared the traffic. In a minute or two he was allowed to proceed and turned into Horsemarket- street. On doing so a woman started to cross the road on the near side, and P.C. Gibbs shouted to her. She hesitated and then turned back, and fell on her left side in the roadway. He pulled up at once and the car missed her. The constable picked her up and witness offered to take her to the Infirmary, but she refused. Witness then took his "fare" to the Central Station, and called at a boot-shop at Market-gate where deceased was and offered again to take her to the Infirmary. She refused to go, and he returned to the Pattern Arms Hotel.—P.C. Gibbs corroborated, and said that the car did not strike deceased. Later a cab was sent for and she was taken to the Infirmary.—Dr. James Johnson;senior house surgeon at the Warrington Infirmary, said that he had made a post-mortem examination, and he thought that deceased had died from heart failure, due to a fatty degeneration of the heart.—The jury returned a verdict of "Natural Causes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LOCAL CRICKET RECORD.</head>
        <p>A LOCAL CRICKET RECORD.</p>
        <p>A remarkable match between Warrington Thursday and Mr. Down's Band was played on the Warrington Cricket Club ground on Thursday afternoon. Down's band had the assistance of Cresswell and Podmore of Warrington cricket team, who went in first and put up a local record by scoring 104 and 50 not out respectively. Cresswell also made good use of the ball taking five wickets for 23 runs. Through the darkness Cresswell's bowling was unplayable. The following are the scores:— -</p>
