        <p>"PETER PAN" IN COURT. SEQUEL TO STREET OBSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>Large crowds gathered round the Borough Police station on Tuesday morning when the victorious individual who styled himself "Peter Pan" was charged of a warrant with obstructing Arpley-road, on the 4th on June and also with a similar ofTenc3 on the sth. Prisoner, for whom a warrant was issued in the early rrirt of June' was arrested by the Preston police on -Monday and brought to Warrington by P.C. Dennett. The Chief Constable detailed the case and raid prisoner had been a source of great trouble to the police forces in the country and was arrested at Preston and brought over by P.C. Dennett. In the interests of the prisoner, be (the Chief Constable) was lilt desirous of saying anything More, but he would give evidence as to the obstruction.</p>
        <p>Detective Iteddington then gave evidence of the obstruction caused at Bridge Foot on June 4th and said prisoner stood there from 8 o'clock until 10.30 and the crowd was enormous. The following night the conditions were the same. _ _ _</p>
        <p>In answer to prisoner, witness said the,spot was used for public open air meetings and there bad been large crowds there previously. The people at prisoner's meetings were the scum of Warrington. Prisoner : I had no idea that Warrington had so much scum.</p>
        <p>P.C. Hewitt gave corroborative evidence. Prisoner said that news had been circulated through the country that he was " a worthless loafer and the Army had fed and clothed him,"and through these state- ments hehad lost an intended position at the Warring c,on Hippodrome, for it had been his Intention to give up public speaking. With reference to the summons issued by that court he did not see it or he would have answered it.</p>
        <p>Giving evidence on oath, prisoner said he travelled Great Britain under an agreement to publish a book oa the "sweating system "or the Salvation Army. He hdd had trouble with the police, but not for stealing, speakiig,etc. He came to Warrington by orders, and had 'a meeting at Midge Foot on Tuesday and Wednesday previous to the dates of the charges. On the Thursday he went to Manchester, but returned on the Friday and had a good meeting at Arpley-road. . The Chief Con- stable went down and told him that he had no right to hold meetings, and took him to the Police Station. A crowd followed them and he asked the Chief Constable to allow him to speak to them and ask them to go away, but be would not allow him. The Chief Constable tclok him to the station and bought his ticket for him to Manchester. He shook hands with prisoner and told him that if he was ever in trouble to mention his name. He knew the Chief Constable's duty was very difficult. Prisoner said his father had the honourable position as a member of the Australian Legislative Assembly. He was sorry the case bad been instituted against him: He had orders to return to War- rington by his employers at Manchester and that was the reason he did so. The Chief Constable: You had been ordered to come to Warrington to speak. Who ordered you? Prisoner: I refuse to answer you anything that is irrelevant to the case.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable: Well you say you were ordered to come to town to speak?</p>
        <p>Prisoner: Yes. The Chief Constable: Well why did you send me this letter. " I have come to town on business, but I am not going to speak. If you wish to acquaint me with anything give it to the boy. I will leave again shortly." Why did you send that letter? Prisoner: Because it was my intention not to come again to Arpley-road. The Chief Constable: Why did you not say that? Prisoner : I did not know the name of the road then. The Chief Constable: Why did you send me this lying letter. Prisoner: It is not a lyini; letter. Prisoner was pressed to give the name of the com- mittee who employed him, but he refused to divulge it. The Chairman (llr.H.Roberts)%a id if defendant did not mention the accredited society which was at his back the Bench could draw their own conclusions as to whether they believed his statement or whether they thought he came to exploit this matter for his personal gain. Defendant said the name of the organization was on the pamphlets in the Chief Constable's possession. The Chairman: Have you any objection to stating in open court the society who are supporting you? Prisoner: I have objection, sir. [ refuse to divulge the name because it is irrelevant to the case. Continu- ing, he said that before he left the witness box he would like to say %%hat great harm the Chief Constable had done him by saying he was a worthies. s loafer, a liar,and a scamp. According to law, a man's convictions should not be read until he was arrested.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : The Chief Constable has not charged you with that this morning and it will not alter the decision of the Bench in any way. Prisoner : Very well I will call the Chief Constable as a witness.</p>
        <p>Prisoner then cllled the Chief Constable and cross- examined him. The Chief said he remembered telling the Bench the prisoner had been convicted of fraud,that</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Rigby, 121, Church- street, Warrington, says :—" I am grateful for the benefit I have received from Doan's pills, and I have no objection to the facts of my case being published. " For between five and six years I was a great sufferer from kidney complaint, the worst part of the ailment being severe pain in my back, which was often so bad that I could not get about. I was almost afraid to stoop, because it was so difficult to raise myself. " I took medicine for three years, but it did me no good at all. It was different however, when I began with Doan's pills, for they helped me from the first and gradually brought me back to health. (Signed) Thomas Rigby."</p>
        <p>he was a worthless loafer, and that the Salvation Army 'tad fed him and sheltered him.</p>
        <p>Prisoner : Where did you get sort eN :clerics ? The Chief Constable: I have the pn"ce records. . ..</p>
        <p>Again addressing the Bench prisoner said the publication of the Chief Constable's statement about him, which had been read out in different towns had shaken him, made him ill, and grieved him more than twelve months, imprisonment could do. It was a question whether the Chief C,nstable had a right to adduce that evidence, and it was going to be thrashed out on a point of law.</p>
        <p>The Cn.airman said the Bench were sorry to see a man of such unquestionable ability in the box on such a charge as that. At ths same time they thought the Chief Constable had acted with great discret'on and had only been doing his duty in this matter. If prisoner would give the Bench an uniertaking to leave War- rington and keep out of it, or at any rate if be would not lecture here again they would dismiss the case. Prisoner said the Bench had - been very kind. He would promise not to talk in Warrington again. The Chairman added that nobody had a stronger regard for the rights of citizens than that Bench, and no one would stand up more emphatically for the right of public speech and free speech, but that must be subject to the rights and feeling of other people. CHARGE AGAINS r A FOOBALIER WITHDR 4.\\ N</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on rues lay before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair) and other magis- trates the char4e against John William Chesteis, a well known footballer for assaulting and br ting John Rowan, by breaking his jaw, was withdrawn.—Mr. Unsworth, who appeared for the complainant said tl,qt with permission of the bench, he would not offer any evidence. There was a civil remedy and complainant and his mother were very poor so that remedy would be more satisfactory. He therefore asked permission to withdraw the charge.—Mr.T. S. Steel, said he repre- sented the rrisoner and he supported the application of Mr. Unsworth. He thought he might say that the matter could be dealt with better in a Civil Court than in that Court.—The case was accordingly withdrawn.</p>
        <p>GIRL BURGLARS. At the Borough Police Court, on Thursiay, Lily and Winifred Burke, aged 12 and 9 respectively, residing in Eldon-street, were chargea with stealing from the dwelling-house of Mr. T. Atkinson, 8, Museum-street, on Thursday afternoon, September 2nd, a gold watch and chain, three gold rings, one gold scarf pin, one earring, a silver spoon, an electro-plated cream jug, two purses, a brooch, three baskets and a quantity of crockery.</p>
        <p>From the evidence it appears that on the above date Mrs. Atkinson went out of the house at three o'clock, and did not return until eight o'clock. She then found the rooms in a state of disorder. On looking through the house she found the articles included in the charge were missing. She gave information to the police, and inqui•ies were made by Detective Metcalfe, with the result that the following day the girls were seen at their home in Eldon-street, and produced the articles, which they had hidden in various parts of the yard. They had sold the gold chain to a woman in Mersey-street. The girls had gained entrance to the house by means of the elder girl dim'. • big up a water spout on to a water barrel and then on to a window sill about Mem feet from the ground. On opening the window she went downstairs and opened the door for the other girl. The affair would appear hardly creditable, but it was witnessed by a young woman next door.</p>
        <p>The case was adjourned from Saturday to see if the girls could be sent to an industrial school.</p>
        <p>- Th 3 Cilia Constalbe stated that the younger girl had not passed the doctor, and he thought it would be better to withdraw the charge against her. She bad evidently been drawn into the trouble by her elder sister. Since the adjournment the girls had been begging about the streets and that appeared that there was no control or care exercised by the patents. The elder girl was sent to the Brook House Industrial School until she is sixteen years of age and the father was ordered to pay 3s. weekly towards her maintenance. The youager girl was discharged with a caution.</p>
