      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MosDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) and Nfear. T. Locker, H. Thornton, W. Roberts and V. Furness. DISORDER WITH A MOUTH ORGAN.—Thomas Wade, of Erwood-street, was charged with being guilty of disorderly behaviour in Erwood-stre?t.—A constable stated that the defendant was sitting on the doorstep at two o'clock last Sunday morning playing a mouth organ with a crowd of children round was fined 3s. 6d. for his pains.—John Barlow, of Orchard. street, was fined 2s. 6d. for being guilty of disorderly behaviour in Orchard-street. Detective Clough said defendant was kicking tin cans about. WITHOUT A LICENCE.—Thomas Kirk, of Warburton- street. was fined ss. for keeping a dog without a licence Defendant said it was not his dog and he never had one. He had enough to do to look after his children without a dog.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—DRUNK, ASSAULT AND WILFUL DAMAGE. —Walter Hart, of 153, Church-street was charged with having been drunk in Knntsford-roaci the previous day and also with assaulting Richard Bretherton in his house on Knutsford-road and further with having done damage there to the amount of £3.—Complainant said the prisoner went to his house and told him he was the man he wanted to see. Prisoner followed complainant into the parlour and deliberately struck him and knocked a number of ornaments and crockery from the shelf.— Prisoner was sentenced to 3 months hard labour.</p>
        <p>ou lili/a</p>
        <p>1 littr4lll)66 4' .': AfT - . 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was again a good meating on Sunday last, which was presided over by Mr. Joseph Owen. Mr. Davey-Sm.th, in the course of his address upon "The one ideal," said that generally if the men that were met had only one idea we did not much care Lir their company. If they always spoke of football or cricket, we did not enjoy it. Paul had one passion, to win Christ and men for Christ, and he was determined to know nothing amongst men but Christ and Him Crucified.—Miss Ashworth sang "Evening Song" and "The Harbour Bay" in good voice. Mr. Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ ; Mr. C. Cotton and Mr. A. llealey also took part in the meeting. Tte closing hymn was " Jesu, Lover of my Soul," which was most hearily joined in.—lt is gratifying to note that 12 new mb ers joined the Society.</p>
        <p>Brick-street Independent Methodist Church. At the above place of worship, the anniversary ser- vices were held last Sunday, the preacher for the occasion being Mr. Richard Lee, of Wigan. A pro- cession was formed, beaded by the band, which paraded the streets of the neighbourhood, previous to the morn- ing and afternoon services. At the morning service Mr. Lee pretv:hed from the words," God manifest in the flesh.' The anthem "I will wash my bands in innocency" was ably sung by the choir. In the after- noon a service of song entitled, "The monk that shook the world," was effectively rendered by the choir, Misses 11. Howell, E. Shaw, and Mr. IV. Barton, jun., singing withciedit. The connective readings were read by Cuuncillor Hawthorn. At the evening service Mr. Lee preached a very helpful sermon to a large con- gregation from the text See that ye refuse not him that speaketh." Two anthems were sung by the choir, "0 taste and see" (Sir J. Goss) and "Let everything that bath breath pmise the Lord," Miss Howell singing the solo with taste and expression.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Late Father Sumner.</head>
        <p>The Late Father Sumner.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the Rev. Francis Wilfrie Sumner, 0.5.13., for tinny years the Superior of St. Mary's (R.C.) Church, Warrington, who passed away at Ample- forth Abbey on the 12th inst.' took place on Saturday. A r(quiem mass was celebrated at Ampleforth Abbey, after which the body was taken to the Monks' Cemetery on the hillside, where the interment took place, the last rites being performed by the Very Rev. Father Anselm Turner, 0.5.11., the Prior. The Rev. Father Basil Primavesi (who had been in attendance on Father Sumner for a fortnight prior to his death), repre- sented the Warrington Roman Catholic clergy.— Preaching at Sr. Mary's Church on Sunday evening Father Primaveei, paid a tribute to the devoted work which Father Sumner did at Warrington, particularly in beautifying that church. On Monday morning a solemn requiem mass for the repose of the soul of 1 Father Sumner was offered at St. Mary's Church, the celebrant being Father Primavesi, Deacon Father Wilsan. and Sub-Delcon Father McLoughlin. Mr. Eckersley presided at the organ and took charge of the musical part of the service.—At the Sunday services at the Church of the Sacred Heart reference was made to the deceased priest, and the "0 merit= " was sung during the offertory by the male members of the choir. At the conclusion of the last mass the organist, Mr. W. Hardiman played the Dead March in" Saul."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church Anniversary,</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church Anniversary,</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in connection with Bold- st-eet Wesleyan Church were held on Sunday last. In the morning the Rev. E. B. Keeling preached to a good congregation. During the service the choir rendered the anthem "Send out Tay light" (Gounod), and MrA.Booth gave a solo in her usual highly fin,shed style. The Rev. Thomas Miller was the preacher in the evening and he took as his text "The Kingdom of Heaven is as a man travelling into a far country" (Matthew xxv. I-1). He said that a striking fetture of Christ's teaching was the 'ease with which He pourtrayed familar aspects of life: aspects which we were afraid to formulate in clear statement. In the parable of the good Samaritan the element of chance in life was indicated. It was "by chance" a man fell among thieves; it was " by chance" that a priest, a Levite or a Samaritan went that way. Life's greatest sufferings and greatest. opportunities seemed to happen accidentally. Our theory of Providence must leave room for unordered events. God did not always actively interfere in the course of events. His ways were past finding out. Sometimes the withdrawal of God created fear and distrust in our hearts. Faithfulness would be rewarded, but unfaith- fulness, however voluble the excuses, would find its condemnation. There was an inert mass in Church life w dch was appalling. Let us do our best. If we could do little, let us do all we can. We must not assume that only the poorly couipped were failures Failures were found in all spheres. Meru talented men and women bad been spiritual failures. Referring to the parable where the man with one talent did nothing, he said that he feared that a ,s the r 3sitiou of many in our Churches to-day. Young men were not living wicked but their powers were not dedicated to Christ's servico. In many aspects their characters were admirable; unfortunately, there was no religious zeal. Deg neration was the result not merely of misuse, but of non-use of faculty. The clearest brain required the interchange of thought in order to retain or improve its alertness. We ought to apply all our powers to the service of Christ. He was worthy of our devotion. Whatever we rendered to him we but gave him back His own. it was His capital with which we traled. Powers of trOnd and body, advantages of education and birth were tokens of His love. Concluding, the preacher said tht gifts had keen bestowed upon us. Were we burying the talents rather than using them ? If so, let us now alter our ways. Soon the Lord of those servants would come, and woe to those servants who were not found watching. The, choir sing "Rock of Ages" and Mrs. Arthur Booth rendered "Hear ye Israel."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Wycliffe Church Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Church Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The nth anniversary of Wycliffe Church was cele- brated on Sunday last, when the Rev. Professor Armitage, M.A., of Yorkshire United College, preached two eloquent sermons to good congregations. in the morning he preached on the text, "if God be for us, who can he against us "—the motto of the Church. Preaching in the evening the preacher took as his text "But I w ye should understand brethren, that the things which hap r 3noi unto me have fallen out rather unto the furtherance of the gospel." (Phillipiaus 1-12). He said Paul wrote those lines in a poor room in the city of Rome. The city of Rome in the time of the later Cresars was a city of splendour and pride. He was not alone and there was a man with him who was in the uniform of a Roman soldier. More than that, there was a chain hindine the wrist of the soldier to the wrist of Paul. While Paul was writing the letter a Roman soldier was with him. Day and night, ay.tking or sleeping, a scornful Roman soldier was with him. Nero was upon the throne. Nero was a man who had put to death his own mother, his own relatives and all those who could lay any claim to the throne. Continuing, he said that Paul aslted what was to separate him from the love of Christ? Was tribulation, persecution, contempt, nakedness and the sword? He answered his own question when he said : "I am persuaded that neither height nor depth. nor any other crew ure shall sel.arate me from the love of God which is in Christ Jesus." God took Paul at his word and He sometimes took us at our word. When Paul wrote that epistle he wrote it to the melancholy jangling of the chain. We do not know how long his imprisonment lasted. At the very end of the Acts of the Apostles we read: "And Paul dwelt for two whole years receiving such as came unto him." Paul was a very sensitive man, who was hoping and hoping that the door would open and he would be released, hut it never did. All the soldiers learnt that Paul was not imprisoned because he wrs a murderer, thief or political agitator, but that he was there for Christ's sake. They were rough men for Paul to talk to about Christ, but he did it. He told them what manner of man He was, how He went in and out amongst the people ; how He love d the little children ; how He visited the sick. He told them what wonderful thingt proceeded out of His mouth. He talked to them of the Cross. Paul wanted to preach in Spain. He was thinking in continents, but God brought him to the touchstone of humility and he was imprisoned. Paul knew what suffering was and he knew how to bear it. Paul said that "All things work together for good to them that love God." The prison was made sweet and the bonds were made toler- able. When Paul was put in prison then his work began. The Apostle whilst in Rome wrote the epistles to the Philippians, Ephesians, Galatians, Colossians and Timothy. Those immortal epistles were written to the music of that melancholy chain. He expected that those letters would just reach their destination rod then be torn up, but God had preserved them. We were tming spiritually fed by Paul in prison. In every country those letters v ere being preached to-day, and they were leading men through the shadows into the Christian faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Gamekeeper Threatened.</head>
        <p>Gamekeeper Threatened.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Tuesday, a poacher named James Davieson, of Warrington, with thirty-two previous con% ictions, was committed to gaol for ten weeks for trespassing in pursuit of game on the land of Sir Richard Brooke, Norton Priory, and for threatening Ernest Harold Long, a gamekeeper, by raising a loaded gun to his shoulder and threatening to murder him. Pointing the gun at Long, Davieson retreated step by step to a boat and rowed away on the river Mersey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>LIVELY MEETING.</p>
        <p>More About the Royal Visit.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Coun- cil which was held at the Town Hall on Tuesdoy evening lasted nearly three hours and was the occasion of some lively scenes. The Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) presided and there were also present :—Toe Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) ; Aldermen A. Bennett, W. Bolton, James Evan, D. Tinnion ; Councillors, T. H. Ashcroft, T. Barton, T. Birtles, J. Broadhurst, T. Chantler, J. G. Craik, E. Cunnington, J. Dickenson, J. Halliwell, J. Hawthorn, F. T. Lashford, J. T. Locker, J. Lord, J. Pemberton, H. Roberts, W. Rogers, J. Sankey, J. Skelland, T. S Steel.o. Toole, G. N. Vernon, J. Wilkinson, and W. J. Wilson ; Messrs. j. L. Whittle (town clerk), A. T. Holloway (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasure-), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Deas (water engineer), J. M. Murray (director of education) and Dr. J. C. Hibbert (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>VOTES OF CONDOLENCE.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>Letters were read fro:u Mrs. W. B. Roberts and Mrs. John White thanking the Council for expressions of sympathy in their bereavement. REPRESENTATIVE MANAGER APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Councillor ROBERTS, Mr. George Venn was appo!uted a representative manager for Christ Church school.</p>
        <p>THE MARKET GATE CIRCUS.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement and Lighting Committee approved plans of new premises to be erected by Messrs. Boots, Ltd., at Market Gate. Alderman BENNETT said the Council would be glad to know that the proposed circus was being proceeded with and he was sure the plans would commend them- selves to public approval. STREET LIGHTING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CRITICISM OF ALDERMAN JAMES EVANS.</head>
        <p>CRITICISM OF ALDERMAN JAMES EVANS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Street Improvement and Light- ing Committee a report of the Chairman of the Lighting Sub-committee with reference to the lighting of various streets and passages off Liverpool-road was submitted to the committee. Resolved : "That the Lighting Sub-committee he dissolved and that it be re-con9tituted as follows :—Mr. Councillor Cr iik, CJuncillcr Dr. Joseph, Mr. Councillor Lord (convenor), Mr. Councillor Wilkinson, and that the above report be referred to such sub-committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT moved and Alderman JAMES EVANS seconded the adoption of the minute. Councillor BROADHURST gild that some time ago the Deputy-Mayor p-omis -d that every legitimate application for street lighting would be met. He (Councillor Broadhurst) had been making application for a lamp to be p!aced in oue of the streets for nearly two years, and yet it had not been met. The Chairman of the C,rmmittee, who walked through the whole of the ward, found a state of things which ought not to exist, and submitted a report to the committee of what should be done. The committee thought the chairman bad ignored them, but considering the application had been before the committee for months past it was only right that the district should he inspected. Councillor PEMBERTON said that no letter had been sent to the committc 3 asking for work to be done. One gentleman bad taken upon himself to do too mucb Councillor BROADHURST : I have sent letters. Councillor PENIBERTON said that if the committee did not receive complaints they could not attend to them. The complaint should have been laid before the committee. Instead of that the committee was not called together but one gentleman took it upon him- self to act for the committee. Alderman JAMES It]vANs said that all that was done was to report as to what, in his opinion, the sub-com- mittee should take into consideration, and should go round the ward to see. The attention of the step. committee was invited to the condition of things at the far end of St. Austin's Ward. There were no lamps authorised and no individual responsibility WRS incurred. There were not so many placards as formerly in November calling attention of the ratepayers to the badly-lighted wards. (Laughter.) Councillor -.PEMBERTON said he did not want to go into that matter.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS : Go the whole hog, if you like. Don't think of me. I know what your kindness is, John, on this matter. Din't consider me. (Laughter.) Councillor PEMBERTON said that when he came to the meeting be had no more intention of speaking on this matter than he had of flying from the roof of the Town Hall, (Laughter.) There were one or two wards well lighted, but it should be their endeavour to light the whole of the town. Alderman EVANS : Ycu do it.</p>
        <p>Councillor PENIBERTON, cont4lning, said that it was not right that one or two wards sould he picked out, tor was it right to have tLe work cf sub-committees done by one person. Alderman FINNION, in moving an amendment that the minute dissolving the Lighting Committee be referred back, contended that what had been done was undoubtedly a vote of censure upon Alderman James Evons, and, in his opinion, it was very ill-timed, for if ever a m:—.l deserved praise instead of a vote of censure it was Alderman James Evans, owing to the valuable work he had done for many years. (Hear, hear.) There was no town in Lancashire, or even in England, that was so well lighted in the back passages as Warrington was. This resolution had evidently been introduced in a temper or pique, or something of the sort, and there- fore it ought to be referred back to the committee for further consideration.</p>
        <p>Councillor H. ROBERTS seconded the amendment, because, he ssid, there was no man in the Coancil who would attempt less than Alderman Evans did to arrogate to himself the powers and duties Of the committee. Really, in these days; when it was so difficult to find public men willing to do a little more than their ordinary duty compelled them to do, it was, as Alder- mrt hod said, poor encouragement to a men to look after the lighting conditions of the borough and save members of the committee a great deal of work. Councillor ASHCROFT said that complaints made to the Chairman of the committee had always been attended to.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENN'ETT conten?ed that the committee had treated Alderman Evans with a harshness that was ill deserved. The punishment did not fit the crime and the cammittea's action struck him av being very ungracious after the hard work Alderman Evans had done for so many years.</p>
        <p>Alderman Boriro-s suggested it would avoid squabbling in the Council Chamber if the committee, at the next meeting, re-instated Mr. Evans. Councillor BROADHURST said the fact that Alderman Evans went round the back passages, which were in .a dismal condition' showed that he was the right man in the right place. His report was a report only. He had no idea of doing the business himself. Councillor PEMBERTON: The committee lid not say the lamps recommended were not required. The griev- ance—and there is no temper about it—is that Alderman Evans has taken upon himself not only tb;.s one case, but it has been going on for years. Alderman EvA.Ns : Name a case. Conn Allor PEMBERTON : One man has been a mem- ber of the sub-committee for two years and has not been summoned to a single meeting. Continuing he said it was the wish of the Street Improvement Com- mittee that the sub-committee should act. The com- mittee should bare been called together. It was not right that a committee of three should be appointed and one man act only for himself, whether he had done it in a good spirit or not. At the same time he thought that Alderman Evans was entitled to praise for what he had done in the past. Councillor WILKINSON said the committee thought it was a wrong principle for any Councillor to take the convener of a committee round the district and then fora simple order to be made for the committee to get the work done. Alderman EVANS : It was not an order.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON: When a gentleman writes a letter and states that lamps are required and Lfter the visit is made, the number is more than doubled -- Councillor BROADHURST : That shows they are</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON: Someone else must say they are required besides you and Alderman Evans. I am a member of the committee and want to be recognized, Councillor BROADHURST : You are recognized. Councillor WILKINSON: Will your hold your tongue! 'I have been a member of the committee for years and I don't know when I was called to a meeting. (To Alderman Evans:) I say distinctly as a member of the sub-committee, you had no right to go ronnd and visit streets with the object of re-arranging the lamps with- out inviting the sub-committee. Man, know thy place. (Laughter). Councillor BIRTLES, alluding to the breezy character of the debate, said they had seen scenes of that sort in the Council before, and they were very unbecoming. (Hear, hear, from Councillor Pemberton.) It would have been better to have waited until Alderman Evans had attended the meeting of the committee so that ho could have answered for himself and not have dragged the matter before the public. (Hear, hear.) He did not think the committee were doing themselves much good by acting as they had acted. He was very sorry to hear the heated diEcussion which had taken place. It did not reflect credit upon the Council. There were only one or two members who caused the obstruction, and some of them got up three or four tirr.es and spoke on the same subject. They ought to be stopped unless they stuck to the minutes. Councillor WILKINSON read the report of the Chair- man of the Lighting Sub-committee and contended that Alderman Evans should have called the sub-committee together and have allowed them to go round the district Councillor HAWTHORN thought it would have been more decorous if the committee had washed their dirty linen in committee. There bad evidently been a mis- understanding about a long standing grievance. Councillor WILKINSON: It is not my grievance; it is the committee. Councillor HAWTHORN contended that Aldehnan Evans had acted very straightforwardly and was to be</p>
        <p>commended for the energy he had put into the work and the great improvement he had effected in the lighting of the street._ _ _</p>
        <p>-Aldeiinan EVANS said his own personal feeling about retaining the position was that he should not continue it. He had kept within the usage of the Street Improvement and Sub-lighting Committee. He had done nothing in the shape of ordering, neither was he responsible for the removal of a lamp without the sanction of the committee. He went to look at what was required, and if he bad been at the meeting of the committee he could have explained the position of affairs. He simply recommended the sub-committee to go and look at what was needed and referred to it in the report and nothing else. Alderman Evans, in conclusion, said: I feel I have walked well, I will not say how far, hut I have done a good deal—a great deal that I shrill not tell. Councillor Pemberton may laugh. Councillor PEMBERTON : lam not laughing. lam only smiling. Surely it is not a crime to smile. You don't expeot me to cry, do you? Alderman EVANS : You can smile as long as you like, but if 30u put me on my fighting mettle I could put you in a corner. I have done my work to the best of my ability. I have tried to serve you faithfully and well for 11 years—(near, hear)—and yet you subject me to the treatment such as that resolution suggests. I leave the whole matter with the Council. (Hear, hear). The amendment of Alderman Tinnion to refer the matter back for further consideration was put to the meeting, 19 voting for it.</p>
        <p>The MAYort asked for votes against, but no-one ztood up and the amendment was, therefore carried. MESSRS. MILLING'S SKY SIGN.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement and Lighting Committee resolved that in accordance with the provisions of the Warrington Corporation Act 1899, Messrs. Henry Milling and Company, Limited, be given notice to remove the sky sign from the premises in Horsemarket-street. Councillor ROBERTS moved an amendment that the minute be referred back. He said that although the Corporation unquestionably had power to refuse to permit the erection of sky signs, he had a very serious doubt as to whether this structure was a sky sign at all. He should rather describe it as a roof sign. There was a very distinct and important differ- ence between a sky sign and a roofsigo, and he should, therefore, be inclined to give Messrs. Milling the benefit of the doubt. He did not think it advisable in view of certain pub!ic improvements to unnecessarily irritate a firm of such long standing as Messrs. Milling, who could be of substantial assistance to the Corpora- tion. Ths sky sign was absolutely free from the possibility of danger and was not objectionable in any sense. Councillor ROGERS seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT thought sky signs were not desir- able for, apart from the question of danger, they were unsightly and it would be a great mistake to encourage or even tolerate them to any appreciable extent. How- ever, Messrs. Milling put their sign up in good faith, not being aware of the fact that the Corporation were opposed to such signs. Having regard to the fact that in the next three or four years the Council hoped the circus would be completed and they would have new premises at Market-gae, they might allow the firm to continue the privilege on payment of a reasonable acknowledgement.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said be understo 3d the sign was an infringement of the law sal therefore the com- mittee could not give permission. The TOWN CLERK said the local Act of 1899 abso- lutely prohibited sky signs and, in his opinion, this was a sky sign within the meaning of the Act. Councillor PEMBERTON : Then I contend we have no option but to refuse it. If we grant this, what is to pre eat any other tradesmen coming forward and want- ing to erect a similar sign. If sou allow Milling's, you cannot refuse any other tradesmen. Councillor BARTON said the Committee had been assured that the sign was dangerous to the public and illegal. If it wore blown down and someone got killed, the Council would be blamed.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION sui;i;e7t:ed the amendment, and with all respect to the Town Clerk, said the sign was not a sky sign within the meaning of.the Act.(Laughter.) Councillor ROGERS : It is built in the roof. Councillor ASHCROFT : There is no doubt it is a sky sign. _</p>
        <p>onnoillor LORD slid that in view of the Act the Committee thought they bad no alternative but to take the course they had none.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROGERS said he thought the law was an ass in this case. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS : In this case the law is our on ass. (Laughter.) Alderman BENNETT said that in face of the fact that it had been made clear that the sign as illegal, he did not think he would be right in supporting the amend- ment.</p>
        <p>After some further discussion Alderman Roberts' amendment was carried by 13 votes to 11. The other minutes of the committee were then approved.</p>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE. THE NEW ADMINISTRATIVE BLOCK AT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS moved ani Councillor BURTON seconded the adoption of tha minutes which included the acceptance of Alr.J. Dolan's tender for the erection of an administrative block at Sankey Sanatorium for £783.</p>
        <p>Councillor ASHCROFT asked the Chairman how many patients there were in the building adjoining the pro- posed administrative block. He was given to under- stand there were very few. Therefore, what was the need for another building? Alderman BENNETT expressed his satisfaction at a resolution with regard to the conference on smoke abatement. It was a very promising sign and seemed to indicate that the committee were taking a broader and more adequate sense of their responsibilities in the matter.</p>
        <p>Councillor HAwntorm referred to a case in which a butcher had been censured by the committee for his action with reference to a tuberculous carcsee. It was one more evidence of the need of an abattoir in Warrington.</p>
        <p>CourOlio; LASHVORD thiiught the committee ought to have dealt more severely with the man. Councillor PEMBERTON asked for an explanation with regard to the proposed administrative block. The expenditure of £783 ought not to be put aside with a wave of the hand. Alderman EVANS said the plans of the administrative block had been sanctioned by the Local Government Board and discussed by the Council. The buildings already erected consisted of wards and nurses' quarters —a small w.ird and a large one. The Local Government Board originally insisted upon an administrative block considerably larger than the one it was now propos.d to build, and it would have cost £2,000 to £2,500. The matter had been well considered and thrashed out—in fact the committee had held back from spending the money ever since the sanatorium was erected, until the Local Government Board brought pressure to bear. They could not take any ordinary patients in the exist- ing bnilding, because it was to be used absolutely for smallpox patients. The minutes were approved. FOOTBALLERS AND VICTORIA PARK.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Town) Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, an application from the Latchford Recrea- tion Football Club for the useof a portion of Victoria Park was considered and refused. Councillor WILSON said there was no other open space available for the footballers. Surely they were not going to put the park under a glass case and pre- vent people from playing games on it. He understood the byelaws provided that the use of the park should be allowed to footballers for at least two hours a day. Councillor PEMBERTON said that in Liverpool and Manchester the parks were used for football, cricket, tennis, and other games. Victoria Park Was a public park and the public ought to have some benefit from it. Alderman Evaxs said that if the committee allowed this club to use the park they would probably have applications from numerous other clubs. After further discussion the minutes were approved, ASSISTANT WATER ENGINEER'S SALARY. The Water Committee resolved th it the salary of Mr. James Gray, the assistant water en&amp;ineer, be increased from 4:150 to £162 10s. per annum. Alderman BOLTON proposed and Councillor LOCKER seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor VERNON proposed that the minute be referred back, as he said tie thought that the present time, when trade w .s bat was very inopportune for increases of salary. Councillor BARTON seconded and said the Council ought to be careful and cautious. Councillor PEMBERTON said that if Councillor; Vernon and Barton knew the services Mr. Gray had rendered to the Corporation, he did not think they would consider the proposed advance of £l2 les. aim an adequate return. He. (Councillor Pemberton) thought he could claim to have voted against more advances of salary than any other member of the Council, but be supported the present recommendation. During the Lawson contract proceedings Mr. Gray did an enormout amount of extra work and if there was any official deserving of an increase it was Mr. Gray. Alderman BOLTON said Mr. Gray was au efficient official and bad done his duty faithfully. Councillor HAWTHORN said he was struck with the modesty of the committee's proposal. Ultimately the amendment was withdrawn and the minutes were agreed to. LIGHTING OF SANKEY-STREET. On the minutes of the Electricity and Tramways Committee, Councillor AsHcaorr complained of the bad lighting of Sankey-street and said that thorough- fare was often in darkness. The MAYOR: That is not on the minutes. THE ROYAL VISIT. COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON AND THE RECEPTION COMMITTEE'S REPORT. LIVELY SCENE. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the report of the Reception Committee appointed by the Town Council to make arrangements for the visit of his Majesty the King to the borough on July 6th, was presented and the consideration thereof deferred. A copy of the report was appended to the Council agenda. The MAYOR moved and Alderman SMETHURST seconded the confirmation of the minutes. Alderman BOLTON said he intended to move an amendment to eliminate the minute and teport of the Reception Committee from the proceedings of the Council. 1 hey had no right to be published tutu l they</p>
        <p>had been discussi in comm ittee. It would be time t: put it of! until the next General Purposes Commitatelly There were certain things which wanted more Ito explaining and better definition than there was in appendix._</p>
        <p>Councillor LASH FORD seconded. The MAYOR: It is not in order is it, Mr. Toels Clerk? Alderman BOLTON: It is in order. The rerort 141. no right to be printed. say Councillor LORD: Was there a resolution proP° econded and carried that the report be deferred tie ? the next meeting of the General Purposes Committee Why it is printed here I don't know. The MAYOR: It says the consideration was defe Alderman BourbN: But if we pass these and the report is printed in the Council proceedlsg:3: will seem as though the Council are agreeable to The TOWN CLERK suggested to the Mayor to wie, draw the report, whereupon Councillor PEMBEWr t exclaimed that the Town Clerk, who was not presents when the matter came before the committee, fie attempting to upset what somebody else had done. the speaker, should obj3ct very emphatically to g;eo such procedure. Why did he have it put on the aliou" at all ? The Tows CLERK: I am not attempting to uPsa the meeting. I only suggested to the Mayor thet should withdraw the minutes. Councillor PEMBERTON: It is a trick, and not wort of your department to put it on the minutes. The MAYOR: I won't allow you to abuse the Towl3 Clerk in that fashion.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEil-B-E—RTON: • You mat stop me. The MAYOR: I will.</p>
        <p>. . .•..•• Councillor PEMBERTON: I have done it. The MAYOR : Sit down sir, and behave yourself_%nt, Counbillor PEMBERTON: The Town Clerk is a ser''" of the Corporation. be The MAYOR: I rule that this mltter cannot discussed.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON: I have no dOubt, you Alderman BOLTON: I have moved an amendre that the report be eliminated. That is in order. Councillor ROBERTS: I ask the ruling of the le Clerk as to whether the ameniment is in order. The AfAsott : lam advised that it is not. Alderman BOLTON : It is in order. The TOWN CLERK: I say it is not•in order. Councillor ROBERTS: I should not withdraw the' report. It is simply a statement.of fact. Alderman BOLTON: It is not true. n(A Councillor ROBERTS: If you say the rep-at true, I will say you are impugning th 3 veracity of tvwcoo as responsible and respectable a body of men as Ye4l so possibly find in the town of Warrington- eta nothing of the members of the Corpciration who vi members of the committee. Councillor PEMBERTON : Who were not there. of Councillor ROBERTS: It is unfair to the member..., the committee, not here to-night to defend themselLe to suggest that they attached their names to somethil"' which will not bear the stamp of truth. be Councillor PEMBERTON : They have not attac their names. Councillor Loan: I would like to ask whether ,thell gentlemen have attached their names and how et—. were present at the meeting. Alderman BENNErr : This discussion seems to .Irt, quite unnecessary. The report was adopted liso unanimity, and a considerable number of the mein of the Council were present. do Councillor PEMBERTON : How many ?—only out of of 29. Alderman BEN-NETT: It was adopted with unaeloit and it appears to be a very clear, concise and ese'lsto statement. I, however, see no objection to the m' eras being withdraata for discussion at the next Gen' Purposes Committee. The MAYOR then withdrew the minute in quee;itge The other minuteaof the General Purposes Comp)" were approved.</p>
      </div>
