      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>BEATEN BY A SHORT HEAD.</head>
        <p>BEATEN BY A SHORT HEAD.</p>
        <p>The man in the street" was digesting the announccment of Dr. Cook's discpvery *hen a wireless wire from Commander Peary informed the world that he had nailed the ...tars and Stripes to the North Pole. If Dr. Cook's story is confirmed, then the gallalt s;c3tlltnander has been beaten by a short head. Peary took his plunge into the unknown in ebruary of this year, and his party consisted of four white nren, twenty-five Esk"mos, and C) dogs. Commander Peary is a veteran explorer. He made his first expedition to reenland in 1886, and since then the greater part of his life has teen devo:ed to exploring .11e Arctic regions. On his 1891 voyage he sustained a broken leg, while in the great `lPedition of 1898 he lost eight toes from frost-bite. Dr. Cook's comment on Peary's 14ccess was, "I hope it is true, for Peary's reports will confirm all my claims,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>SUMMER PILGRIMAGES.</head>
        <p>SUMMER PILGRIMAGES.</p>
        <p>MORETON OLD HALL.</p>
        <p>Everybody has heard of this dream in black and white, and everybody has seen the pictures. We invite the energetic pilgrim to come down with us into Cheshire to see whether it is as pretty as the pictures, and whether former chroniclers have exaggerated. We take our preliminary lift on the rail- way, this time to Hartford, and follow the quiet and beautiful road that leads through the forest of Rude Heath to the sleepy village of Holmes Chapel. After a modest refresher" which is more than due, we begin the second stage of the journey. Two roads lie open, and we choose the one that leads to :_liongleton, branching to the right and pass- ing the fine old church at Astbury. There is a modern Moreton Hall, which is a gorgeous place, but nobody seems to worry lbout it. It is the old " magpie " building half-a-mile further on that has drawn us hither.</p>
        <p>We had never rested our eyes on the timbered pile known as Little Moreton Hall, or to give it its everyday title, Moreton Old Hall, and we approached it with feelings almost akin to fear. We had read of it times without number and had accepted the assurance of those who ought to know that it is by far the most picturesque and impos- ing, by long lengths the most engaging, the most complete, the most perfect of all the timbered buildings of the land. This is tall praise, and since the timbered buildings are the most attrative of all buildings it would Follow that we are setting out to enjoy the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>, Bullock Bros.,'lllaccicsfiehl. MORETON OLD HALL (COURTYARD.)</head>
        <p>, Bullock Bros.,'lllaccicsfiehl. MORETON OLD HALL (COURTYARD.)</p>
        <p>luxury of a lifetime. We try to call up the proper emotions, as all true pilgrims should, but we have a haunting fear that we may be disappointed. Viewed from the road it is not strikingly impressive—possibly was not meant to be. It modesty turns its face away from the gaze of the passer-by, and looks towards the heights of Mow Cop and the aoon-day sun.</p>
        <p>I Bishop Moreton who contrived and worked out the marriage of the two heirs of the houses of Lancaster and York and in this sweet romantic fashion closed a feud which had devastated the kingdom. This John Moreton was Bishop of Ely and figures in Shakespears "Richard III." As a bishop we should never have heard of him but he did immense services as matchmaker—a fact which may bring solace to many who from gender and other causes are never likely to be bishops. THE GREAT GALLERY. The greater portion of the building is unfurnished, and we may wander at will through the big rooms exploring the nooks and corners, and speculating about them to the heart's content. The staircase by which we ascend to the rooms at the front is remarkable in being built around one long shaft of oak. This single piece of timber standing upon the ground floor reaches to the third storey, and the heavy stair circles round it. It is at the top of this stair that we are ushered into the most remarkable chamber of all, the long gallery. It fills the upper part of the main building and'runs the entire length of it. Like the similar room at Haddon Hall, it is oak-panelled throughout, and has the added charm of a decorated oak roof and fine windows that run 'almost the entire length of the room. Carved and painted at the ends of the room are two quaint representations which exhort the beholder to put his trust in knowledge and not in luck. As showing the dangers of trusting to the latter,Dame Fortune is shown with blindfolded eyes and on her wheel is written Qui modo scandit corruet statin which may be translated, "He who is now climbing will soon be falling down." All the large rooms on the south side are panelled from floor to ceiling in oak of rare beauty, and though divested of furniture and somewhat forlorn they have a quiet beauty and dignity which no amount of neglect can quite destroy. In the moonlight they would be splendid. The leaded windows casting fantastic ,shadows and all the ghosts walking would make a picture we would gladly wait to see. But half-day pilgrims can't have everything. _ THE KITCHEN AND GREAT HALL. We have held in reserve the exploration of the portion of the hall used by the tenant. A little maid shows us the kitchen with its gigantic octagonal oak table,and the sideboard with as many drawers as there are letters in the alphabet. It is difficult to believe that these shelves and drawers (so fresh do they appear) were in use when their owner was disturbing himself over the visit of the Spanish Armada; .and that the Moretons who used them are sleeping in graves so ancient that all trac?; of their resting-place is lost.</p>
        <p>The magnificent "Great ' Hail" with its carved dining table next engages us and we pass from it 'to the retiring room which' adjoins. These rooms are lighted by the famous octagonal bays and we may sit here if we choose to take our tea—a luxury, by the way, of which the builders of Moreton Hall never knew. WINDOWS AND MOTTOES. One of the most pleasing things to the antiquarian visitor is the preservation of the beautiful old glass. The Hall was built at a period when there had been a healthy revolt against dinginess and the builders revelled in the magnificence and largeness of their win- dows. It was the time when Lord Bacon complained that the houses were "so full of glass that we cannot tell where to come to be out of the sun or the cold." The fine glass must be seen to be appreciated. There is genius in the design of its diaper pattern and it preservation through centuries of upheaval is remarkable indeed. The fondness of our forbears for diamond-writing has some illus- trations here. On one of the panes can still be read the distich cut with a diamond: "Man can noe more knowe woman's mynde by teares</p>
        <p>Than by her shadow judge what clothes she weares." This is dated 1621. On another pane we have "I stay here both day and night To keep out cold and let in light." A fine crusted old Tory has given us his</p>
        <p>motto "Henry Mainwaring All change I scorn." Some of these autographs 'on the glass are nearly three centuries old.</p>
        <p>THE WATCH MOUNDS.</p>
        <p>It is a calm warm day and we walk across the fields to survey the old place from a dis- tance. Time has laid a band upon it, but the care with which it is being preserved promises that many generations will yet be charmed by its old-world beauty. So may it be.</p>
        <p>We pause in our walk to inspect the two large mounds raised on each side of the moat. These have given rise to much speculation and are commonly considered to be watch- mounds. It is likely that they were made from the soil dug during the making of the moat, and may have been placed here as places from which all corners by the road could be observed and an alarm given. Possibly Roundheads and Cavaliers have been sighted from hence, but these are the piping times of peace, and as the pilgrim sits to the table that now adorns the summit he is watching for nothing more dangerous than ti-e maiden bringing his pot of tea. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PERFECT TUDOR MANSION.</head>
        <p>A PERFECT TUDOR MANSION.</p>
        <p>It is when we stand full in front after a journey down the drive that we realize what a treasure we have in this old pile. A perfect moat flows around it that can be crossed only by the stone bridge that leads to the fortified door. We find ourselves looking for the portcullis and have to remind ourselves that it is after all not a castle, but just an old Manor house. As such it is perfect. There have been . no modern "improvements" and little restoration. By some providence this great heap of wood and wattle has been spared to us through three and a half centuries to show us what a fine manor house was like. It only needs an attendant got up as a lord or lady of the -sixteenth century and the illusion would be complete. We should be back in the spacious days of Drake and Raleigh and stately Queen Bess. Yes, here is the date cut in enduring oak MDLIX. Here are fortified double doors, double barred, behind which they slept in safety, window nooks to gossip in when days were calm, and hiding places to run to when days were stormy. It is now kept as a farm house and the tenant occupies some of the rooms. Externally the fabric is exceedingly well looked after and the upkeep of those vast masses of black and white must surely absorb the whole rent of the farm to say nothing of the modest three-penny pieces paid by visiting pilgrims. Internally things are much better preserved than we could expect and the most expectant and fastidious visitor can hardly be disappointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FAMOUS BAY WINDOWS</head>
        <p>THE FAMOUS BAY WINDOWS</p>
        <p>The fine gate house, three stories in height, rises beautiful- above us as we approach to make the close acquaintance of the Hall. But beeutiful as it is it has not a tithe of the charm of the court yard. We pass through the archway to enter a square pebble-paved central court on which open out the windows and doors of the State apartments. It is a picture to call forth an exclamation from all but the most callous. These are the far famed octagonal bay windows and they are in timbered architecture what the Henry VII Chapel of Westminster is in decorated Gothic, its last and consummate flowering of beauty. Every timber blossoms into carving, inscriptions run over them, quaint figures fill the panels, and beautiful glass completes the charm. The pilgrim who has stood in the courtyard of Moreton has seen the fairest thing he will ever see in that architecture of oaken timbers of which Cheshire is the true home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>"BY THE GRACE OF GOD."</head>
        <p>"BY THE GRACE OF GOD."</p>
        <p>The inscriptions may be copied. Around the upper bay windows we have: "God is al in al thing. This windows whire made by William Moreton in the yeare of oure Lorde MDLIX."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>In the lower bay and more easily read :</head>
        <p>In the lower bay and more easily read :</p>
        <p>" Rycharde Dale carpeder made thies windows by the Grac of God." That "by the Grac of God is very significant. It was a great task, and he felt he had achieved something. 'He certainly had. And if, as is probable,this Richard Dale designed as well as wrought the house front our admiration must be unstinted. The fact ••• that his master allowed him to cut his name so prominently suggests that he owed to him a debt of gratitude, and did not stint the praise. _ _ _</p>
        <p>If we may hazard a guess this is the oldest part of the house and was once the front, but in process of time the present front was built before it, leaving the rich old oaken house in the shelter of a courtyard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ANCIENT CHAPEL AND A STORY.</head>
        <p>THE ANCIENT CHAPEL AND A STORY.</p>
        <p>From this quadrangle we gain entrance to any part of the building. We decide to explore first the tiny chapel. It will be a quiet place to rest in after our journey and as we sit we will recall a story of the original Moreton of Moreton Hall. We dug it up out</p>
        <p>of an ancient tome and as it is ecclesiastical in flavour it will suit the atmosphere and give a side liglit on the period to which the hall belongs. It seems that Rode of Rode and Moreton of Moreton had a dispute on the subject of their seat in church. They neither of them seem to have.read the parable that urges men to take the lowest place, for they quarrelled as to "which should sit highest in the church and foremost go in procession." Like foolish men they went to law and Justice Bromley and Sir William Brereton tried the case. They had law in abundance before the matter ended, and law costs, of course, in proportion. A jury of twelve good men and true finally had to judge, " twelve- of the most auncyent men in Astebury " says the manuscript. The two disputants pleaded on grounds of ancestry, blue blood, antiquity and precedent, and the jury must have had a weary time while they listened to it. The twelve ' auncyent men" hit on a happy verdict. They refused to take them by antiquity or blood and reckoned them (whioh must have been a sore blow) by money. The xrdict was "He that may dispende in landes by title of enheritance ten mark or above more than the other."</p>
        <p>This satisfied the jury, bat didn't satisfy the disputants. And so there was more law and more bickering and a big family- feud. There is only one satisfactory way to finish a family battle and that is by a good wedding. So Tom Rode married Alice Moreton and as they both sat in the same pew the question of having the best seat could not crop up. And of course they lived happy ever after,though doubtless the lawyers went into sackcloth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>FAMOUS MATCHMAKER.</head>
        <p>A FAMOUS MATCHMAKER.</p>
        <p>The old house ," gave breeding to that famous Bishop Moreton" says the Vale Royal Itinerary, and it was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>SAMPLING PROTECTION.</head>
        <p>SAMPLING PROTECTION.</p>
        <p>LABOUR'S FATE IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>By Mr. H. Whitham.</p>
        <p>The word Protection carries with it a meaning that has not, as yet, been put to the front by those who are so strenuously urging upon the workers the necessity for Tariff Reform. 1 shall attempt in this article to Prove that workmen and employers in England are now going through such a revolutionary epoch as they never before experienced. Now conditions are being created, and unless these are directed with great caution and with as much provision as human foresight can bring to bear upon the industrial situa- tion the results of the passing of the competitive system will be, to put it mildly, disastrous. All this wild and Criminal talk about building Dreadnoughts and making every man into a soldier is not patriotism it is just the opposite inasmuch as it takes away the mind of the intellectual from the consideration of those phases of civilization that make for peace on earth and good-will towards all men. If you depend upon Canada, Australia and others of your Colonies joining in the work of building an Imperial navy, you are depending upon a reed that will bend beneath the weight, and will certainly break. There is now being called together in Canada a convention, of labour composed of representatives of the two largest labour organizations in the world, and the call of this body indicates that the war talk in England will be taken up at the convention. On this point it says: • "Canada is a country of peace. .1 he problem of living on lines of harmony is difficult enough with. out having fastined on us a vast expenditure ior non-pronuetive but highly dektructive effort. War is hell' for the workingaitin. Labor -cannot be si:ent at th;s juncture but bliLu'd..unite with all eace-loving citizens of Canada to squelch in its iLlancy the forced .3entiment that would involve us in the quarrels of ()Mr lands." Interesting as the above subject may be I must leave it and speak of the matter thdt ,is nearest to action. What is the other meaning that the word Protection carries with 'it? It is this, the ward stands for a method that enables the manufacturer to keep from and outside of America the results of cheap European and Asiatic labour, and admitt ad libitum, the cheap European labourer. We should deal with the Asiatic as we deal with the European, but for the proven fact the Asiatic rich man comes as a subtle and forceful business man, and wherever he plants himself he employs cheap labour and sells cheap goods, and like the plague he drives out his white neighbour. The Asiatic pays a higher rent for stores and residence. Leases them if possible and puts an extra floor in each room. The patriotic American sings "My country 'tis of thee; sweet land of liberty" &amp;c., &amp;c., and in spite of sanitary laws, pockets the extra rents. Keep out the restate of cheap European labour, but scour all Europe for cheap labour, and import that. There is at McKees Rock, Pennysylvania a very large plant known as the Pressed Steel Car Co. This Company believes in three truths, God, the Church, and the protective tariff. It has a stock of -five millions of Pounds and a large portion of this stock is water. A very large percentage of the men employed are known as "flunkies "and they do not know how to either read write or speak English. At this time of writing the men to the number of 5,000 have been out on strike. They are not allowed to organize, and so it is not organized labour that is making the trouble. Riots come about when scab labour is introduced and at this date near upon a dozen dead men testify to the beauty and efficiency of that immortal slogan of greed, Protection. I should like to give the history of the invention of the Pressed Steel Car. How it was invented by a poor labourer McKee. How the invention was got hold of by the capitalists and hovv all concerned have made wealth by leaps and bounds, but I must confine myself to the question of Free Trade in Labour alongside and as an outcome of Protection in all things of an inorganic nature. • What is known as the "pooling system" is the cause of this strike. The company fixes the cost price which it will allow for the cars. This cost is divided among the various gangs engaged in building the cars. The shiftless and the idle get the same share of the pooled earnings as the faithful workers. Allow me to note here that this an outcome, not of Socialism, but of Capitalism. The man who works piece work is no better off than- the man who works by the day. However they work, much or little, no man in the pooling system knows what he has earned until he opens his pay envelope. In the process of 'making a car it goes from one gang of workers to another. If gang No. 1 de their work perfectly and pass it on to No. 2, and there the work is spoiled, No. 1 must share in the loss. This is one of the " benefits "of pooling. The loss may be caused by- the foreman misreading his blue print instructions, thereby necessitating the tearing out of all the rivets. A worker states, " I know of a case where fiftten or twenty car-sides were misriveted because the foreman had made such a mistake." '0 hell,' he Kik', that's in the pool.' " The pool rights all mistakes. When a machine breaks down or there is a shortage of material the loss in each case is charged against the entire number of men at work in that particular pool. A case was put to the President of the Company. Take a pool of fifty men operating five machines, ten to a machine. One machine breaks down, either through flaw in machine or carelessness of men operating. The President could see nothing unjust in making the other forty men bear the lost time instead of the company. The company wants all the fat but none of the lean. A captain of industry is a fine fellow and receives much applause at the hands of a blind public. Carnegie is one of these and while he is struggling hard to give away the fortune he has made, he finds time to sometimes tell the truth. When lie was before the Ways and Means Committe of the House he made the remai k "The jig is up." He was somewhat premature; all the same, he knows where his profits came from. -This is the situation :—lf you threw a stone .every five minutes for one hour at the 5,000 Pressed Steel Car strikers you would run precious little risk of hitting an American. "Give us free trade Labour' and a tro- tection on what these free trade. labourers' prodace in order that we can compete with the cheap labour of Europe. Don't blame us for buying labour in the cheapest market, for you have made it possible. Don't blame us for discharging highly paid Americans and substituting miserably paid foreigners. Don't blame us for fooling the people; business, you know." Such is the cry of the Pressed Steel Car Company, and similar corporations in the steel trade. I am quoting largely from a report of the situation made by the Rev. George R. Dunn, .D.D ~and here is what he says of the wages earned by these Hunkies, who are supposed to make the laws under which they work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>JUNE 15, 1909.</head>
        <p>JUNE 15, 1909.</p>
        <p>Per Hour.</p>
        <p>Check No. 5,023— Received for 5 days' work, 3, 95 dols. Sc Check No. 4,621— Received for 2 days' work, .40 2c Check No. 4i495 Received for 7 days' work, 3.80d015. slc.</p>
