      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>JUNE 30, 1909.</head>
        <p>JUNE 30, 1909.</p>
        <p>Check No. 4,753 Received for 3 days' work, 3.85 l3c. Check No. 8,014— Received for 4 days' work, .90 2ic. Check No. 8,134— Received for 13 days' work, 18.85 141 c Check No. 7,213</p>
        <p>Received for 4 days' work, 3.10d01s 121 c. Cheek No. 6,588</p>
        <p>Received for 14 days' work 16.80d01s 12c. Check No. 5,016 _</p>
        <p>Received for 4 days' work 4.15d01s 101 c. Check No. 4,050</p>
        <p>Received for 9 days' work, 10.35 111 c. Check No. 4,950 Receiued for 5 days' work 3.45d01s 7c. Check No. 4,912</p>
        <p>Received for 4 days' work, 4.00d015. loc. These twelve men, whose names I can give, averaged less than 10 cents per hour. Of the thirteen cases cited by the committee of the strikers to offset the statement of President Hoffstot that the men are well paid, not one received as an average hour wage Lover 17 cents. These cases are all taken from the refficial pay envelopes of the Pressed Steel Car Company. Men are killed daily, and no one is admitted to the grounds who is likely to make trouble. A contractor living in the town said to the reporter," They don't think as much of a human being as they do of a piece of pig iron." A large number of the employes live in Scheenville, derisively called " Hunkeyville," where there are long rows of boxlike company houses, some 200 in number. They are double houses of four rooms each, well ventilated, but without water. The company charges 12dels. per month for four rooms, expecting that each renter will take in boarders to help make the payments. If England adopts one plank in the Protection platform her people will be forced to witness the workings of the spirit of greed as we heroin America have been witnessing it during the months that have been taken up in building a tariff schedule. Each man has been keenly interested in the dominant business of his own district. "You help me to keep the tariff on imported stockings, and I will help you to keep the tariff high on gloves.'' Employers and the Press, s hich is in the hands of the advertisers, -all join at frightening; that is intimidating the workman, and so hypnotise his faculties that the local political bosses have no difficulty in gaining votes. Even were this an exaggeration the Speaker of the House has so much power that he can altogether block or forward any measure; and so it follows that he and a few very rich men rule America.</p>
        <p>Now I wish my readers to have another example of the treatment meted out to the unorganized common labourer. Early in July a lady clerk in the office of one of our contractors was shot dead. From the testimony adduced at the inquest, it appears that the motive for the crime lay in the fact, that at the present time the firm now referred to, as well as most other firms employing ordinary labour, have a pernicious system of paying the labour in checks and drafts at dates remote from the time at which the checks are issued. The time of payment is delayed two and three months and more, and the poor man is compelled to discount his earnings with brokers at rates varying from 20 per cent. and more, if he wishes to receive his money at once. This paper he is often unable to dis- count at all, and has to wait the pleasure of the boss. This evil prevails throughout the country. Coupled with this is the practice of compelling the men to board and lodge under conditions, provided by the employers, who also keep out of the man's wages sums varying from 2s. to 4s. per month for hospitat and insurance purposes, which purposes never materialise. My own experience has had variety. The only time I ever went out with one of these con- structing gangs, I worked a week and having had enough 1 asked for my pay, and was told 1 was in debt and was handed a pass to take me back home. The pay check system has worked with me in this way:. After receiving them I have seen the large majority of the men go to the saloons and without difficulty, but not without price,get their checks cashed. Not being a customer of any particular saloon I spent ten cents and took a ride to the cashier's office. All wages are paid monthly, the consequence is, every one pays his tradesmen's bills monthly ; and the child of the house, grows, up fully habituated to the practice of carrying the book to the corner grocery.. As you walk the street you notice that most tradesmen ale anxious to inform you that "your credit is good," and they very much desire to have " your name on their books. Now then, touching this poor girl bookkeeper who was so-foully murdered because she represented this abominable system. -Cunningham, the murderer, presented to the girl a draft for 12dols. 50 cents. She began to say why she could not pay it; he at once shot her dead, and escaped. The police arrested another, and an innocent . man, and at once proceeded to put him through the various " degreen," a method peculiar to American police courts. The real criminal at once gave himself up, with the state- ment that he could 'net hide away, and see another man punished for his crime. A Nevvipaper report says : Cunningham' the selt-confessed murderer of Miss Braszh, passes his time in h:s cell at the City Prison with apparent cheerfulness. Singularly, the plisoner seems to 11 gaining in weight, and was • quite bright and chatty 3esterday. Asked if he had been stricken by any Budd2n twinge of remorse for his cold-blooded deed, he sLook his head negatively, and said: "Not at all. I have felt all right." "Do you still believe that you were just ified in shont'ng down that young girl ?" "Certainly," he answered," she was keeping me from getting my money. I earned the money and it was mine." "Would you advocate that all others who might be deprived of their wages resort to the name bloody method in getting satisfaction ?" "Oh, as to that," replied he," everybody has his . own ideas." "Are you able to eat and to sleep ?" " Yes," he said. "1 didn't have much appetite before when I was worrying about my money, but since I have been in jail I have got back my appetite and I eat and sleep fine." • When asked if he had planned any defence, he replied that he had not given that matter much thought yet, but presumed that he would not be able to advance much of a defence, seeing that he had freely confessed to the murder. . _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Thc man i perfectly satisfied with his work. He has sacrificed himself to end a pernicious system, and it is scarcely likely they will get a jury to agree to hang him. The common remark is, Cunningham shot the wrong person. As I close my writing the evening papers give the following with reference to the strike now going on-in Pennsylvania.</p>
        <p>cic-tv Etism ENT WILL BE ASKED TO INVESTIGATE CONDITION OF WORKERS.</p>
        <p>PITTSBURG, August 23rd.—Three columns of smoke came from the chimneys of the Pressed Steel Car Company's plant in Schoenville to-night, the plant being in operation despite efforts of mobs to scare off the imported worki,gmeo. The strikers seemed to realize for the first time that the com- pany could do without them. More State police have been ordered bore to augment the company of mounted constabulary on duty at the car plant. The strikers realize that the mounted troops are too much for them. Yet all day the troopers were subjected to abuse from windows and doors whenever they chanced to pass a strike sympathizer home. In retaliation every striker or sympathizer who left his doorstep was searched. Besides searching, the troopers examined the strikers personally, and if they bore bruises or traces of being clubbed, they were promptly arrested, as the troopers considered such evidence proof that the men had participated in last night's rioting. Twenty-five men were arrested belore nightfall. Those who resisted were manacled to the troopers' horses and dragged through the streets to the plant entrance.</p>
        <p>H. WIIITHAIf,</p>
        <p>San Franciaco.</p>
      </div>
