      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WHOSE HAND?</head>
        <p>WHOSE HAND?</p>
        <p>BY ANNIE 0. TIBBITS.</p>
        <p>Aut:ior of "The Love that Won," "Flunted Down," "Hidden Hands," "The Finger of Fate," "Robes of Shame," ex.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIX.</p>
        <p>For a moment there was a dead silence. Through a swimming mist Olga saw those two face each other, and the whole world seemed to stand still as she saw rec'ognitior. leap to their eyes. It almost seemed as if Ethel recognised Sam Perry too, for she stooci' still with a jerk, and faced him with a look of startled surprise. Then she bowed, in a queer, cold, stilted fashion, and turned away. Dick Sterry had gone up to him. "The last time I saw you was six years ago," he said'. "You were his friend. Can you speak to me ? " For an instant Sam Perry seemed to shrink, and for a moment he looked nervous, almost afraid. Then the coarse grin spread over his face.</p>
        <p>"Well, yes, I was his friend—in a way," he said.</p>
        <p>Dick's face was grave and stern. "I be- lieve you gave evidence against me?" he said. "How could I help myself?" Sam Perry broke out. "I had to. But I said you weren't guilty, and I stuck to it, and nobody was gladder than I to see you get off, even though I only saw you at Greatbridge once or twice. I tell you, I was glad when you were acquitted." Dick hesitated still, but Ethel's face had brightened, and she put out her hand. Oh, thank you," she said. "Mr. Sterry our friend, and we are glad to know any- one who believed in him at that -terrible time."</p>
        <p>Olga stood, pale and cold, against the tree, staring blindly, wildly, helplessly at those three who had met by a ghastly chance. Sam Perry was smiling blandly into Ethel's face. All his confidence had re- turned. He was serene and unruffled, talk- ing glibly now that the ice was broken and Dick Stery had fallen back. Dick watched him suspiciously, pulling at his moustache As he stood. The horror of the time that this man's face had conjured up for him was back upon him hideous and cruel.</p>
        <p>• When Ethel turned away at last he went with her, with his face grey and his hands trembling. They left Olga with the man. She was standing in her curious, rigid atti- tude, and when Ethel turned at the bend of the drive she seemed to be standing still. "What a strange thing that that man should have turned up now," Ethel said as they went. "Isn't it funny? It seems miraculous."</p>
        <p>Dick nodded, but his eyes were on the sky. "Yes, but I wish you hadn't asked him to Jbme to see Geoffrey," he said. "I don't Like the fellow. I distrust him."</p>
        <p>Ethel bent towards him. "Distrust him 7hen he stuck up for you so?" she cried, " and when he believed in you at the time of the trial?" "But</p>
        <p>• "So he said," said Dick, bitterly. I don't like him. I don't believe in him."</p>
        <p>Ethel was silent for a moment, then: "I wonder why he has turned up now?" she said, and she was suddenly taken aback by a queer movement on Dick's part. He stood still abruptly, and, with a set, grey, terrible face looked into hers.</p>
        <p>"That is what I am wondering," he said, huskily. "Directly I saw him—talking• to Miss Murray. I—l—wondered--".</p>
        <p>"Wondered what?" Ethel asked.</p>
        <p>Dick hesitated for a moment, then slowly and almost reluctantly drew from his pocket a scrap of paper..</p>
        <p>It Was a cutting from a newspaper, and, as he opened it, his face looked cold in the strong, hot sunshine; it looked frozen, and haggard, and old.</p>
        <p>_ " Have you seen the newspapers to-day?" he asked.</p>
        <p>Ethel shook her head, and slowly he handed to her the cutting he had in his hands.</p>
        <p>"It seems folly," he said. "I think I am a fool to think of such a thing, but somehow I believe that that has to do with the Great- bridge _murder. Look at it." _</p>
        <p>Echel took it in her hands and read the same words that Olga had read only a short time before: -</p>
        <p>"On board th "The boat is sinking, and there is in sight. In a few moments the end upon us all, and being about to die I declare that I murdered my husband at Gre years ago, and I ask forgive 'for the shadow of shame that all these year.</p>
        <p>Signed 0 Witness "What does it mean?"</p>
        <p>Ethel stared at it. she asked.</p>
        <p>"It means," said Dick, "that someone on board a sinking boat confessed to a murder, and I believe it was the murder of Stephen Henderson that was meant. Do you see the letters 'G r e'? That might be the beginning of Greatbridge Hall. And suppose- it is, Ethel? Suppose it is? Most of the words are washed away, and the signatures are lost, but it mentions my husband.' Therefore, it is the confession of a wife. And to-morrow I am going to see if I can find traces of Stephen Henderson's wife. It is only a chance, a great chance; but somehow I have a strange feeling about that paragraph. And now, that man Perry being here, too, seems BO strange." He broke off, and for a moment or two Ethel stood in silence. Then she put out her band to him.</p>
        <p>"Let me come with you," she said, softly. "Let me come and help; I may be useful to you. Oh, do let me come. If you should - have to stay more than one day you can put me in a train, and I will come back as good as gold." "But I shall have to go early," he said. "I'll be up at six," she replied, "or ready, • anyhow, to catch the first train. Oh, Dick dear, do let me help you." She was irresistible, and he yielded. • "We'll tell Geoffrey to-night," she said, "and get him to agree. He is sure to agree, and then Dick, °how will you set about things? then, will you do first ? " Dick stared thoughtfully in front of him. . Then he said, slowly: "When I made in- quiries about Stephen Henderson six years ago I found his wife living in a poor part of Kensington, near Notting Hill, in the house of a woman named Crawford. It was to her I went, and her 'whom I saw. Mrs. Henderson was out teaching, and. Mrs. Crawford told me all I wanted. She seemed to admire Ws. Henderson. She said that she had nursed her from a baby, and still seemed to look upon her as a child. She gave me a great many particulars, and she satisfied me that Henderson was legally married • and that he was a bad lot. She hated him. I believe if she could have murdered him she would have done. She said he had married his wife in order to ruin her mother, and that, in fact, 'her mother was at that very time dying through his cruelty in that very house. She told me all about the marriage, gave me the name of the church, and shewed me the cer- tificate which she had taken possession of." He broke off, and then went on again: "I shall go and look her up," he said, "and find out what Mrs. Henderson has been doing since the murder. I expect the police • looked her up at the time, but I scarcely knew or cared what they did, but now—now I'll find Mrs. Crawford, and, through her, trace ilendereon's wife. A woman had been 'to, the Hall on the day of the murder, but no one could tell who it was. No one saw her except Henderson himself and the man who h was with him—that man Perry was there." •He stopped again. They were walking slowly, with bent heads, over the grass under the shadow of the trees. Ethel looked up, -sighing a little. • What was Mrs. Henderson's name?" she • asked, rather suddenly at last. " Olga," Dick replied, and Ethel looked up .soUh Ntart.</p>
        <p>. -she cried, breathlessly. tic w—strange ! " . IDicit walked on with his to bent. 441</p>
        <p>"flow-</p>
        <p>thought so too at first—for a moment," be said, "but Olga is not an uncommon name Rer full name was Olga Elizabeth Trennant. I opied it from the certificate which Mrs. Criwford shewed me." Ethel was silent for a moment or two, then she drew a long brer th. "Of course, there are lots of Olgas in the world," she began, "and even that man—" She broke off, then looked round into Dick's face, hesitat- ing, with her own convulsed.</p>
        <p>• "Oh, Dick," she • broke out, after a moment—" oh, Dick, even though that man has turned up in this strange way it means nothing; it can mean nothing."</p>
        <p>He took her hands in his and looked steadily, gravely into her eyes. "No, no, dear, of course, it can mean nothing," he said. "If I thought it did—if I really thought it did—l should not take you with me to-mor- row. But it is just a coincidence, that is all." She tried to echo his words,. but a strange fear was upon her. That Henderson's wife was named Olga, and that they, should have just seen Perry, who had been at Greatbridge Hall at the time of the murder, talking to Olga Murray, now seemed somehow uncanny and curious. And suddenly the very breath seemed to leave her body. She lifted her hand, in which she still held the little cutting, and stared at it wildly. It had been signed by someone whose name began with 01</p>
        <p>• • • • • • • • • • Olga stood rigid, with her back to the tree and her eyes staring wildly before her, until Ethel and Dick were out of sight. She felt icy cold, even in the hot sunshine. Her head throbbed and ached, and when she turned again to face Sam Perry it seemed as if she was falling again—falling down an immeasur- able abyss, down a shaft deeper than any the Hall could boast.</p>
        <p>She put up her hand to her forehead and turned to see the leering red face that was looking at her. It seemed to dance up and down before her eyes, to advance and recede, to fade and come again in a strange way. Her head throbbed. A hot hammer seemed to beat on her brain, and only one thing was clear—that somehow or other she must get rid of Perry. She must get him away now, and then when he came again it would be too late. She must get rid of him for a time. Nothing would matter afterwards. " You must go now," she said, huskily. " I—l must go in." "Then, my dear, I'll come with you." he replied. ' "I have a mind to be introduced to Sir Geoffrey."</p>
        <p>She tried to lift her head. She could not see. A strange blackness had swept his face out of sight. She shut her eyes. "Sir Geoffrey is not at home," she said, quietly. "He is away for the day, but he will be back—to-morrow night. You must go away until then." He looked at her suspiciously. "You are not going to get rid of me." he said. "I</p>
        <p>She lay still, waiting. The afternoon passed with Mrs. Verriker at her side, anxious and nervous about her.</p>
        <p>"Geoffrey will scold," she said. "He will say we have not taken proper care of you. Poor little girl!</p>
        <p>She vut Fier soft hand on Olga's forehead, and Olga burst suddenly into a passion of tears. .</p>
        <p>"Oh, you remind me of my own mother," she sobbed. "You—you make me think of her."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Verriker, thinking it might help her to speak about her, sat down beside the bed, but Olga turned from her. "Don't ask me to speak of her," she cried. "It breaks my heart to think of her. She died—ruined through a blackguard. She died broken-hearted. Oh, my poor mother!" Mrs. Verriker sat for a moment in silence. Then she changed the subject, and hearing the grating of wheels on the gravel, went down to meet Sir Geoffrey. He came up full of anxiety. "Why, what have you been doing, Olga darling?" he asked. "Sitting in the sun? Oh, my sill little girl. Well, I shan't leave you again. shall see that you take care of yourself." Mrs. Verriker followed him, and with his hand on Olga's he looked round. "Have you heard anything of this freak of Ethel's? "he said. "-Sterry has got some business in London to-morrow, and she wants to go with him. Shall we let her, mater?" Mrs. Verriker looked round in a little sur- prise. "Well, he would take care of her," she said, "and she is dying to see a London shop. Since we have had some money to spend she has been longing to get new clothes. No, I don't mind her going, dear. He will take care of her."</p>
        <p>Olga heard dully, and scarcely understood.</p>
        <p>"They will be hack in time for dinner to- morrow night," he added, and the word " to- morrow " roused the fever in her veins again. To-morrow Sam Perry would come. To- morrow he would face Sir Geoffrey—would introduce himself as her friend—and—and what? And a sudden cold swept through her, a cold that froze her blood and stopped the beat of her heart. Why were Ethel and Dick going to London? She gave a low moan and dropped back upon her pillow. Geoffrey bent over her hastily. "It is only my head," she murmured. " Isl.othing—but my head."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CHAPTER XX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XX.</p>
        <p>Ethel looked down</p>
        <p>Know- too muen, ann ir you play me any tricks I'll do for you." She took no notice, only stood quite still, with that dreadful pain in her head threaten- ing to drive her mad, and he went a step nearer to her.</p>
        <p>"Do you hear?" he cried. "I'll do for you if you play me any tricks. I'll go now, but before I pass the lodge I'll find out whether you are lying to me or not, and if he's here then I'll come back. Do you under- stand?"</p>
        <p>"Yes," said Olga, heavily, "I under- stand."</p>
        <p>"And you understand that you're in a bad boat, and that you've got to keep in with me? I can do for you if I like, even if I can't actually prove that you—that you murdered him. I can very nearly prove that, I think. But, anyhow, I can upset your apple-cart with Sir Geoffrey, and you don't want that, I reckon? Well, I can tell you that I don't either, and if you agree to work with me, all right. I shan't be hard on you. A few chances are all I want; a little ready money occasionally. But I'm not a bloodsucker, and I'll be fair. Only you must be fair to me. See?"</p>
        <p>He stared at her for a moment and then turned on his heel. "Well, I'll come back to-morrow night," he added, "unless I hear at the lodge that Sir Geoffrey is at home, and then I'll see him to-day." He went off, leaving her staring after him. She stood for a long time after he had gone, afraid and unable to move. The pain in her head that had been gone in the morn- ing was back now worse than ever, ten times worse than it had been even at the time she had fallen down the shaft, and with it, ten times sharper, ten times fiercer, a pain at her heart that would never die.</p>
        <p>She turned at last and went slowly back towards the house. As she crossed from the shade of the trees the sun seemed to strike at her head. She put both hands up to it and then suddenly, with a loud cry, she reeled and fell headlong. The butler saw her from the hall as he passed the wide-open door. He shouted quickly to a housemaid who was passing and then ran out.</p>
        <p>Together they carried her in and upstairs, and a few minutes later Mrs. Verriker had sent poste haste for a doctor.</p>
        <p>And Olga lay still, lay broken like a reed, helpless and impotent. Her haggard eyes searched the doctor's face and her heart sank. She would not be allowed to get up again that day. Fate had intervened; heaven had stopped her.</p>
        <p>A faint, cold shudder ran through her. It seemed to her that heaven would always stop her and perhaps bring about the end in its own w[,!,y at last.</p>
        <p>She lay very still. She would give it up and let things drift, she thought. She could do no more. Things were too strong for her. Her own love for Sir Geoffrey had been too strong at first, and now circumstances were checking her. She could not go. Whatever she did, however she tried, she felt she could not go. Something would always occur to 'nevelt t her.</p>
        <p>It was very early next morning when Dick Sterry's dog-cart rattled up to the Hall door. The dew was thick upon the grass. The sun shone forth through a haze between the trees. A slight breeze was waking the world to life, and birds were calling, and fluttering, and flying. It struck a little cold as Ethel came out, and her face was a little pale, and looked pinched in the morning light. "I -don't believe I ought to take you," Dick said, as he tucked the rug round her. "It is too early. You will be dead tired-"</p>
        <p>- and gave a. shorp cry.</p>
        <p>"No, no, don't talk rubbish," she said, almost sharply, "I must come." She wriggled into the rug, and then they set off for the station. Olga's face haunted her as they went. Her name, her initial, the man who had talked to her yesterday after- noon, ran stupidly and persistently through her thoughts. It was nonsense, of course— stupid folly, and cruel even to harbour the thought, yet, in spite of herself, she won- dered—wondered how and where Olga had met Sam Perry before, wondered who was the woman whose name began with an " 0 " who had confessed to the murder on board the boat, wondered last of all with a sudden painful doubt why Olga had told them so little about herself, why they knew nothing of her, why her past life seemed a sealed book to them.</p>
        <p>_ They reached the station in plenty of time. It looked deserted, and cold, and cheerless, and it seemed ages to Ethel before the train rushed in at last. It was still longer before they reached London, and when they ran steaming into the great terminus she rose with a quick throb at heart and with a feel- ing of excitement. What were they going to find? What hideous secrets might they an ravel that day? They drove first to an hotel for breakfast and then their work began. They made their way towards Notting Hill. Dick remembered well his own journey there six years ago, and he made his way straight to the street in which Mrs. Crawford had lived.</p>
        <p>It was a dismal place, and a poor neigh- bourhood, as he had said, but it struck him now as being poorer, and more wretched and sordid even than it had been before, and the house in which Mrs. Crawford had lived had an air of desertion, and untidiness, and misery. Six years ago it had been neat, and clean, and well kept. He rang the bell and an untidy servant, who stared stupidly when they asked for Mrs. Crawford, answered them. "Mrs. Crawford?" she asked. "You'd better see the missis." She invited them in, and left them the same room into which Dick had been shewn years ago, and he looked round eagerly when he saw that it had not been altered. It was almost exactly the same as it had been years ago.</p>
        <p>-In a few minutes a woman came in and looked at them both curiously. "Did you want to see my mother?" she asked. "If your mother is the Mrs. Crawford who was living here six years ago, yes;" Dick said. "Is she at home?"</p>
        <p>The woman shook her head. "She is," she said, "but she isn't fit to see anybody. I doubt if she'd speak if she saw you. Did you see her six years ago, sir? " "Yes," said Dick. "Perhaps she would remember me if she saw me again. I had a long talk with her-" She wouldn't remember, sir. She re- members nothing and nobody, only a girl that she was fond of, and her she has in mind continually. She was fonder of her than she was of me, I always say, and though she's forgot everyone else she hasn't forgot her."</p>
        <p>"Was it a Mrs. Henderson ? " Dick asked, quickly, and the woman stared in surprise. "That was the name," she said, "and a nice young lady enough. I used to see her sometimes when my husband and I came here: Mother had lived with Mrs. Trennant —Mrs. Henderson's mother, you know, sir— as nurse, and she just worshipped Miss Olga, and she always said she wouldn't have got married to the blackguard she did if she had had charge of her. She often used to say that Mrs. Trennant and her daughter would have been well off and • happy if she— mother, that is—hadn't been married, but had been looking after them instead. And they came her just after I got married, so that mother was just pleased. Olga Trennant was the life of her, and she hasn't been tha same since she went. In . fact, we always think there was something behind - that turned my poor mother's head—something about Miss Olga, though we could never make it out, and mother is mum about it. Nobody can get a word from her. She never men- tions Miss Olga's name except to me, and then she looks as though she had said some- thing she was afraid of. And she's just ach- ing 'for a sight of her, as I know, sir. But we don't know what has become of her."</p>
        <p>"It was that I wanted to ask," said Dick, quickly. "I wondered if you could give me any information about her. NY e want to find her."</p>
        <p>The woman shook her head. "She went sway well-nigh seven years ago," she r :d. "Her mother died suddenly, and Miss Olga was half-mad. She was ill for a bit, and my mother nursed her through, and then my poor mother's own trouble began." " Indeed? " said Dick.</p>
        <p>"She went wrong in her head," the woman said. "She got so queer that we had to send her to an asylum for a time, but they dis- charged her, for there's nothing in the least harmful about her. Only she sits dumb and speechless, day after day, day after day. Sometimes you can't get even a yes or a no out of her. She's got the idea in her head, that she's going to harm somebody if he speaks, and so she's afraid to speak at all. Doctor says lunatics are like that—take ideas about things; and my mother's like it, and we can't think what made her so. She had no trouble in particular, nothing of any account, and we put it down to the death of Mrs. Trennant, though why mother should get that queer idea into her head about not speaking nobody can ever tell. It's rum, sir."</p>
        <p>Dick said it was. He was looking round the little room. "But might we see her?" he asked. "Would it do her any harm—just for a minute?"</p>
        <p>"Oh, no harm, sir. She'll only sit and stare at you, and shake her head. Sometimes she'll speak, but very rarely, and then only yes or no—half a word, as you might say, sir. Would you mind coming into the other room? She is getting that feeble we don't like to move her _about too much." _ _</p>
        <p>Ethel sprang up, and they followed her to a small room at the back, where an old bent and shrunk figure sat knitting aimlessly at a big square of wool. "It's all she can do," the woman ex- plained. "It helps a bit. She makes these shawls, and we sell them sometimes, and it pleases her." Ethel was examining it, and she looked up to speak, but the old woman was staring at Dick with a strange look growing on her face—a strange look of horror and fear and hope combined. Her hands were beginning to tremble, and her daughter went forward hurriedly.</p>
        <p>"Mother, this is Mr. Sterry come to see you. Do you remember him? The woman thrust her aside. She dropped her shawl and put up her hands as though to ward off a blow. Her features had awakened to life. The dumb-stricken look had gone, and there was remembrance in her pale eyes..</p>
        <p>"Why have you come again? Have you got pews of her? Have you come to tell me comething about my poor girl?" Dick scarcely knew how to reply. The old woman's eyes were fixed on his sharply. She seemed as if she was waiting in fear of what he would tell her.</p>
        <p>"No," he said at last, "I have not brought any news of her. I came to see if yon • could tell me any." Mrs. Crawford fell back in her chair, nodding her head vaguely. "To be sure,'! she said. "But I know nothing. I never did know anything. Remember that—l never did."</p>
        <p>She folded her hands and set her lips. "I suppose you have no idea 'where we could find her?"</p>
        <p>Mrs. Crawford shook her head. "No, no, know nothing," she muttered. Her daughter looked up. "It's no use; you'll get no more out of her," she said. Dick made another effort. Ile Txut oves the old woman's chair and faced, with a queer feeling at heart, the strange look of distress and trouble, of fear and anxiety that he saw in her, eyes. He remembered how fond she had been of Mrs. Henderson six years ago, and he shrank a little from what he was doing. He was trying to make her betray the girl she loved, trying to get from her poor dull brain knowledge which would run Mrs. Henderson to earth, and perhaps—who knew 7—stamp her a mur- deress.</p>
        <p>A heavy cold settled upon him. He felt suddenly tired and sick at heart. At that moment he hated the work he had in hand. Yet he must go on. For his own and for Ethel's sake he must go . on. He gave a glance at her now and her face encouraged him. "You remember me coming years ago, don't you?" he asked. "You remember I came to find out if Stephen Henderson was legally married to his wife. Do you remember shewing me the marriage certificate?" The dull old eyes grew strange for a moment, then the old head shook.</p>
        <p>"Long ago," she muttered,- "years and years ago, and she is gone." "And don't you know where she is nowt" asked Dick, "or anything about her?" The light faded from the old eyes. "Nothing, nothing," she muttered. • Dick asked more questions, but they were all in vain. Mrs. Crawford would say nothing more, and he and Ethel turned away at last in despair. "I'll come again," he said to the daughter. "Perhaps she may speak when she gets used to me. You will not mind if I come again?" She shook her head, following them out into the passage. "No, I don't mind," she said. "Only you won't get anything from her. There's one thing I might tell you, though I didn't dare to before her, for it gets her into such a state and makes her ill if I do. It is that Mrs. Henderson went as governess to a Mrs. Cathcart after her mother died, and she might be able to say what became of her, though I doubt it, for I remember her coming once—nearly four years ago it must be now—and asking if we knew her address. But you might go and see Mrs. Cathcart, in Kensington-square. I didn't dare to give her address before mother, for it does upset her so. It's like as if she doesn't want anyone to know that Mrs.- Henderson went there." Dick made a note of the address, and then they set out for Kensington-square. "We didn't do much there," said Dick, "but let's hope we shall have luck with Mrs. Cathcart."</p>
        <p>. They found the house, a very different one from the one they had just left—big, stiff, im- posing, one of the old, dull, red-brick Ken- sington houses that have an old-fashioned air of dignity and solidity. In answer to their knock an old grey-haired woman came to the door, and ushered them at once into a drawing-room when they asked for Mrs. Cathcart. A minute later Mrs. Cathcart herself joined them—a middle-aged woman, with a smiling, pleasant face. "Olga Henderson!" she exclaimed, when Dick told her what he wanted. "Olga Hen- derson ! Why, I've wished often and often that I could get some news of that girl, but I have heard nothing of her since she left me to go out to Australia—why, it must be nearly seven years ago now. She was not with me very long, but we all grew to love her, and the children were inconsolable. Why she went I could not understand. I offered her every inducement to remain, but she would not. Her mother had died just before she came to me, and her old nurse—a Mrs. Crawford. who seemed to be the only friend</p>
        <p>she had in the world—went mad, and so I think, poor girl, she felt she must get away from. Engrand.</p>
        <p>"Any-ay, she went. She got a situation out there. I heard from her that she was happy. I had, perhaps, three letters, and then no more. Oh, I would give a great deal to know that she is all right and happy." She broke off, and Dick hesitated a moment before he put the next question. "Did you know her husband?" he asked</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cathcart looked up sharply. "No." she said, "no, I am thankful I did n'A. Poor girl, she had a bad time. You know, of course, that he was murdered? That, no doubt, was one thing which sent her out of Rngland. Oh, her life must haves beon ctowded with horrors just. before Vie came to me—the murder of her husfrAnd, hr mother's death, her own illness, and thy Mrs. Crawford. Poor Olga! Poor late girl !" She got up abruptly, and going over tot I.s Liantelpieco. took from it .a photograph.</p>
        <p>" There she is," she said. "1 -neirer her without feeling heartsick about her. .he put the photograph into Ethel's handt, tir.:.t Ethel, looking down at it, gave II</p>
        <p>tTo be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BACKING THE BUDGET.</head>
        <p>BACKING THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>LABOUR LEAGUE'S OPEN-AIR CAMPAIGN CONCLUDED.</p>
        <p>More About the Land Question.</p>
        <p>With its meeting near Arpley Cannons on Monday evening, the Warrington Progressive Labour League brought a highly successful open-air campaign to a close. From their inception the meetings have been splendidly attended and the utmost enthusiasm has been manifested on behalf of the Budget and progres- sive legislation generally: The League has reason to be proud of the campaign which must have done invaluable work in spreading a true knowledge of the Government's democratic and far-reaching financial proposals. Monday evening's meeting was presided over by the Rev. R. E. Letheren and the principal speaker was Mr. Robert Lewis, of the Lancashire and Cheshire Liberal Federation, who paid a welcome return visit. There were also on the platform :—Mr. E. Wynne Jones, Mr. J. Howes, Mr. E. England, Messrs. J. Morton and J. Howes junr. (secs). The CHAIRMAN offered his hearty congratulations to the League on the splendid series of open-air meetings which had been held during the past few months. It must have been a great surprise to some of those people who always thought the worst of the workingman to discover that those meetings had not so far been used for the purpose of protesting against any part of the Budget—notwithstanding the taxes on tobacco and beer —and that they had gathered together week by week to learn what they could about the measure and to have its principles made quite plain to them. The more they had learned about it, the better they liked it, and they were prepared cheerfully to pay their share of the additional burden. All they asked was that the classes who enjoyed so many more privileges should pay ,their share all the more cheerfully. (Hear, hear). He would lika very sincerely to congratulate the meeting on the remarkable cheerfulness they had displayed under circumstances that were quite discouraging enough for even a Mark Tapley to consider it a credit to be jolly, because they heard to their great consternation a few weeks ago that their friend the Marquis of London- derry was se shocked and shaken by the Budget that he would no longer be able to send them his annual consign- ment of grouse.(Laughter).And yet somehow they seemed to think they would survive that serious loss. (Laughter). That day they read in the paper that the poor Duke of Buceleuch had written to a football club to say that on account of the Budget he was no longer able to continue his annual subscription. (Laughter). He supposed the football world would be shaken to its very foundation by that serious and sad loss. (Laughter). Poor man ! He had only got seven mansions and no doubt he had sustained a very serious compound fracture by trying to live in all of them at the same time. (Laughter). His poor little patches of back garden only amounted to 460,000 acres, and the thought of having to pay a halfpenny in the £ on the value of his undeveloped land had broken his heart. (Laughter). The last straw had broken the camel's back. Continuing, the speaker said that on studying the Budget they were bound to feel that the Chancellor of the Exchequer meant business, and their presence at those meetings week by week in such great crowds proved that they meant business too. (Hear, hear). The Chancellor of the Exchequer had succeeded in pro- ducing a Budget which was something more than high and dry finance, because he (the speaker) could not think the workingmen would have gathered in crowds to be fed on what was generally considered the rather dry fare of facts and figures unless there was something in the Budget that touched the people's hearts and raised the people's hopes. (Applause). It was a very serious situation that the Chancellor of the Exchequer had to face. To devise ways and means of meeting a deficit of £16,000,000 might have been quite enough to ensure disaster for an ordinary man and to bring dis- credit and perhaps defeat on his Government. But in one and the same measure to meet that deficiency and to open up a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>FAR-REACHING VISTA OF SOCIAL REFORMS</head>
        <p>FAR-REACHING VISTA OF SOCIAL REFORMS</p>
        <p>and national development and to show that, without unduly and unfairly burdening any one class, the national resources were sufficient for the tasks that lay ahead, was an achievement of such splendid courage that in itself it was almost enough to ensure success. (Applause) This Budget had begun the business—and not a bit too soon—of trying to fit the burden of national taxation a little more fairly and equitably, according to the backs that could bear it. (Applause). He wanted them to feel that in the fight which was before them the issues were quite plain and clear. The nation had to decide whether the burden of taxation was to be placed upon our hard earned food or upon our unearned wealth. (Applause.) When the forces were drawn up in battle array,on the one hand there would be the lords and on the other hand labour, on the one hand the dukes and on the other the democracy. When they realised that on the one side the forces were those of selfishness, privilege and interest, and on the other side those invincible forces of truth, principle, conviction and right, they could not have a moment's doubt as to the result. (Applause). Mr. ROBERT LEWIS, who had a cordial reception, said someone had sent him a copy of a Warrington piper containing a letter from a Mr. Chrimes, calling him (the speaker) names. It was easy enough to call a person names but if Mr. Chrimes happened to be pre- sent that night he (the speaker) hoped that his courage would not fail him and that his modesty would not stand in the way, but that if he was desirous of asking a question he would put it at the meeting rather than write a letter to the paper after the speaker had gone away. (Hear, hear.) The Budget was very much alive just now, but he wanted them to realize the very serious positioninto which we were getting. They must not lose their heads. They needed a calm courage, a calm judgment, a resolute will, an unfailing determination. If the issue were put to the people, he wanted them to vote straightly and plainly and clearly. He was not anxious for a contest with the</p>
        <p>ROUSE OF LORDS. He was not anxious that we should have a condition of civil war for a time. (A Voice: "It won't last long.") No, but it always left some hurt behind it. He was not anxious to have it, but if the Peers had resolutely made up their minds that they were going to thwart the will of the people, then war must take place, for if it became a question between the Peers and the people, the people must rule and not the Peers. (Applause.) 'What were they going to fight about? First of all, the provision of old age pensions and the paying for them, and he was bound to say there was no issue to which he would I rather join himself in an appeal to the people than that, Nething had appealed to the people like that provision —the taking away of the greyness out of so many lives, the brightening of so many homes, the cheering of so many hearts—and the mass of the people had realized that in passing that Act the Government had done a wise and just thing. (Hear, hear.) In paying for the pensions they were only doing what they really ought to do. The workingmen were pre- pared to bear the tax on their tobacco and beer, and to bear it smiling. The tax they objected to pay was the protective tax of the brewer. They were not prepared to pay him Hs. 9d. in order that the Chancellor of the Exchequer might get threepence. All they were pre- pared to pay was the threepence, however it might be extracted, and they would smile in the "stumping up." When the same demand was made from the lords and the dukes, they turned round in a moment and began to whine. They said they could not afford. It seemed that in these days the wealth was passing out of the hands of the mass of the people into the hands of the few, and the few were not desirous of parting with it. We were right in demanding that they should pay a fair contribution to the national exchequer. If any man could point out to him where any one of the taxes in the Budget bore unduly upon any section of the com- munity, be was prepared to consider that case. But he was bound to say tbat so far as the Government were concerned, no point had been raised against their proposals that seemed in any way to be hard without receiving patient consideration and, so far as had been humanly possible, they had met every case. They were desirous that nobody should suffer hardship, but what they said was "You have got to pay," and in their I demand from the rich monopolists they had justice and fairness on their side. One would almost come to the conclusion that the Government were asking these men for their very skin. But the Government were not asking from them their own money. All they were ask- ing, so far as the land taxes were concerned, was that the people who had created the value should share in it. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Lewis instanced the case of the</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL and the enhanced value which its construction conferred upon land belonging to Lord Egerton of Tatton. He was not going to say a word against Lord Egerton. He</p>
        <p>knew his services to the Manchester Ship Canal were valuable beyond expression and helped the promoters in carrying to a successful issue that great and wonder- ful project. The company wanted certain land belong- ing to Lord Egerton for dock purposes, and his lord- ship was asked to name his price. His price for this land which was submerged and practically valueless for building. was £83,000. He actually received for 5,,,81 acres of land £63,000. What was it worth before? £25 or £3O an acres. The speaker claimed that it was industrial and commercial necessity which created tbe. enhanced value. lf the proposals cf the Budget MI been in operation,2l2,ooo would have passed from Lora Egerton into the national exchequer, and when the division came .26,000 would have been retained in the national exchequer and £6,000 would have gone to the people of Salford in reduction of their rates. (Applauseci" But the £63,000 was not all that Lord Egerton ha The moment that land was piocured for the docks, the value of every b:t of land around the docks went II also ; and the estate of Lord Egerton of Tatton wall" for many years yet t 3 come receive annually se enhanced price because of the project which somebodX else established and carried out. The people alone made the value, and the community had a right to to". on the value which they had created. Mr. Lewis nevi referred to the.</p>
        <p>PARK AT oRMsKIRK. Some years ago that park was p irt of a farm, and the rent received fram the land now used as a park vv" £.5 per year. Lord Derby made a present of that knd to the people of Ormskirk as a puolic park, on condition that they laid it out and kept it as a park. That erest done, and the roa3s were made and gas laid on. Wibtl' happened? For oue little house now reared on th° land surrounding that park Lord Derby every Year received double the sum that the ground which wan 01 a park brought him in. The giving of the land was a right, but the enormously enhanced value which .Wag,,, 0' attached to all the land around now went entirely 1," the pocket of Lord Derby. And yet because the peoll asked for some return from that which they theraselve were creating, they were called thieves, robbers, reale; tionists, and, worst of all, Socialists, as though wile, they had said that they could not say any (Laughter.) Under the Budget the people who Int;u the roads around the park and developed the land in t neighbourhood, would reap the reward of their induattie and would pocket for the community as a whole aen), of the profit which they had created. (Applause.) Then there was</p>
        <p>• SEFTON PARK, Liverpool,which was originally bringing in Lord Sera" £1,200 a year,but when the people of Liverpool regale, it for a park they had to pay him £250,000. (" Shame" Trafford Park at Manchester, which nobody wento before the Manchester Ship Canal came along and inej, the land valuable, was sold for £360,000, but those voli' had made the value had to go with Gut a penny. DeB ing with site values, Mr. Lewis quoted the eel of Lansdowne House, London. Its area was 8110 square feet and the public value put upon it was OPT:0 square foot. The site value, if it were to come into toe open market,was £650,000 and yet its assessed rates value was only £2,soo—("shame")-70. per sclln..Tb. foot. Just across the road there was the Bath CT Its area was 11,000 square feet and the estimated ca 'no" was £6 per square foot. The site value was £66,0u ; but its assessed rateable value was £3,050,0r 55. 6013,,ef square foot. Cardiff Castle, which bad an area., 508,000 square feet, was rated at £947, but de t. Howell's tailor's shop, which stood on 400 square fet; was rated at £974. ("Shame"). And the man w" got off by paying £917 for 508,000 square feet was the sort of person who said this Budget was an iniquitie; it was Socialistic. The moment they began to inqulw; into it, they came to the conclusion that those men vf were greatly concerned for the poor worktn, population of the country were only concerned ig; them when they were asked to pay for the decrel?l; workers who were forced out of the industrial strife We the low wages so many of them had when they Ile young. The Conservatives thought they would be to gull, beguile and fool the people with talk oboe; tariff reform. The masses of the people, however, Wel realizing that the Budget would mean for them a</p>
        <p>REDUCTION OF THE RATES AND TAXES he': and of the burden that so long had been laid on t shoulders. It meant the solving of some of 0, problems that were pressing for solution, the lightenil of the toiler's lot, and the brightening of the toiler home. It took away the darkness which had so loaf. enshrouded him and it made him thankful that thee was a brighter day dawning for him. There was Ica panacea for our troubles in tariff reform; it would g. to them. Tariff reform would make the millioniTe richer and the monopolist cheekier. It would make trt unscrupulous financier immensely stronger, but „,", would mean that the masses of the people could not the food that they ought to have. It would hold thetwo in the chains of a slavery that it would be diffinulto shake off. The conditions to-day were not all we OW" d like them to be, but they were infinitely better Id more hopeful than any conditions tariff reform el:et bring about. In conclusion Mr. Lewis said the Btlub meant something more that that everybody had to P,11.; their share. It was a step in the direction of the Wit tion of the terrible question of unemploymeot'llo ..g t meant the brightening of many a man's life by 010 him to insuie against unemployment. It meantbg there would be some opportunity of developing our Aid land, and it meant a brighter, happier, and more spleritich Britain than ever had been in the history of the %Or (Applause). eng, Mr. J. HOWES said that was a magnificent mee-I and the magnificent speech they had listened to •rof worthy of the occasion. They were in the naiddlleaed the fight. The forces of democrocy were we' 9. together, and there were more labourers than duketo (Laughter.) Therefore they need not be uneagY tat the result of the conflict. In a short time the /30"„bn,-, would leave the Commons and go to that place villerrmiie very few Liberal travellers returned. (Laughter.) .`,ot lords might do as they liked, but the people woilla -be desert the Chancellor. (Applause). So long as ,be fought for the cause of the people, he would have a people with him. Next Monday they were to hallia. visit from a great champion of land reform—gr. , 0 (Applause). They expected to see the Parr 11;or crowded, for no man had done more heroic virork et land reform than that great Scotsman. The Budged was great in itself but even the Budget was eeliPsot by the great Development Bill which the Governe had introduced. If that measure passed, there ner far no-one out of work. We should have employn3en'' „rs. all who cared to work for at least the next three ye- a. When the local tariff reformers commenced their °Peed air campaign the Labour League also inauguras',,,tag meetings and said they would be on the ground as i„r9 as their opponents were. The tariff refornived surrendered last week. They had annoilecnt, that their campaign was over for the Preset-be So, having driven the tariff reformers from field, the Labour League closed their opea,.dy campaign that night. When their opponents were re'fe, to commence another campaign, they would be the'of (Applause). With regard to the representation of Warrington, it wag not merely a question with hiretbe Mr. Crosfield or anybody else. It was a question of of champion of democracy being sent to the 11011..eetbe Commons to defend democratic principles. It we° jot question of the common-sense representative azgiel,' to the brewer or any other trade that might wan," frle ' trample upon the people's rights. Whether the li(1 threw out the Finance Bill or not, the people Sill.;'o, have the Bill, the whole Bill, and nothing but the They would have no tax on food, but a tax on ace? la ated wealth instead. (Applause). Mr. Howes collo:111%ff. by moving a vote of thanks to the Chairman anu - Lewis. Mr. ENGLAND, in seconding, said he was persuadgi in .be his own mind that the Lords would throw out t'do. Budget. Then the people• would throw out the fler „0 (Applause). There was nothing else for it. .alia-g elective auditor for Warrington, and knowing sornew„ld about the rates of the town, he said the Budget vi10"...0 relieve the rates of Warrington to a considerable degols and that meant that eventually the re re ought to go down. Every householder should thereffet support the Budget. He would go further and say °ore this Budget would in Warrington as elsewhere find °the employment for the people, because one-half of ii,e9 fund received by the Government from land "TO would go to the local exchequers and it would 00,50 Corporations to improve and widen the roads, establish better conditions for the people in the l'')",be This was the best Budget that ever appeared ia (TO history of this country and it opened up a Wise so vision. The national development fund meant that .00, end could be put to the unemployment questniald Tariff Reform was not only unnecessary but it make things worse. The vote was heartily carried and the meeting,.foC chided with enthusiastic cheers for the Budget 80" Mr. Lloyd-George.</p>
        <p>II PLANTAINS IN LAWN.—There is no One who OWOS der lawn who is not troubled by plantains. A lady reti of of "Gardening Illustrated "claims that a few dragoon paraffin poured into the centre of each plant Will sci it, make them wither up and disappear. She has trie red and with great success on her lawn, which was co." 95 with them. The oil-can of a mowing-machine 10 excellent waY of putting on the paraffin.</p>
