      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>Preston Brook. litt• A. BROO3IE'S SHOW StiCCESID.—Mr. A. Broome, Itsl the Windmill Farm, secured the following prizes at e Koutsford Show on Saturday with his typical four- leer-old shire filly Windmill Duchess. For prizes ?Lifered by the Tarporley Hunt .Steeplechase fund for ' best cart mare, 2nd prize; for the best mare or ,„l'eljling for draught purposes (open class), 3rd prize. In W'ztion to these she mon the silver medal given by the hire Horse Society for the tess shire mare,filly or filly '4l exhibited at the show. • Croft. • 6eust,"T HORTICULTLMAL SHOW.—This show was in the Croft Day Schools on Saturday last. The Ow was opened by Captain Withington, Culcheth tha,ll' A good collection of flowers and vegetables was ilt-wll. Owing to the inclemency of the weather the ost,eadant.e was not as good cs other years. One of the illef features of the show is the Onion exhibition, and teurch enthusiasm is shown in this particular class, but o,inff to the sunless summer the onions were not as —ge as other Tears. Mr. J. Sanderson took the Ist,. rize With a onion weighing 15;ozs. Mr. J. Mains fel- p°,riag closely with oce weighing Thiozsmhich took 2nd e..,,51e• Another interesting feature was the children's k'utbition of window plants and wild flowers. After he'll competition Sidney Smith• carried off the Ist 04+ with a splendid Fuchia in the window class ; and 14 the wild flowers Hilda Dodds Ist girls and 3. Taylor boYs. A very interesting hour or two was spent 2c.amining the beautifnl blooms in which much labour "mct time had been spent.</p>
        <p>Culchcth. . tvnt. ~PEN"DENT METHODIST CHAPEL—The usual AfAiltnl3' P.S.A. was held on Sunday. afternoon last. Mr. mred, of Kenyon, occupying the cnair in the absence ,tveirton, Qvie iperintendent. Solos were very ably sung by 0 s Nellie Runcorn and Messrs. J. R. Fletcher end H. a speech being given to the children Mr. R. 1:21,O,1. A collection was taken at the close of the "v'ing in aid of the missionary fund. I.oe t 6 „AL WEDDING.—The wedding was solemnized at 11 9wchurch Parish Church on Saturday last of Mr. I,,lTortoo Charnock, of "Home Cot" Culcheth, and ;;4LBs Eliza Dawson of the same address. The bride who e..3,given away by Mr. R. Hodgkinson, of Boundary ges, Croft, was attired in a fawn costume with pi rim cture hat to match and wore a beautiful diamond bi": 1 gold brooch the gift of the bridegroom. The voluesmaid was Miss 31. A. Howard, of Culcheth, 'alto heir° a cream dress with hat to match, her ornament gold brooch set with rubies the gift of the tiliedegroom. Mr. R. Hodgkinson acted as best man and Afiating minister was the Rev. E. %V. W. Kaye. 1-I._et partaking of the wedding breakfast Mt. and Mrs. qarnock left for Llandudno where the hone) moon is ng Vent.</p>
        <p>Daresbury. eollirliE RANGE Comerarnoxs.—The Rifle Range nreanlittee held a meeting at the Oaklands on Monday, tha tl the Secrets*. (Mr. Shepherd-Cross) announced preset Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., had very generously eted the club with a silver challenge cup to be theilallY shot. for by the members. It was decided that etiy, enP be called" Sir Gilbert Greenall challenge and that the winner this year be the marksman Makes the highest average at all three ranges $.119g August and September. Mr. Sloane, of the Tjil'e'!es, Moore, has given another silver challenge cup. ▪ to be named "The Townships Challenge Cup" Will be competed for in October by teams of four the!' from each of the following townships within CZ.,9lub's area :—Daresbury, Preston Brook, Newton- -41-kresbury, Moore, Hatton, Keckwick and Norton, Grange and Higher Walton. The competition kah'eTear for the cup is to tat_e place on the 100 yards "ze, seven abots each. eir. • it-4PM ,BURGLArtY.—On Thursday afternoon, Ab'' dames Antrobus had an adventure with a burglar. teilant half-pest ens she left the house to go to a ghbour's but was only away two or three minutes; hetr.eturning she was rather surprised to see a rubber- wo Mark on her gate step, this naturally led her to 4.pder who had been. However, she entered the house 4a thought she heard a slight noise, which prompted tlitt to go upstairs. When almost at the top she saw a 0„,13:8 sleeve disappear behind the bedroom door; with- -4,Tpesltat ion Mrs. Antrobus entered the room, the man an attempt to get past her but she shut the door her back against it ; she then made a grab at the eLI and secured him by the throat and forced him into th.air Lear the window. With the other band she eZtted the window and called for help, one of the Dar hours was attracted 11 her call and sent for the Piel.e.aman who lives quite ne.-Ir. Fortunately P.C. th„".on was at home at the time and was very soon on 8.- obi .ene. On being searched it was found that the 4'Y Property which the men had taken was two pocket 444 ere b iefs. The man was taken to Stockton Heath pp° locked up, but for Mrs. Antrobus's courageous and %I rant action he would have got away and had he had eitnther five minutes in the hcuse alone he might have lead away with a considerable sum of money which "•Antrobus had in another room.</p>
        <p>Penketh. A PENKETEI PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. 1,4„ tsPecial meeting of the Pcnketh Parish Council ov: °aid in the Council School cn Monday evening, ,Ahich Dr. Murray presided. Others present were: 111484. J. G. Withinehaw, J. Woodward, D. Houghton, leiContledge and E. Fcster (clerk).—The Clerk read a rekr from the General Post Office, Liverpool which titia.at follows :—Sir,—lt is proposed to postpone the -nt Opening of the Penketh Sub-post Office from egi2. to 8 a.m.. the hours which are now usual for post 1710 this class and also to close the office from affot!:,ra. to 7 p.m. on Thursday in order to a half-holiday to tbe sub-post master. tor ',a, Iteration will not affect the existing arrangements, tei.Loe delivery collection or despatch of letters and flgkraras for Penketh received on Thursday afternoon • e delivered from the Sankey Bridges sub-pcst shall be glad to learn at your earliest command qther your Council agree to the arrangement." • `t. Withinshaw moved that the letter be agreed to tiZo.nflitions that the delivery of letters belonging to tlohulfferent works were not interfered with—The resolti- WI, was carried.—Mr. Routledge move d that the Clerk leftla to the Surveyor of the G.P.O. with regard to the Ist„vr-box being too small and asking them to supply a to °et one.--This was agreed to.—Mr. J. Woodward aiina'Plaiced of child' en under five years of age not being %red to attend school. There was plenty of accom- the ation in the new schools.—After some discussion Matter was dropped. Padgate. A 1.4,, qq,„GATE WESLEYAN CHAPEL.—The usual monthly A6.11011'11 meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on tlie7BY evening last, the Rev. T. Gladding occupying 4 nalair. A solo entitled "Was there ever kindest qatl;herd " was very beautifully rendered by Mkt; Dr."„tlY, after which a very enjoyable paper was given by aike'ennett entitled "The History of Famous Hymns late the Reformation." During the evening a very tt, resting letter was read by Miss Jessie Ward from 11, Quad friends in the West Indies, giving a descrip- -114; of the successful Guild work over there. The nth."ng Was not so well attended as usual owing to SpeZ,r attractions in the town, but those who attended s". a very enjoyable time together. " TENSES CLUB GARDEN PARTY.—Saturday lea t a red letter day in the annals of the P.L.T.C. this, NA,Ig the first occasion on which an appeal has been %Tr beyond the limits of its members for financial Irk:lance, to which end a well arranged Garden party neld in the extensive and beautiful garden at thifehe Heath by kind permission of Mrs. Bennett. Of v.ortunately alike for the club funds and the pleasure kft4ue Visitors, the weather broke down immediately )11,it the opening ceremony which prevented the teutctra seeing the well arranged flower beds at their the midst of which are a well laid bowling • croquet lawn and clock golf green—at their best. tln;l9 or.ening cesemony took place on the verandah of ot uoNviing green pavilion. Mr. Elijah Lunt (president rlittelli• ae club) presiding, and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins arged the duty of declaring the fete open. Mr.Limt au interesting account of the club from its forma- ! Wintue twelve years ago when it was commenced as Apid:ale37 Guild club at a very low subscription. Itic.cations for membership were so numerous thatit 411. decided some three years later to work it on ilnektrY club lines and although the subscription was kgy ,raised, yet by careful management it been possible to keep out of debt.— rikilne to refuse further it on account of ko, nticn of accommodation, t has been decided to dilucu.ase two new pavilions for ladies' and gentlemen's !tiviV,nB-rooms, and to convert the whole of the present gov,:ao into one large club room and if possible to lay atip kg 'tore courts, and the garden party was decided on ellos of raising the necessary money. Mr. Jenkins, Ni.,.BPecch full of interesting points, referred to the • eut love of sport in the British and compared tbe laid 'tons and cruel sports of the old da3s, such as bull tkAti hear-baiting, cock-fighting, &amp;c., with the more ntt_te and chivalrous sports of to-day. He also spoke e /narked effect which the great religious revivals artt e‘xerted in helping to suppress that love of the alevh :14',Ithich made sport rather a degradation than an L'en-`;ng influence such as we now found it.—Dr. t. (vice-president), in proposing a hearty vote of Agit, a to Mr. Jenkins who, be said, had been one of the tile °nil of the club since its formation, stated he was taeftiginator of the club and felt very proud of its ‘ta tnned success. He had seen it pass through all the Asa Leo of swaddling clothes, shorts, and now to the long stage of full-grown manhood, and said the club ‘B4otredividuals was filled with ambition, anxious to nnd improve itself. He also spoke of the benefit was accruing to the nation through .our • teaching people the benefit of open rur.— t'Orrfea* S. Page seconded and and the proposition was ;•itight`t,with acclamation.—On the motion of Mi. E. „`attnioao, seconded by Mr. J. H. Mason, a hearty vote of eonbri was proposed to Mrs. Bennett for the use of the 'a, and to Mr. H. S. Page for the use of his 4 Court and dining-room, which latter was utilized</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 11, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 11, 1909.</p>
        <p>for a succession of admirable concerts, the artistes being Miss Pakes and Miss Corney (the recitations of both being greatly appreciated), solos by Misses Davey, Gandy and Messrs. E. Whitehouse, W. J. Jenkins, and Dr. Bennett, whilst the Misses England and Mrs. Haviour contributed instrumental music. The exoellent programme of sports consisting of tennis, bowls, croquet, clock &amp;elf, and shooting competitions, was much interfered with by the rain. Mr. Nicholson won the clock golf prize, whilst Mrs. Newton won the advertisement competition. The art gallery and inside shows were well patronized and in spire of the rain an enjoyable afternoon Was spent and a sum of money exceeding expectation was raised, namely 05.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : And then the chairin2n of the committee proposes that he should be the engineer. Councillor ROBERTS : It is a perfectly proper letter for the man to have written.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : Certainly I quite agree. Councill‘r PEItiBERTON : I say it is a most improper thing for a man whose firm is going to contract to make the bridge for the people who work under his firm's licfnce,find not only that but he is actually proposed by the chairman to be the engineer. Councillor ROGERS: That is not his fault. He did not ask to be appointed and there is nothing in the letter which conveys that impression.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMB-ERTON, continuing, said some people were pulling for a certain system and they knew why. What the Corporation had to do was the best they could for the ratepayers. They had to get the best bridge in the most economical manner. Let them pass the committee's recommendations and get the bridge going. Members of the Council got up and said they wished to see the scheme carried through, but there was always some reason for putting it back.</p>
        <p>Alaerman BExisErr said he bird often heard Councillor Pemberton wax exceedingly eloquent and get very excited on trivial pretexts, but that night he had excelled himself. Be seemed to live in an atmosphere of suspicion and to get his popularity by flinging broad- cast veiled innuendoes which were exceedingly Lnjust to members of the Council and which in this instance were witbsut a scintilla of foundation. He almost raved and foamed at the mouth agaipst these shadows. wy don't know-the cause of his violent outburst" con- tinued the alderman. "If he is aiming at me, I fling his accusations back in his face and say I have acted with.straightforwardness and a single desire for the best interests of the town." Proceeding, Alderman Bennett said it was easy to tear passion to tatters, to wax wildly eloquent and ferociously insinuatory, but it did not help the case at all. This sudden affectation of almost unreasonable zeal to proceed , with the matter, did not seem to come well from Councillor Pemberton, know- ing how he had been one of the principal persons who had caused the delay. He believed CouncillorPember- ton was sincere, but his sincerity often took a per- verted course. He (the speaker) had a great respect for the Borough Surveyor but he thought the committee proposEd to put work upon his shoulders for which he was not intended,and which was not within his duties. If they adopted a higgledy-piggledy method of , procedure, if they let 'em all come" and bring their plans just as they liked, without &amp;finite specifica- tions or design, they would simply have chaos, and it would tax the ability of the Lest and most able assessor to say which was the best and most suitable plan. If they appointed a definite engineer from the start, he would accept the responsibility, and the whole reputation of the bridge and the success of the scheme would rest upon his shoulders. Knowing what slaves to cheapness some members of the Council were he was afraid that if a batch of schemes was submitted they would chose one which was not the best, simply because it happened to be the cheapest. The principle of appointing an independent engineer was decided upon- with unanimity, and that resolution had never been rescinded. They also came to the conclusion that one gentleman (Mr. Webster) was eminently suitable and it became merely a question of terms. However, the committee had not the courtesy to give Mr. Webster the opportunity of coming before them to explain the matter, but they rudely dis- solved the sub-committee and threw the whole thing into the melting pot again. Councillor PESIBERTON : The terms were not satis- factory.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : You never gave him the oppor- tunity of discussing them. Continuing, he said that to prove his good faith in the matter and to wipe out any possibility of feeling, Mr. Webster offered to take as part payment the £4OO or £5OO he had already had for work done in accordance with the agreement with the Corporation some years ago. £1.1,000 was the figure recently quoted as the probable cost of tbe bridge. Five per cent would be £7OO and 21 per cent. £250, so that Mr. Webster's total fees would come to £1,050 of which he had promised 'to waive over £4OO. He (Alderman Bennett) had heard vague whispers that he bad some interest in advocating the appointment of Mr. Webster. If he had been getting paid for the job, he could not have heard more unpleasant whispers. He flung that back in the face of anyone who had dared to makONthe suggestion. He had no interest in the matter except to get the best; job it was possible to get. He had advocated Mr. Webster because he had never heard the tame mentioned of another engineer of greater eminence and ability and of practical experience in bridge building. There was no other engineer with a greater knowledge of our river and its conditions. He (Alderman Bennett) was proud to say again that every- thing else being equal, he always leaned to his own townsman in making such an appointmcnt. The voting was as follows : For Councillor ROBERTS'S amendment: Alderman Bennett, Councillors Wilson, Sankey, Vernon, Toole, Hawthorn, Robertt3, Birtles and Craik and the Mayor- -10.</p>
        <p>Against :—Alderman Bolton. Councillors Wilkinson, Halliwell, Skelland, Ashcroft, Lord, Pemberton, Locker and Cunmugton-9. The amendment was therefore carried. ANOTHER COUNCIL MEETING. TENDERS TO BE INVITED.</p>
        <p>. The Bridges Committee again met on Thursday even- ing to receivelhe report of the Borough Surveyor. A long discussion tJok place and at the closes special Council meeting was held to confirm the minutes of the committee. Alderman Smethurst (chairman of the Bridges Committee) presided and there were also present at the Council meeting :7-Alderman Bolton, Councillors Hawthorn, Lash ford, Cunniugton, Pember- ton, Lord, Chantler, Vernon, Sankey, Wilkinson, Skelland and Locker.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON proposed: That the report of the Borough Surveyor be taken as read. That the report be adopted and tenders invited in accordance with it.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON seconded and Alderman BOLTON supported. Councillor VERNON said he thought the Council were acting on wrong lines. They all wanted a good bridge —not one which at some future date would cause them a lot of further expense. They ought from the first to employ the most eminent engineer. He bad no feeling with regard to the selection of Mr. Webster; he had never mentioned his name. They ought, however, to have the most eminent engineer they could get in a matter which they were told was going to cost £20,000 or £30,000. The sum that would be entailed by the appointment of an engineer was paltry in conpanson with the amount that was going to be spent on the scheme.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said that what they had to do was to get the best possible bridge. They were not going to be tied to one man. They wanted a series of plans sending in and then they could get an expert to adjudi- cate as to which was the best. If they had any doubt about it,they co_uld appoint an engineer to see if there was any weak spot before proceeding any further. The course now recommended by the committee was the right way of proceeding, unless they wanted to tie themselves to one particular design or to one man's idea. Councillor WILKINSON said-the matter was one in which there was practically no difference if they would only look at it in a proper business-like way. The gentlemen who would have oppoeed the resolution that night were conspicuous by their absence. Why, he could not say, but it was very deplorable to him as a Warrington man and one who wished as much as the Chair:nen or other members to have a bridge that would be a credit to to the town, to note that the members who so strenuously opposed the committee's proceedings on Tuesday were not present now to defend their action. There was nothing in the ideas of the gentlemen present that night and those who were not there, that would make one atom of difference in the construction of the bridge. The only difference was that one man said : •" We want an engineer to draw up the plans and get out the speci- fications," whereas others said "We c et do that ourselves through the contractors who will contract for the bridge. At a given point we will call in an expert to tell us which of the systems is the best." He was con- vinced they were moving on sound business lines and no doubt they would be prepared to pay an expert handsomely for his work when they called him in. Councillor LORD said they were proceeding on safer lined than they would be if they followed the advice of Councillor Vernon. They were ,getting the brains of the most eminent ferro-concrete engineers in the country, whereas if they went on tbe lines that Coun- cillor-Vernon advocated they would be dependent on one man. If that man made a mistake, where would they he? Councillor HAWTHORN said he thought it would have been better to have had a comeetent engineer from the beginning, but he could see that that course would throw the matter into the melting pot again and defer it for a long time, if they were to begin de nom. It was only the strong feeling he had that they should push on with the work, and the understanding that they wouid have an expert engineer, to adjudicate on the plans, that caused him to waive his objection. He hoped the matter would be pushed forward strenuously. Councillor PEMBERTON said they were trying to get the, best bridge they could for Warrington without being bled. Some people wanted to throw £l,OOO away on a man who hid never built a ferro-concrete bridge. Now they would get the best men in the country—men who bad built bridge after bridge, if not in this country, on the Continent. The resolution would leave it open to the firms tendering to introduce any system or com- bination of systems. There was an open field for every- body. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried, only two voting against it. The CHAIRMAN said he was sorry more members were not prc-sent. However, the Council had taken a step foriVard. He apologised for the absence of the Mayor, who was detained by business. The Council futther decided that tenders must be sent in within three months.</p>
        <p>Tenders will accordingly be invited for a 134 feet single span ferro-concrete bridge, 70ft. or 80ft. wide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>A DANGEROUS CORNER.—A serious accident was narrowly averted on FadAy afternoon of last week at the corner of New-road. Mr. and MI s. H. Pearson, Kingsbury, Lymm, were going out in their motor, and on the way down to the village they came unexpectedly on a lurry and horse at this corner. The tar collided with the lurry shaft and pushed both horse and lorry towards Eagle Brow. Fortunately the horse was quiet, and the. only damage done was to the splash board of the motor, which was torn off.</p>
        <p>CHORAL SOCIETY.— This Society commences its fifth session on Tuesday the 14th inst. Its career up to the present has been very gratifying and there is every reason .for looking forward to an increased prosperity. Each year the society has grown in membership and competent critics aver that it has also materially pro- gressed in musical ability. It is intended to produce Grant's "Holy City "in Lymm Parish Church on the afternoon of Sunday, November lat, at which a collection will be taken in aid of the "District Nurses Fund." The hon. secretary, Mr. F. P. Brown, will be pleased to receive applicltions for membership from any lady or gentleman possessing some musical qualification. BUDGET UNEASINESS AT LTATAI.—A meeting in opposition to theßudget was advertised for Wednesday evening in Newfleld,but did not take place there, Coun- cillor Groves objecting,so they pitched their stand at tbe top of Booth's Hill. The meeting was under the auspices Cheshire County Unicnist Club, the speakers being Mr. Herbert Carey and Mr. James Reid. Neither gentlemen seemed to have reckoned on the Workingman's know- ledge of Budget questions at Lymm, for they were quite at a loss to answer some of the questions put to them. The meeting was lively from beginning to end and did a good turn to the Budget, rather than retard its progress. Although every member of the adjoining Conservative Club was present it was a complete failure to convince the siorkingmen of Lymm that the Budget is not popular.—At the close Mr. Mines called upon Mr. Cowper to propose a vote of thanks to the sFeakers and the" show of hands "in favotir of the meeting was two sticks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>STOOD BEFORE MIRROR TO CUT HIS THROAT.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Earlestown, on Monday after- noon, on the body of Edmund Atherton, aged sixty-two, of Green-lane, Higher Ince, who was found dead with his throat cut. It appeared that deceased was formerly employed as a fireman at the Maypole Colliery. The disaster had had such an effect on him that he had been unable to stork since. He had lceen very depressed and had suffered from sleeplessness. From the evidence Le had evidently stood Lefore a mirror to destroy himself. A verdict of ',Suicide whilst of unsound mind" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>BUTCHER'S SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>A BUTCHER'S SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse conducted an Inquest at Fades. town, on Monday afternoon, into .the circumstances surrounding the death of Harry Waster, aged thirty- three, a butcher. of Wargrave-road, 'Earlestown. Deceased formerly had a butcher's business at Birkdale for over seven years, and he came to Earlestown six weeks ago. Mary Webster, widow of the deceased, said he had recently been very despondent. On Friday morning about half past-nine deceased went to the rear of the butcher's shop, but it was not until half-past twelve that his body was found in the cellar, his throat being Jerribly gashed. A verdict of" Suicide whilst of unsound mind' was returned. It was stated in evidence that deceasbd's father committed suicide over twenty years ago by drowning himself.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CANAL TRAGEDY. POLICE COURT SEQUEL. •</p>
        <p>Herbert Cook, of Station-road, Haydock, and his wife Edith were summoned at Newton-le-Willows Petty Sessions on Thursday on a charge of ill-treating a child. Only the man appeared.—Mr. E. W. Swift prosecuted for the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. The case arose out of what transpired at an inquest at Earlestown on August 25 on the body of *Elijah Perkins (9), son of Mrs. Coo 4 by a former husband, when the man and woman were severely censured by the coroner and jury. It was stated that on august 18th the lad stole 2d. out of his mother's pocket, and when she saw him at night taxed him with it. He admitted that he had bought a 2d. ball. She gave him something to eat, and said, "Clear out, you little wretch. I hope I will never see your face again." The lad was never seen again, but his body was afterwards found in the canal at a place where his mother had told the police she thought he would be.—Mr. Swift said that as this was a joint charge he would have to apply fer a warrant for the woman's arrest.—Cook raid his wife was ill in Led.— Mr. Swift said he was told that the police who had charge of the case when the lad drowned himself did not believe the story of the woman's illness. The best plan was to have a warrant, and if they found she really was ill they would not execute it immediately.—The bench granted the warrant, and adjourned the case against the man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>PAID-IN MILESTONES.</p>
        <p>At St. Helena Police Court yesterday morning, William Johnson, whose residence is a travelling caravan, failed to answer a summons for allowing his horses to stray in toan. A police constable said that shen he told Johnson he would be reported, defendsnt replied: "I will pay you in milestones; that is bow us blokes always pay." Defendant had since cleared out of the town. They always do that," remarked the Chief Constable. A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ALLEGED ILLEGAL OPERATION.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED ILLEGAL OPERATION.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS WOMAN FURTHER REMANDED. At Liverpool Police Court, on Tuesday, before the Stipendiary, Mr. T. Shepherd Little, a married woman, of respectable appsarance, named Annie Houghton, was charged on remaud with performing an illegal operation on a married woman named Caroline Daramann.—Chief Detective-Inspector Holbrook, in applying for a further remand, said that the complainant was still confined to the hospital. It had been necessary to take her deposi- tions, but during the week a more favourable report had been received as to her condition. Should she have ix relapse, however, it might be a serious matter. Corn- glainant was unable to leave the hospital.—Mr. E. W. Swift, of St. Helens, applied for bail.—The Stipendiary: No, not on these depesitions ; it is too serious a matter.—The prisoner, who lives in St. Helens, was remanded for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>YIELDED FO TEMPTATION.</head>
        <p>YIELDED FO TEMPTATION.</p>
        <p>Ernest Ormson, a young man who had been arrested at Garston, was charged at St. Helens on Tuesday with breaking into the dwelling-house 37, Ecclasten- str eet, and stealing £4 125.,a razor,and 6oz. of tobacco. Chief Inspector Goodall said that about the middle of Novanber last prisoner was taken to the house of John Kelly, in Eccleston-street, by 01313 of the sons. He told a pitiable story of having been turned out by his father. Mrs. Kelly got her husband's permission for the youth to sleep on the sofa. She fed him, and be stayed there nine days. He was in the house when the lodgers paid Mrs. Kelly for their week's bdard. She put the money in the pocket of her skirt. Eariy next morning the dress was missed from the bedroom, and was afterwards found downstairs under the sofa. The pocket was cut out, and the prisoner had gone along with the contents. He aas arrested in Garslon on Monday, and when charged by Police-constable Whalley said, "I am guilty; but if she won't go hard against me I am willing to pay her back. It was a temptation on my father turning me out. —Orison was remanded until next week.</p>
        <p>REV. J. HIRST HOLLOWELL'S SON MARRIED' A large gathering at the Milton Congregational Church, Rochdale, on Thursday witnessed the marriage of Mr. J. Hirst Hollowell, jun., of Manchester and Rtchdale, only son of the Rev. .J. Hirst Hollowell, secretary of the Northern Counties Education League, to Miss Ethel Mary Scott, only daughter of Mr. Henry Scott, Brentwood, Rochdale. The bride was given away by her father. Mr. E. S. Hoyle, of Chisley Hebden Bridge, acted as best man. The honeymoon is being spent in London and Cornwall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CHESHIRE TERRITORIAL'S ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE TERRITORIAL'S ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>. It was on Thursday night found necessary to amputate the right leg of Corporal Harold Les, assistant clerk of the Northwich Urban Council who was accidently shot by a comrade on Altcar shooting range last week. Corporal Lea, who is in the Northern Hospital, Liver- pool, was seated on an ammunition box when a cart- ridge jammed in the rifle of Private Jones, of Hartford. After the rifle had been discharged it was found that the bullet had taken off the end of Corporal Lea's index finger, passed through his thigh, and struck the ear of Private Alfred Hughes. Corporal Lea was the only one rcally seriously injured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>COONCILLORS PEMBERTON AND ROBERTS DIFFER.</p>
        <p>Lively Discussion.</p>
        <p>TENDERS TO BE INVITED.</p>
        <p>The much-vexed question of the erection of anew Wairington Bridge formed the subject of another lively discussion at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. The General Purposes Committee bad resolved that Mr. Alderman Smethurst be appointed chairman of this committee when sitting as a Bridges Committee; that the Borough Surveyor prepare a statement and descrip- tion of the proposed new Warrington Bridge, giving the size, width, and other details, and that specifications, designs, and tenders be obtained from firms experienced in ferro-concrete work; that the proposed tenders shall be for a complete bridge, and shall inalude:—(l) The, removal of the old bridge and its foundations. (2) The constructions of new foundations and abutments. (3) The construction of proposed new training walls, the alteration of the tram rails, gas, water, electric and other mains, and the provision of new mains, and to provide for the maintenance of the bridge for 12 months after completion; that the Borough Surveyor be authorized to consult the Board of Trade with respect to test loads of the bridge; that a special meeting of this committee be held on Thursday, the 9th September, in the evening, for the purpose of receiving a report of the Borough Surveyor as to the conditions of tender for Warrington Bridge end that a special -Council meeting be held immedialely afterwards for the purpose of confirming the minutes of the meeting. The DEPUTY MAYOR proposed and COUneillorßAßToN seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor ROBERTS said it was MU considerable reluctance that he had remained that night for the pur- pose of moving an amendment owing to a feeling that it his amendment should be successful it might possibly delay a matter which already bad been delayed far too long, and in regard to which, he did not think he was at all exaggerating when he said the public of Warrifigton had become extremely impatient. He was so convinced that the resolution instructing the Borough Surveyor to prepare a statement and description of the proposed new bridge ere., was so entirely the wrong method of proceeding with this important woik,that it it was only for the purpose of placing upon record his own opinion of the unbusitiesslike proceduie the resolution sanctioned, he felt bound to move an amendment that the recommendation be referred hack. The matter was discussed at the meeting of the General Purposes Com- mittee, and ho moved an amendment that a competent engineer should be appointed to design the bridge and draw up specifications after which the Council should proceed to obtain tenders for the work in question. The minute which appeared on the agenda was moved by Councillor Pemberton, who gave three reasons in support of his action. In the first place' he said no English engineer was suflicieetly acquainted with ferro- concrete work to be entrusted with such an important matter. Secondly, he said, if such an engineer could be found, the expenses in regard to the designing of the bridge would be practically doubled, because the various firms who had a monopoly of certain patents in regard to ferro concrete, employed their own engineers, and those engineers would have to be paid in addition to the engineer appointed by the Council. His third objection was.that they could only adapt into that particular bridge, one particular method of ferro- concrete stork, and they could not adapt into it the best methods of the different systems of ferro-concrete. He (Councillor Ro)serts) assured the committee that he could refute those statements, and he would do so that night. The first objection was that no English engineer of sufficient eminence could. he found who had had enough excerience of ferro-concrete to be justified in being appointed by that Council. That was a very gross and serious slander upon an important profession, and was absolutely untrue. He could quote two cases of concerns with which he was financially associated in which in each instance an English engineer designed and carried through an important piece of ferro- concrete work. In both cases there was only cne pro- fessional charge made and that was made by the English engineer who designed and carried through the work, and the companies simply paid royalties to the patentees for the use of their system. The statement that the employment of an engineer would entail double fees had therefore no foundation in fact. The engineer would adapt into his Scheme all or part of any systems of ferro-coacrete he might feel disposed to adopt and the various companies to which those system belonged would simply be paid their royalties. In one of the firms with which he was connected the work was carried out by Mr. Surtees, the engineer of the Newton Urban District Council, and in the other it was carried through by their own engineer. Councillor -Roberts quoted from advertisements in the "Local Government Officer and Contractor" for re-enforced concrete build- ings and in both those advertisements it was stated "Any system or systems of construction may be adopted for the whole or any part of the work." It was also stated that the gentlemen to whom application for specifications Bcc, must be made were English engineers, employed by the Board of Works, to carry out these undertakings. This branch of construction, he main- tained, had become part of the work of every recognized engineer and architect of eminence. The strongest point in support of the appointment of an engineer was that if he drew up the designs and specifications and got out the quantities, they could invite as many people as they liked to tender, and every one of them would be tendering on precisely the same conditions. If they adopted any other method, they introduced a considerable amount of difficulty unneces- sarily and they did not fix the responsibility on any one Individual. He very much doubted whether in the long run-they would get as economical a job as they would by engagiug their own engineer. Alderman BENNETT seconded the amendment.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON advised the Council to wait until they bad_ beard the Borough Surveyor's report at Thursday's meeting, before taking action. Councillor BIRTLES asked for the production of a letter which bad been received from an eminent engineer in ferro-concrete. Alderman SNIETHURST read the letter which bad been written by the engineer to one of the ferro-concrete companies. It urged the importance of appointing an independent engineer, which would ensure the certainty of obtaining a structure exactly suited to their requii e- nients. In cases of other similar bridges and works, they had aluays acted in conjunction with an appointed engineer. Councillor BIRTLES asked tbe Council to go about their work in a systematic way by first of all appointing an engineer. In building a house they employed an architect, and in erecting a bridge they should have their own engineer. _ _ _</p>
        <p>CounciIIorWILKINSON said be hoped the Council would stand by the proceedings of the Bridges Corn.. mittee. He contended that the position of the Council was vastly different from that of the concerns instanced by Councillor Roberts. Their Borough Surveyor would draw up the general plans, and they would ask for tenders from the various ferro-concrete companies. They would not then be tied to one system. Councillor PEMBERTON said he never stated that there were no English engineers who could carry out ferro-concrete work. What he -did say was that there were no engineers in England who had ever carried out a ferro-concrete bridge in this country. Councillor ROBERTS: I. will give an 'emphatic contradiction to that. I could name an engineer. • The DEPUTY-MAYOR: I have three or four photo- graphs. Councillor PEMBERTON : There has never been a bridge of this magnitude carried out. It is a different thing to use different methods in works—one system for the warehouse and another for the boiler house, &amp;c.— but this is a bridge and it is very unlikely that different systems can be incorporated in the construction of a bridge. Continuing be said their Borough Surveyor was a practical architect and engineer and he had done any amount of ferro-concrete work. Ferro-concrete was not a new thing. No one was more desirous of forwarding the scheme than he (the speaker), but he would rather wait another twelve months than throw away £l,OOO of the ratepayer's money. There was no reason why the Borough Surveyor should not prepare the specifications and then the various ferro-concrete firms could compete. It was idle to say the appointment of an engineer would not entail double cost. The patentees of every system of ferro-concrete incorporated in their tenders their own engineers' fees. If the Council went the right way about it, they would get a cheap bridge, because all these firms were falling over each other to get the job, as no ferro-concrete bridge of this size had yet been constructed in this country, and it would be a standing advertisement. Because they wanted a bridge, they were not going to be pushed into extravagant expenditure. Referring to the letter which had been read by the Deputy Mayor, Councillor Pemberton contended Putt it was improper and scandalous that such a letter should have been written. He never knew of such a thing in his life, as that a firm which was going to tender should have their advice submitted to the Council. It savoured of self-interest. Councillor BIBTLES : As members of the Council we were not in possession of all the facts, and we were entitled to know them.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I say the letter ought not to have been read to any committee. • What resolution of any committee asked for it ? What right has any firm that is going to tender for the work to say what should be done? It is most improper. You get a gentleman like Councillor 13irtles coming here to bolster things up. Courcillor BIRTLES : I object to such language. I bolster nothing up. I speak straight. The MAYOR: You are quite right, Councillor Birtles. Councillor BIRTLES : It was a most insinuating remark to make.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You need not upset yonnelf. Councillor ROBERTS: Where does self-interest come in? I did not know anything about the letter before to-night. Where does self-interest come in when a man deliberately recommends the Council to appoint an independent engineer?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ROAD HOGS AND SKY PIGS!</head>
        <p>ROAD HOGS AND SKY PIGS!</p>
        <p>A Cheshire Rural District Councillor, protesting against the Government scheme to make special roads for motor traffic, said that we had got road hogs already, and next we should have to deal with sky pigs. Soon we should not be safe either on the ground or above it, and even now it was scarcely safe to go through the dcor into the street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>evon, I ornwall</p>
        <p>The mnt equAle climate in the United Kin.e.,tim. SPED A HAPPY, !MUTH 110LiDAY IN ENGLAND'S PLEASURE RESORT.</p>
