      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>A.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SAD WIDNES DROWNING FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at Widnes Police Station, on Tuesday, on the body of John Povilitis, a young Pole, aged eight years, who was drowned in the Sankey and St. Helens Canal on Satur- day last. Maggie Wassalowski stated that she was playing with Povilitis near the canal bank. He said he was going to have a swim. His cap blew into the water. In trying to recover it he fell into the water and was drowned. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidentally drowned."</p>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S ,ACT.-LICENSEE CHARGED.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Thursday, James Fishwick, licensee of the Waterloo Vaults, was charged with allowing a child under the age of fourteen years in the bar. Mr. Knowles defended. Inspector Bretherton said that on the 30th nit,. Police-Constable Rochford sass-Jane Owens come out of the vaults carrying a child and a small bottle containing whisky. He took the woman back to the hotel and asked the barman if he had served the woman with whisky. He admitted it. Thomas Kenyon, the barman, said that while serving, he did not notice any child.—The Bench dismissed the case, giving defen- dant the benefit of the doubt. They,however,expyessed the opinion that the police were justified in bringing the case to court. OBJECTED TO THE HOUSEKEEPER. SAD DOMESTIC STORY FROM WIDNES.</p>
        <p>_ At Runcorn Petty Sessions, before Mr. F. J. Norman and Mr. F. Wisnall, on Monday, William Grounds, described as an insurance agent, of Frederick-street, Widnes, was summoned under the Married Women's Aot, 1895, for deserting his wife. Mrs. Grounds was wheeled into the court in an invali'd chair. Mr. H. N. Linaker appeared for the wife, and Mr. Knowles for the defendant.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman: Before you start, cannot you st ttle it? Mr. Knowles said Mr. Linaker had told him that if defendant would get rid of his housekeeper he thought the wife would go back. He replied at once, " we will get rid of the housekeeper." Mr. Norman: It was the housekeeper that was the cause of the trouble, I suppose? Mr. Linaker said the parties had been married eighteen years, and there was one child, aged fourteen or fifteen. They lived comfortably until eight years ago. At that time Mrs. Grounds was suddenly stricken with a very bad form of rheumatism, and completely lost the use of her lower limbs. She could not walk nor help her. self in any way. They went to live at her mother's house, and stayed there three and a half years. The defendant's conduct with regard to other women was not what it should be, and Mrs. Grounds was extremely unhappy. Ha introduced a woman named Mrs. Price into the house, and she came regularly. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker added that on one occasion Mrs. Grounds told Mrs. Price what she thought in plain words, but that night defendant took Mrs. Price home, and did not return until two o'clock in the morning. Mrs. Price was living in a house by herself, the husband working away. After that Mrs. Price did not come to the house again. The defendant then took a house in South Bank- ' terrace and Mrs. Grounds engaged a maid, but the defendant told her he had already engaged for this Mrs. Price to keep house for them. Mrs. Grounds said, "If you are going to have that woman there afterwhat has taken place I shall not come to live there." Defendant replied, "Well, she is coming. whether you want or not." Subsequently Mrs. Price went there and acted as housekeeper, and Grounds left his wife at his mother's. From that day to this, four and a half yearsago, he had never given his wife a cent., and had been to see her once only. Ile had been written to twfee, and bad not replied. Then Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, wrote to him, and Mr. Knowles replied that defendant was perfectly willing to provide a home for her. That was all very well.—Mr. Linaker suggested that defendant was only promising to get rid of the housekeeper for the purpose of this case, and they did not know how long it would last. Ile quoted a case to show that a woman was justified in refusing to live with her husband if he acted improperly. He could not prove 'actual misconduct. __ - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles: Your worships know what the defendant's occupation is? Ido not want to mention it ; I would rather write it on a piece of paper. (Mr. Knowles then wrote on a piece of paper, which he handed to the bench). Mr. Norman strongly advised that the case should be settled. He was very sorry Mrs. Price's name bad been mentioned, and that these allegations bad been flung out. It was a great responsibility. The best thing would be to adjourn the case for a fortnight, end if they did not agrf e, then the case could be fought out in the best possible way. It was going to be a case of far- reaching consequences, as was shown by %chat Mr. Knowles had written on this paper, end innuendos and suggestions should not be thrown out. Mr. Knowles said he had a strong defence, but it did not serve his purpose to mention it now. If they were to try to get these people together it was no use throw- ing stones. The case was then adjourned for a fortnight. It was later announced that the case had been settled by Mr. Grounds allowing his is ife Ss. a week and paying costs, and all allegations against Mr. Grounds were withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
