      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Sabbath Breaking at Lymm.</head>
        <p>Sabbath Breaking at Lymm.</p>
        <p>—lt is a pity that some people have little regard for the Sabbath Day. I allude to the individual who let his field last Sunday for Jewish sports in Lymm. I term it a disgrace and am not alone in the opinion. The way which some trippers carry on in Lymm during the week is a disgrace, and we do not want Sunday sports or anything of that kind encouraging. Very few people can say that they have not seen or lieiird the • commandment, Remember to keep the Sal bath dav holy !</p>
        <p>Sir;</p>
        <p>hope oilier iiihabitants will take this' matter up, if it occurs again.—Yours etc.,</p>
        <p>ERNEST WRIGHT.</p>
        <p>Ash Villa, Lymni.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>AT RHOS-ON-SEA.</head>
        <p>AT RHOS-ON-SEA.</p>
        <p>There everlasting breezes play Above the broad and balmy bay. While, on the gentle hills behind, Green branches wave,in every wind; And, out in front, great ships go by, And fade and vanish silently; And, twice a day, we watch the tide Creep up, the crumbling weir to hide; And, further on, the Little Orme, With rugged brow, confronts the storm, Or veils the crimson afterglow When in the West the sun sinks low; And, softly shining, calm and still, We see the distant lights of Rhyl, A diamond necklace gleaming bright Upon the dusky breast of Night; Or watch the silver moon, new-born, Above the smiling fields of corn; Or scan the velvet skies, and gaze On suns and stars and Milky Ways, Until, at last, we half forget The smoke, the fever, and the fret In Mammon's dark and crowded hive, And, for a fortnight, really live. ARTHUR BENNETT.</p>
        <p>August, 1900,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>There are some good turns at the Palace and Hippodrome this week. The best one is that of the Gascoynes, who are described as comedy jugglers. Their feats are simply marvellous. The male is the principal, and as he performs his tricks with the assistance of the young lady he keeps the audience in merrimEnt with his jokes. Whilst he .is juggling with four knives he engages in a talk to this effect. "I say you see that man over there taking that gate away." "Yes" she replied" shall I tell him to bring it , back ?" "Oh ! no, he inight,take affence." And sol it goes on all through his performance. At one time he balances 15 chairs upon his forehead and follows this up by balancing the lady on a chair. " 1920 " by Brien McCullough is a thrilling and realistic story of the invasion of England by the Germans on 21st October 1920, the 115th anniversary of the battle of Trafalgar. The scene is laid in the interior of a Thames Fort house, and it is the day of Lieutenant Kingstone's marriage to Berth deGrandon a daughter of Lady deGrandon. Countess Zarza becomes the "slave" of Count \Top Arenberg, who offers her £15,000 if she will get the • plans of the submarine mines. Lieut. Kingstone leaves the keys of the desk, which contains the plans, ,with his wife, who gives them to her mother to unlock the handbag, Countess Zarza appears on the scene and tells Lady de Grandon to go for Bertha's jewels and put them in the bag, as they would be much safer. Lady de Grandon readily assents and leaves the room. The Countess alone seizes her opportunity, unlocks the desk and takes out the plans. She places them in a letter which bertha has addressed to the Count, apologises for not asking him to the wedding. Suspicion falls upon Bertha and her mother, but the latter explains that she left the key in the room where the Countess was alone. They recover the letter addressed to the Count, and the Captain tends for the Countess Zarza. He tells her that he has sent to the Count for the name of the person who stole the plans, as he had promised it him. 'Of course this is all bluff. On this pretence she confesses. A moment later there is a shout that England is invaded and the German warships are seen entering the Thames. Lieutenant Kingstone rushes to the switch-board and explodes the mines, the invading fleet being blown to atoms with a realistic bang. So ends the sketch. The principals are Alr.C. F. Collings, Mr. Brien McCullough (the author), Mr Harry J. Crane, Misses Mabel Mannering, Nellie Nelson and Edith Lewis. Harry Vardon contributes a veutriloqival act entitled "on the phone." The sisters Gladwiu are extremely smart novelty dancers and vocalists. George Bendon, the white coon, sings songs and fully deserves applause, whilst Dave O'Toole keeps the audience roaring with laughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>RE-OPENING OF ST. ALBAN'S, BEWS EY-STREET</head>
        <p>RE-OPENING OF ST. ALBAN'S, BEWS EY-STREET</p>
        <p>The first Catholic place of worship in Warrington after the Reformation date back to the middle of the eighteenth century. It was very small and simple, merely a loft over a coach house in Friar's Green. A little later the Catholics rented a small place in Dallam-lane. But in 1766 the first Catholic Chapel was built at the Horsemarket end of Bewsey-street. In 1823 the present St. Alban's was builtby the Right Rev. Dr. Molyneux, 0.5.8. The old chapel at the end of Bewsey-street, now the League Hall, -became the school. Little change took place either within or without St. Alban's till 1893 when the church was re-benched and the present beautiful sanctuary and altar erected. In 190 Q, the six large side ivindoWg, which were in ordinary square Panes and fixed in wooden frames, were replaced with stone jambs and mullions in Romanesque style, and filled with Hard- man's finest painted glass. We now come to what has been done this year. The old porch, which was partly within the Church, has been removed, and a very fine and large outside porch has been erected, and is surmounted by a large west window, consisting of six lights, thirteen feet high by two feet six wide. Over every two lights there is a circular light. The centre circular represents Our Lord as the great High Priest and at His feet are nearly life-like figures of Saints' Peter and Paul. In the circle to the right is Blessed Sir Thomas More, and below, him St. Alban and St. Basil the Great. On the other side is Blessed John Fisher, Bishop of Rochestei, and behind St. Placid, the first Benedictine Martyr, and St. Elphin, Patron Saint of Warrington. All the figures are in rich colours, and are fine and bold in design. These windows are also executed by Hardman of Birmingham. Over the porch front door there is a carved pediment. The central figure is St. Alban, holding the Cross in his hand. He is ordered by the judge to sacrifice to the false gods, and the reply to the question as to his name and profession is :—" My name is Alban, and I! worship the one true and living God." The executioner refuses to strike off his head, and "throwing down the sword confesses himself a Christian, and is martyred with the Saint. The whole has been carved by Mr. Milburn, the famous sculptor, of York. On the north side of the porch is a striking dome- shaped Baptistery. The style of the whole building within and without is Romanestple. The architect is Mr. Charles Walker, of Newcastle-on-lcyne and the builder Mr. Ashworth, of Manchester.</p>
        <p>It must not be omitted that the organ has been thoroughly cleaned and overhauled, several new stops added and pneumatic action fitted to the pedal organ. This has been done by Wadworth and Bro. of Man- chester, the original builders of the organ. The re-opening of the church takes place to-morrow (Sun- day). Doubtless many will go to hear the famous Father Robert Hugh Benson,who preaches morning and evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WHITE it WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw st., opposite top of Bold st.,Liverpool.</p>
