      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Baskerville—Houghton.</p>
        <p>There was a pretty wedding at All Saints' Church, Daresbury, on Wednesday, when Mr. Edward Baskerville, son of Mr. Thomas 13askerville, Arple,y- street, Warrington, was married to Miss Fanny Louisa Houghton, daughter of Mr. Thomas Houghton, of Daresbury. The Vicar (Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones) officiated. The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in a blue costume with picture hat to match, and carried a bouquet of choice flowers. The bridesmaid (Miss Annie Houghton) wore brown empire dress, and her bouquet was composed of sweet peas. Mr. Frank Worthington acted 'as best man. After the ceremony a recep- tion was held at the house of the bride's father. Many handsome presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Sanders—Bladen.</head>
        <p>Sanders—Bladen.</p>
        <p>A very quiet wedding took place. on Thursday morning at Wycliffe Church, when Mr. Arthur Harold Sanders, of Lovely-lane, was married to Miss Gertrude Bladen, daughter of Mr. John Bladen, of 272, Lovely-lane. The Rev. H. Wall officiated. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attii•ed in a light green costume, and wore a white hat trimmed with white feathers. Miss Cissy Crook was the bridesmaid, and wore a fawn empire dress and a heliotrope merry widow hat. Mr. Ernest Sanders (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man. The reception was held at the home of the bride's father, and later the happy couple left for Blackpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Stafford—Burdett.</head>
        <p>Stafford—Burdett.</p>
        <p>A wedding which aroused'considerable local interest was celebrated at St. Paul's Church on, Thursday when the contracting partiekwer, Mr. John William Stafford, The Park, Eccles dad "Miss Marion Burdett, 10, Oxford-street, daughter of the late Mr. Alfred Burdett. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. W. Bracecamp. The bride, who entered the church leaning 'on the arm of her brother, was attired in a white alpaca dress and wore a tulle veil surmounted with a wreath of orange blossom. She carried a shower bouquet of white heather, white roses and lilies of the Valley, the gift of the bride- groom, The bridesmaids were Misses Lizzie and Maggie Burdett, the sisters of the bride. The fer-iner wore a blue silk marquisette and the latter was dressed in rose coloured voile. Both wore black hats and carried shower bouquets of sweet peas, the gifts of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. Herbert Stafford (brother, of the bridegroom). The reception was held at the home of the bride's mother and later the happy couple left for Llandudno, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride wore a beech coloured cloth travelling costume with black hat. The following is a list of presents:— Mother of bride, household linen Mr. and Mrs. W. F. Burdett, white quilt Master F. I3urdett, cake stand Miss L. Burdett, tea service Miss M. Burdett, dinner service Father of bridegroom piano and su:ta Mr. and Mrs. Stafford, toilet set and table cloth Mr. and Mrs. S. Stubbs (sister of the bridegroom) dining table and wedding cake Mr. A. Stafford, ornaments Mr. and Mrs. C. Bradshaw of the firm of Bradshaw and Blacklock, Manchester, solid silver cream jug, sugar tongs and tea spoons The employees of Messrs. Blacklock end Co., Chippendale overmantel, brass cnrb and fireiroas Mr. White (H. Blacklock and Co.), pictures Mr. Bradley (H. Blacklock and Co.), mirror Mr. Andrews (Eccles), clock Aunt of bridegroom, cups and saucers Miss Stubbs (Eccles), set of jugs Mr. I'. Jackson Lee, white quilt Mr. 'l'. Birtles, water colours Mrs. Peter Leigh and family, silver cream jug and sugar Lasin Mr. and Mrs. W. W. Leigh, Worcester ornaments Mr. and Mrs. J. D. Leigh, cut glass water jug, sugar basin and cream jug Mrs. Marsh (aunt of bride), picture Mr. H. Ma sh (cousin of bride), picture Mr. S. Marsh (cousin of bride), picture Mrs. Burdett, Mr. and Mrs. A. Burdett, copper kettle Miss R. Burdett (Bexhill-on-Sea), Egyptian cloth Mrs. White (Latchford), prongs Mr. S. White (Latch fond), dessert spoons Miss White (Latchford) table knives Mr. and Mrs. J. Starkey, picture Miss Broomfield, embroidered sheets, and electro- plated bread knife Miss Gardner,Damaslc tablecloth Miss M. Cook, sitting-room coal box Mr. Newburn, plant stand -Er. Proctor, picture Miss Ross, toilet tidies Mr. Peake, silver jam dish Mr. J. Fairclough, trinket set Miss Harrison and Mr. Parkes, silver salts Miss Gorst, linen duchesse set Miss L. Smith, embroidered linen cloth Miss L. Potter (Seedley), table centre Miss N. Potter (Seedley), cushion covers Mrs. Milton, pillow cases Miss Clare, cake stand and cheese dish Mrs. and Miss A. Middleton, linen pillow cases</p>
        <p>Miss Ella Middleton, fruit d'oyley - Mrs. Bottomley, table centre and piano cover Miss Bottomley. tea cosy Mr. P. Moores, photograph frame Miss Banahan, jam dish Mrs. Hackney, jun., watercress dish Mr. and Mrs. A. Wright (Latchford), cake stand Mr. and Mrs. Robertson, dinner plates Mrs. Taylor (Latchford), duchesse set Miss Taylor (Lltchford), toilet tidies Mrs. Mee (Latchford), fruit dish Mias Gillan, water jug Mrs. Whiteman, ornaments</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " Dare-Devil Dorothy."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " Dare-Devil Dorothy."</p>
        <p>We should think that in some respects "Dare-Devil Dorothy" is one of the most remarkable pieces which has yet occupied the boards in Rylands-street. It is described as a "musical comedy novelty," and it is all that and- more. For instance the first act is pure melodrama in which the villain hisses " Ca-hurse you!" and finally plunges a knife into the back of an old and wealthy -uncle who had threatened to make a new and awkward will. The hero turns up at the same moment in convict garb (he has escaped, but is innocent) and the new crime is promptly fastened upon him. The curtain rises again a minute later on a vaudeville turn by the Misses Lillie and Iris Elvido, two young dancers who have made great strides—graceful strides too--in their profession. Then we are introduced to a bush farm in New South Wales,Australia,where the murdered man's nieces and heiresses are living ft lively tut poverty-stricken existence. The villain and the hero both turn up in good time, have natural differences, and vice is left triumphant. The next scene is in high society—the nieces have come into their own and the swains are still in attendance, and at enmity with one another. A comedy turn or two are thrown in, and then we are switched on to circus-life in the last act. These incongruous surroundings witness the death of the villain, and the return to grace and fortune of the hero. Dare-Devil Dorothy is in the play, of course, but she hardly does sufficient daring of his Satanic Majesty to cause that gentleman a single tremor. In fact Dorothy was too mild for the title-role. She was pretty and charming and sang nicely, but the devil-daring was more imaginary than real. The piece) ought at least to live up to its title ! Mr. Harry Ugar, the general utility and funny man, has quite a gay time, and creates f) good deal of laughter. Judith (Miss Nellie Harcourt) is a vivacious young person who never lets the ball stop rolling, and Ruth Montrose (Dorothy's sister) does all that ,is required of her in creditable style. Miss Mary: Otto takes the part, and her rendering of "Never mind the weather" was deservedly encored. Mr. Lewis Dayton makes a realistic villain of the scheming, unscrupulous- adventurer type, and the counter-part is well-played by Mr. G. A. Fox, a baritone of note. Mr. Deane Cliffe represents a dandified sportsman in capital style</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. Wilson Benge's company will present "The eire of her wedding." -During the week commencing September 20th, there will be a _special attraction in the shape. of the first production of Mr. B. S. Townroe's patriotic drama, "A nation in arms." The performance each evening will be under distinguished patronage. Seats may now be booked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NEWSBOY'S REPROOF.</head>
        <p>THE NEWSBOY'S REPROOF.</p>
        <p>Like newsboys elsewhere, the Glasgow newsboy is alert and independent, but no one, says the "Herald" of that city, would suspect hina of a capacity to administer a dignified reproof in a manner indicating strong self- restraint. One who was refused a match the other evening in a tobacconist's shop, laid a shabby cigarette upon the counter, produced a halfpenny, and asked fora box of vestt.s. When he got it he set fire to his tobacco, dropped half-a-dozen matches into a ragged pocket, and handed the box back over the counter. ‘Jist you keep these on the shelf, ma'm, and gie one to ony gentleman that asks fur a light."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>HOWLEY WARD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>HOWLEY WARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>POLLING ON MONDAY NEXT.</p>
        <p>The election to fill the vacancy in Howley Ward, created by the death of Councillor W. B. Roberts, will take place on Monday next. The polling booth will be at the National Schools, Church-street, and it is expected that the result will be declared at the Town Hall about 8.45 p.m. The ,number of electors in the ward is 1119. There are two candidates in the field— Mr. George Christopher Howard, metal merchant, 8, Froghall-lane, (Indepen- dent) and Mr. Charles Dutton Parkin- son, chartered accountant, Bewsey Chambers (Conservative). Mr. Howard in his address says that his desire is to serve the ward with which he has been so closely connected all his life by adding his support, to, and pressing forward the development of Howley Ward by the construction of a bridge which' will serve the interests, not of any particular class, but of the town in general, and Howley Ward in particular. Mr. Parkinson says there. are many vital questions awaiting solution, and he specially mentions the widening of Warrington Bridge, the provision of better,access for the Howley Ward to the ViCtoria Park, the completion of the circus at Market Gate and the widening of our main streets. He is in favour of steady municipal progress and believes that it was never more important than</p>
        <p>[Birtic, MR. C. D. PARKINSON (C.).</p>
        <p>' '4l4ft fr 141%.0 rit•W*4o,4ttiirg,•*s • • • • • ." • •.' , • • :44 .„.•, ;*. i</p>
        <p>.% • ' photo, E i. 4; • • '• Birties. " '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>LECTURE ON ARMENIA.</head>
        <p>LECTURE ON ARMENIA.</p>
        <p>An interesting and instructive lecture on Armenia and its people was given by Mr. E. C. Quine, of Liverpool, before a moderate attendance at tha Gospel Hall, Forster-street on Thursday night. In the course of his lecture, Mr. QUINE said that mankind first originated in Armenia, as the Garden of Eden was situated.there. Mankind was also given a second start in this country, after the great flood when the Ark rested on• Mount Ararat. We would naturally think that the country would be most peaceful, but not a bit of it. Through the Turks the atrocities were most frightful. This country had been the battlefield of all the countries surrounding it and there was not a country in the world which could show more battle fields than Armenia. There was not a country thil.t had spilled more innocent blood. What was the cause of it all? Why religion. When Abdul Hamid ascended to the throne of Turkey then the trouble began. He thought by committing the most h. rrible deeds by massacres of the Protestants (and of course the Tutlis are Mohammedans, he would get the greater reward when he died. The Armenians were the slaves of the Turks, and were put to all kinds of torture if /they did not suit the taste of their masters. The lecturer went on to describe the country, which was of great dimensions. From the sea shore rise great cliffs thickly wooded, which could supply the whole world with timber, but under the sway of the Turks the wood is left to rot. The dountry also abounded in all kinds of wild animals, and was also rich in copper, but the mines were worked at a loss through the influence of the Turks. There are also a number of exceedingly high mountains, one of which was Mount Ararat, which was 17,000 feet high. There were many mountains that rose seven, eight, nine, and twelve thousands of feet high. The country which could be the richest in the world was in consequence of misrule, stricken with poverty. The Americans avere thinking of sending missionaries out there, and 'God-speed them when they got out. An Englishman who went out not many years ago did a great deal of good. He was a private in an Indian Regiment, and on seeing the condition of the Armenians, he left the army and journeyed amongst the people. But he died at the early age of thirty-five. The people there want Englishmen as missionaries, and they considered them the best.</p>
        <p>The speaker then proceeded to describe the methods of torture committed on the Armenians and quoted a true case. There was an old man who was charged with stealing, of which he was totally ignorant but a Turkish soldier who was guilty of the crime charged the old man with it and this was sufficient. He was seized and hung up by the heels for a number of hours and then taken down, and his hands put in a wooden vice. Split canes were then inserted under his finger nails which were lifted off. He was then hung up by his thumbs. Another mode was to imprison a number of people in a small hole in the ground and there leave them for months at a time, just giving them food and drink enough to keep them liiver The Turl42 had 42 idea a ventilation or sanitatipn and they would put twenty or more people in a cell which was only large enough to hold' six or seven people. The conditions of the people came to such a climax many years ago that all the great Powers intervened and the Treaty of Berlin was sigped and Consuls ware sent out to maintain peace. But Abdul Hamid only laughed in their faces and gave the order "to kill all the Armenians." The lecturer then gave horrible accounts of massacres that had been enacted in different parts of the country. The most terrible of which was at Sassoon, where 25,000 people were put to death. Many of them took i refuge n a place of worship. The women and children got into the galleries thinking they were safe but the Turks filed on them through the windows. They afterwards took the roof off and dropped fire brands upon them below. Then they brought the fire engine, but not to put the flames out. They put one end of the hose in barrels of kerosine and pumped the oil on the burning building and as the wretches rushed out they were shot down. Those who stayed in the building were of course burnt to death. The lecturer quoted another horrible deed, where one Turkish soldier made a bet that he could strike four Armenian's children's heads off.at once. Four children were accordingly taken from their mothers (who dared not Utter a word) and they were placed in a row and ' the soldier with one stroke of his sword decapitated them. Russia had several times tried to get hold of Armenia and had-even paid to have these massacres committed in order that she could be proclaimed justified in fighting for the country. Russia was as bad as the rest. Mr. Quine also mentioned the great massacre that took place in April last and read letters from various missionaries who were out there at the present time in which they described the horrible lives that the people have to lead. The Spanish Inquisition was a mere nothing to what is going on in Armenia, the Inquisitor's cruelty cannot be compared with that of the Turks. - - - -</p>
        <p>The lecturer concluded by saying that he was lecturing for funds to help the poor children who were left orphans. A collection was made in aid of these.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Honour for a Warrington _Art Student.</head>
        <p>Honour for a Warrington _Art Student.</p>
        <p>The drawing of a draped figure by Mr. S. W. Clatworthy, a student at the School of Art, for which he was awarded a Bronze Medal in the National Competition, has been purchased by the Board of Education for the Government of New Zealand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MR. G. C. HOWARD (hid.)</head>
        <p>MR. G. C. HOWARD (hid.)</p>
        <p>now to take a broad and comprehensive view of our municipal responsibilities. Every public-spirited citizen should be prepared to do his utmost to assist in the great task of improving the town and bringing it into line with all the most progressive boroughs in the kingdom. Whilst he is a Conservative in politics, he shall retain his right to act as he thinks best on all municipal questions. A meeting in support of Mr. Parkinson's candidature was held in the National Schools, Church-street on Monday evening. Mr. John Fairclough presided, and the speakers included the candidate, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Mr. Robert Pierpoint, Alderman Bennett, Councillors Vernon, Sankey and Wilson. Another meeting was held last evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LORD ROSEBERTS NEW LIBERALISM.</head>
        <p>LORD ROSEBERTS NEW LIBERALISM.</p>
        <p>VICIOUS ATTACK ON THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>Points from the Speech.</p>
        <p>He was an independent politician wishing to show why the Budget should not become law. It is not in the best interests of the nation that the Budget should become law. The most remarkable Budget ever pre- sented by any Party. The destiny of Great Britain is in the melting pot. The Budget is a revolution without A.ny mandate from the people. No form of property is safe, or meant to be safe, under new taxation. Land clauses must immediately result in greatly increased unemployment.</p>
        <p>The -Budget sought to establish an inquisi- tion unknown to Great Britain and a _ tyranny unknown to mankind.</p>
        <p>The Government's policy is Machiavellian with the object of spurring the House of Lords to throw out the Finance Bill.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering in the City Hall, Glasgow, yesterday afternoon, to listen to Lord Rosebery's eagerly anticipated speech to business men on the Budget. The hall affords accommodation for 3,000 people, but the applications for tickets far exceeded this number, and a great number of people w ere necessarily disappointed.</p>
        <p>After speaking for an hour and forty mtnutes, his lordship wound up by saying :—Tariff Reform was an evil. but Socialism was the end of all things. He would follow the road of public economy; the strengthening of character and, of property and of public confidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON REVISION COURT. -- Angry Suffragette's Message. (Continued from Page 5).</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON REVISION COURT. -- Angry Suffragette's Message. (Continued from Page 5).</p>
        <p>When the Warrington Revision Court re-assembled yesterday morning, the Revising Barrister read a post- card whicji had come to hand addressed to Mr. Wharton, Revising Barrister, Warrington," and bearing the Manchester post-mark. A cutting from a newspaper containing an abbreviated report of his Honour's remarks on the suffragette question was pasted on the card, and the writing which accompanied it was as follows :—" Surely this must be a mistake, its never the Mr. Wharton i knew, he was such a line sensible man, besides he was married and loved women, unless you have joined the haters of all women-kind, of course I Gin 131141'Am:id Liberal agents talking in that strain, we have only to read how they treat women who go to there meetings, but they like their help in election• times. Shame on such men they must be batchelors and even forget they ever had a mother, but I suppose he goes to chapel God bless him, Amen:' The Barrister remarked that it was because he loved women that he did not believe in lunatics going about throwing iron balls and saying they expected they might kill somebody. That was what he thought they ought to be birched for. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>THANKING THE I3ARRISTER.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE I3ARRISTER.</p>
        <p>At the close of the business, Mr. Holbrook proposed a vote of thanks to the Revising Barrister for the kind manner in which he had listened to each case that had been brought before him. He also thanked Mr. Woodcock and his staff who had prepared a most excel- lent list, and the offioials of the Town Clerk's depart- ment for their courteous help. Mr. Howes, in seconding, said :the list was as near perfect as it could be. The overseers were really marvels. The Revising Barrister's kindness to both agents was greatly appreciated by them. The Revising Barrister said he ought to thank), first of all, Mr. Woodcock for very much information and for a marvellous list. He also had to thank the agents for their forbearance. There was much that he bad to be thankful for in the fact that they did not have any squabbles amongst the political parties, and he was hoping that he would be able to go through the whole of his district, as he had done for two years, without imposing a single halfpenny for costs. He thought it spoke very well for the whole of the division and for the political agents that there were no costs. None were wanted and he hoped they never would be.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Trades and Labour Council.</head>
        <p>Trades and Labour Council.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Trades and Labour Council was held on 'Tuesday evening, Mr. D. L. Owen presiding over a fair attendance. Mr. E. Plinston (secretary), supported the chairman at the table.—The Secretary read a letter from the National Council dated 27th August, 1909, asking them to write to our local member of Parliament, President of the Local Government Board and the Prime Minister for their support of Clause 30 of the Town Planning Bill. The Bill was to come up on 30th August, so they would be glad if they would write them immediately. Continuing, he said, • that as the Council did not meet till after the Bill was before Parliament he -wrote to tie persons named. He knew what was the feeling and therefore he thought it was alright.—His action was endorsed by the Council. —A letter read by the Secretary, from the Central Labour College, London, asking for support was, on the proposition of Mr. Smith, and seconded, by Mr. H. j Sinclair, ordered to lie on the table,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>" SUB-ROSA " AND THE BOLD BUCCLEUGH,</head>
        <p>" SUB-ROSA " AND THE BOLD BUCCLEUGH,</p>
        <p>The Duke, the Budget, and the Football Club,</p>
        <p>" Sub-Rosa " in the "Morning Leader" of yesterday welcomes the Duke of Buccleuch into the ranks of the Budget Protesters. He says:—</p>
        <p>have been waiting for his grace, feeling sure that one of his nationality and rank and boundless wealth would be sure to squeak and economise. And behold, the bold Buccleuch has been roused, he has stalked into the fray, and has taken a terrible revenge. Because of this wicked Budget this owner of six palaces and countless acres, this possessor of heaped-up treasures for which he never did a stroke of work, has—prepare yourself, good reader, for a shock—he has stopped a subscription of one pound a year to a football club! And thus the great landowner and noble- man retains in his breeches pocket—assum- ing that he wears breeches—no less than forty saxpences. Moreover, he has done rather well by passing on such liabilities as he could to the Old Age Pensions Act. He has always been a shrewd man, a fact that is well known in Edinburgh. Some time ago that city wanted some land for a gas works, and they bought it from the duke. The land was rated on an average at £5 10s. an acre, and thus 30 years' purchase on the assessed value would have been just £165 per acre. The area required was 105 acres, and so at the rate mentioned the noble duke might have expected £l7, 325.</p>
        <p>Was he content with a little bargain of that sort? His noble lordship's grace, or his ducal grace's lordship, or whatever his style and title is, was by no means satisfied with anything of tile sort. In effect he said, " Awa .wi ye," and after many a hech and a hoot,-and an aiblins this and aiblins that, he was graciously pleased to take £124,000, or 214 years' purchase. It was enough to make even the Duke of Northumberland stare, and he is also quite a careful man. And the late Duke of Buccleuch managed to get £150,000 out of Londoners when the Thames Embank- ment was made, after having in vain tried to stop the improvement altogether because it interfered with his back garden.</p>
        <p>As I have said, I am glad the present duke has let all the world know about that sovereign saved, though I can understand the attitude of good Mr. Ashworth, of the Central Association, when he gloomily declares that the dukes would have done better. had they kept their mouths closed. As it is, the richer the duke is the louder he yelps. The revenues of the Duke of Buccleuch are vast—larger than those of some reigning princes. And he is the man to knock off one pound from the funds of a football club and to haggle over a few shillings with aged labourers so that he shall benefit by the Old Age Pension Act, which the peers cursed and reviled—and passed only because they did not dare to reject it.</p>
        <p>The luck of the Buccleuchs can be traced to a source to which other noble families owe much—to the free and easy ideas and conduct of that most religious and gracious monarch, King Charles 11.—Defender of the Faith. And ever since the family got well started they have shown themselves quite ready to pick up anything that came in their way. Only once, so far as I can find out,has their luck deserted them, and the tale is worth telling, for the bold Buccleuch on that occasion came a cropper, and it is said that he was in a terrible state of grief and rage about it.</p>
        <p>Somewhere about the year 1860 the duke of that day sent into the town of Sanquhar a claim for arrears of stipend and interest due to him as "Titular," the amount being £llO ss. 11d. The Town Council of Sanquhar, fortunately not composed of such toadies as too often kow-tow to the occupant of an adjoining palace, said in effect that they liked the duke's cheek, but that if he was going into any little matter of arrears they begged to put in a counter-claim for £1,156 is. Bd. for "arrears of feu-rents for lands held by his grace from the town." The bold Buccleuch of 50 years ago (the Embank- ment man, I think) danced with rage, but he danced in vain. The Court of Session looked into the matter, and the two actions ended in each side getting what it claimed. Thus when the old duke of 1860 went through his accounts on the next Saturday " nicht," or aiblins on the following Sawbbath morning (secretly, of course), he would find that while he had recovered £llO Rs. 11d. from Sanquhar, that excellent town had screwed £1,156 is. Bd. out of him. Result (heartbreaking result!) his noble lordship's ducal grace was ,E 1,045 15s. 9d. out of pocket (if any) on the little deal. I believe that the noble family still observes the dnniversary of the day on which this took place as a day of humiliation and all retainers are put on short allowances of oat- meal and snuff.</p>
        <p>S. L. H.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-11_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MR. HALL CAINE'S NEW NOVEL.</head>
        <p>MR. HALL CAINE'S NEW NOVEL.</p>
        <p>In "The White Prophet," by Mr. Hall' Caine (Heinemann, 2 vols , 45.), we have a strong dramatic story dealing with Egyptian politics, full of vivid scenes of life in Cairo and in the Desert. In our imagination we can see the usual Drury-lane actors portraying their parts in the great home of melodrama. With very little trouble we can fit these actors with their parts. 'The scenic effects would be sufficiently striking,—the Khedivial Racecourse at Cairo, the palace of the British Agency, Khartoum, the Camp of the White Prophet in the Soudan Desert, the Banquet- ing Hall of the Ghezireh Palace on the island in the Nile, the General Court Martial in the Ministry of War. Our dramatis personae and those with whom they are acquainted are sufficiently high placed to interest the most fashionable of readers—not only are we intro- duced to the inner secrets of the home of the Consul General for Egypt but are given an idea of the bon camaraderie of our reigning Monarch, who takes a kindly interest in the love affairs of our heroine and instead of confirming the sentence of death under which our hero lies is so much moved by his manifold and manifest virtues as to promote him to the rank of Commander of the British forces in Egypt. We are not sure that the Censor of Plays at the present moment is sufficiently intimidated by the attack on his position made by certain advanced authors to allow the parallelism of the lives and acts of the White Prophet (a colourable imitation of the Mandi) and the Founder-of the Christian Faith to be reprcduced on the stage, but Mr. Hall Caine can do without this advertisement and will perhaps, in the dramatised version, not follow too closely his novel. We shall not spoil the entertainment by disclosing the plot, which we must confess to thinking somewhat thin and working to an obvious conclusion. In our opinion any use of colourable imitations of prominent persadages, foreigners or British, is not in the best of taste, and we would rather welcome from Hall Caine's pen a tale of the wondrous island in the Irish Sea that he has made so famous.</p>
        <p>The book is published in two volumes and so as to be of convenient bulk for the hand or pocket—and forms one of a series which should initiate a successful revolt against the form and price of the ordinary novel.</p>
      </div>
