        <p>A HOLE THROUGH THE EARTIL „0 The inhabitants of the earth are still very far full knowledge of the planet on which they 1-ost They have explored its surface until they have al to exhausted the regions of sea or land which have road be discovered. Astronomically they have deterrior its shape, size, and weight. But as to its ,'-roat* constitution they are sti3l profoundly ign"-tba° It is only by chance, and indtrectlY they have penetrated a little way into its interior. Aope,„ railway tunnels which pierce the mountains have ot no more than traverse the inequalities of the eras ths the earth. The deepest shaft which penetrateg St earth was constructed between 1893 and 190-40 ak, Paruschowitz, near Rybnik, and is about a mile milcse quarter in depth—scarcely more than an insigal." scratch upon the surface of our globe. st I have had the idea in mind for some time Pao . sinking a shaft into the earth for the express Pare-tbe scientific exploration, descending as far belor.eone surface as the utmost resoUrces of mldern Stipa would permit. This idea has just been forcibly re:A. to my attention in consequence of the recent ; quakes and the extremely contradictory opin% geologists upon the interior state of the globe. "or globQ liquid or solid? From the most ancientier scienlifio men hay° considered the problem low doll its hearings, without having succeeded in lightiogo,4 la' any fatisfa9tory solution.—CAmm.s</p>
