        <p>R. u NOO UNION.—CONTRACTS for PROVISIONS and SUPPLIES for the Half-year ending March 26th, 1910. The Guardians of the Poor of the Runcorn Union are desirous of receiving TENDERS from parties will- ing to Contract for the supply of Good Beef, to consist of vein pieces intact and Flat Ribs with bone (without brisket) one round weekly not to exceed 251bs.' and 45Ibs. per week of the Best Butcher's Meat, including veal, lamb and pork (in season) for the Officers; Sides of Mutton (all meat to be English killed), Groceries, Bread, Tobacco and Snuff, Drapery, Clothing, Boots, Bocit repairing, Potatoes Straw, Drysaltery. Brushes and Mats, Oils and Colours, Coal, Slack, Coke, DEal Coffins, Horse Hire (for funerals and carting). Tenders are also invited from Tradesmeq for the services per hour of Journeymen and Apprentices of the following trades :—Plumber, Joiner, Whitesmith, and Bricklayer. Parties tendering for the supply of the above- mentioned Goods will be required to deliver the same at the Workhouse, free of cost to the Guardians, how and when required. Tenders to be accompanied with samples (except Drapery Goods and Clothing) as far as practicable. The Guardians do not bind themselves to except the lowest or any Tender. Persons desirous of tendering must apply by letter to my office in Runcorn, for a printed Form of Tender (and none other can be entertained), where a Schedule of the various articles required and farther particulars may be obtained. Sealed Tenders' addressed to the Guardians, to be forwarded to the Clerk's Office, Runcorn, on or before Monday, the 27th day of September, 1909, endorsed "Tender for .f) The Tenders will be opened and considered at a Meeting of the Guardians to be held in the Board- room, Dutton, on the 28th day of September, 1909, at 1 p.m. Parties whose tenders are accepted must enter into a written contrdct and be prepared to enter into a Bond with two Sureties, if required by the Guardians. By order of the Board, GEO. F. ASHTON, Clerk to the Guardians. Runcorn, Ist September, 1909.</p>
